+    {"<Tartarus>", "<塔尔塔罗斯>"},
+    {"“And if you gaze for long into an abyss, the abyss gazes also into you.”\n    -Friedrich Nietzsche, _Beyond Good and Evil_ , Aphorism 146. 1886.\n\n“Well, in our country,” said Alice, still panting a little, “you'd generally\n get to somewhere else — if you ran very fast for a long time, as we've been\n doing.” “A slow sort of country!” said the Queen. “Now here, you see, it takes\n all the running you can do, to keep in the same place. If you want to get\n somewhere else, you must run at least twice as fast as that!”\n    -Lewis Carroll, _Through the Looking-Glass, and What Alice Found There_,\nch. 2 “The Garden of Live Flowers”. 1871.", "“若你长久凝视深渊，深渊也会凝视你。”\n    -弗里德里希·尼采，《善恶的彼岸》，箴言146。1886年。\n\n“唔，在我们这儿，”爱丽丝说，仍有些喘，“你要是\n像我们刚才那样飞快地跑上很久，一般总能\n到达别的地方。”“那是个慢吞吞的国度！”王后说。“可你看，在这儿，你得\n拼命跑，才能留在原地。要是你想去\n别的地方，至少得跑得比那快一倍！”\n    -刘易斯·卡罗尔，《爱丽丝镜中奇遇记》，\n第2章“活花的花园”。1871年。"},
+    {"“I mused upon the mystery of fish, their strange and mindless beauty, how—\n innocently evil—they prey upon each other, devouring the weaker and smaller\n without rage or shout or change of countenance. There, in the realm of water,\n which is also earth and air to them, the great fish passed up and down,\n growing old without aging and enjoying eternal growth without the softness of\n obesity. It was a world without morality, a world without choices, a world of\n eating and spawning and growing great. I envied the great fish, and (in other,\n smaller ponds) the lesser fish, darting and flashing and sparkling gold.\n\n They speak of ‘the beast in man,’ and of ‘the law of the jungle.’ Might they\n not (so I reflected, strolling underneath a sky of clouds as blue and as white\n as the tiles and marble of the Altar of Heaven), might they not better speak\n of ‘the fish in man’? And of ‘the law of the sea’? The sea, from which they\n say we came...?”\n    -Avram Davidson, “Dagon”, 1959.", "“我思索鱼的奥秘，它们那奇异而无心的美，如何——\n天真地邪恶——彼此捕食，吞下更弱更小的同类，\n没有愤怒，没有呼喊，面容也不改分毫。在那里，水的国度里，\n那对它们既是大地又是空气之处，大鱼上下游弋，\n老去却不衰朽，享受着永恒的生长，而没有\n肥胖的松软。那是一个没有道德的世界，一个没有选择的世界，一个\n只顾进食、产卵、长得巨大的世界。我羡慕那些大鱼，也（在别的、\n更小的池塘里）羡慕那些小鱼，穿梭闪亮，泛着金色。\n\n 他们谈论‘人心中的野兽’，谈论‘丛林法则’。他们\n 莫不（我一边这样想，一边漫步在一方云天下，云色蓝白\n 如天坛的瓷砖与大理石），莫不如谈论\n ‘人中之鱼’？谈论‘海洋法则’？那片据说\n 我们由之而来的海……？”\n    -阿夫拉姆·戴维森，《大衮》，1959年。"},
+    {"“He saw a large cavern and a vaulted [roof], in height equalling the stature\n of a full-grown man and it was hewn in the live stone and lighted up with\n light that came through air-holes and bullseyes in the upper surface of the\n rock which formed the roof. He had expected to find naught save outer gloom in\n this robbers' den, and he was surprised to see the whole room filled with\n bales of all manner stuffs, and heaped up from sole to ceiling with\n camel-loads of silks and brocades and embroidered cloths and mounds on mounds\n of vari-colored carpetings; besides which he espied coins golden and silvern\n without measure or account, some piled upon the ground and others bound in\n leathern bags and sacks. Seeing these goods and moneys in such abundance, Ali\n Baba determined in his mind that not during a few years only but for many\n generations thieves must have stored their gains and spoils in this place.”\n    -_The Arabian Nights_. trans. Sir Richard F. Burton, 1885.", "“他看见一个巨大的洞穴，拱形的[顶]，高度\n抵得上一个成年男子的身量，是在原生岩石上凿出的，\n光线从岩石顶面上的通风孔和圆窗透进来，将洞中照亮。\n他本以为在这盗贼巢穴里只会见到一片昏暗，\n却惊讶地发现整个洞室堆满了各式各样的货物，\n从地面一直堆到洞顶，有驮载成捆的丝绸、锦缎和\n绣花布料，还有一垛又一垛五色的地毯；\n此外他还望见数不清、算不尽的金币银币，\n有的堆在地上，有的装进\n皮袋和麻袋里。眼见这些货物钱财如此丰盈，阿里\n巴巴心中断定，盗贼们必定不止在此藏了几年，\n而是好多代人积攒的赃物都存放在这里。”\n    -《一千零一夜》。理查德·F. 伯顿爵士 译，1885年。"},
+    {"“The hole now stood some three meters in diameter beginning from the dais -\n though its outline shrank and dilated slightly like a pulsing, living thing.\n Through it we could see shapes seething and changing constantly with the\n colors and the light. Purple trees grew upside down from a pink sky, changing\n into black flowers and the flowers into feathery tongues of giant yellow worms\n lumbering over a shaking, exploding desert. It was as if the world couldn't\n make up its mind what to be yet. Looking at the scene for even a moment gave\n me a headache, and anyone looking at it for long would surely go mad.”\n    -Laurence Yep, _Dragon War_. 1992.", "“那个洞如今直径约有从讲台算起三米——\n虽说它的轮廓会像活物般搏动，微微收缩又扩张。\n透过它，我们能看见各种形状翻腾搅动，随着\n色彩和光不断变化。紫色的树从粉色的天空中倒着长出，\n又变成黑色的花，花又变成巨大的黄色蠕虫般羽毛状的舌头，\n在震颤、爆裂的沙漠上笨重地爬行。仿佛世界还没\n拿定主意该成为什么模样。哪怕只看这景象片刻也让我\n头疼，而任何人长久地看下去，肯定都会发疯。”\n    -劳伦斯·耶普，《龙战》。1992年。"},
+    {"“I know not whether Laws be right,\n   Or whether Laws be wrong;\n All that we know who lie in gaol\n   Is that the wall is strong;\n And that each day is like a year,\n   A year whose days are long.”\n    -Oscar Wilde, “The Ballad of Reading Gaol”. 1898.", "“我不知法律是对是错，\n   也不知法律是否不公；\n 我们这些身陷牢狱的人只知\n   那墙壁坚固牢靠；\n 也只知每一天都长得像一年，\n   一年里的日子多么漫长。”\n    -奥斯卡·王尔德，《雷丁监狱之歌》。1898年。"},
+    {"“Then I began taking off my hands. I put them in the top drawer. I removed my\n feet and put them into the closet, along with my legs. I put my head in a box\n and hung my torso up on a hanger. Then I walked out of the apartment, climbed\n up the stairs to the roof. I opened the door and stepped out into a beautiful\n green meadow.”\n    -Celestial Navigations, “The Sniper”. 1995.", "“然后我开始把自己的双手卸下来。我把它们放进最上面的抽屉。我把\n双脚取下，连同双腿一起放进壁橱。我把头装进一个盒子，\n把躯干挂在衣架上。然后我走出公寓，爬上\n楼梯来到屋顶。我打开门，走进一片美丽的\n绿色草地。”\n    -《天界航行》，《狙击手》。1995年。"},
+    {"“Gravity, a mere nuisance to Christian, was a terror to Pope, Pagan, and\n Despair. To the mouse and any smaller animal it presents practically no\n dangers. You can drop a mouse down a thousand-yard mine shaft; and, on\n arriving at the bottom it gets a slight shock and walks away, provided\n that the ground is fairly soft. A rat is killed, a man is broken, a\n horse splashes.”\n    -J.B.S. Haldane, “On Being The Right Size”. March 1926.", "“重力，对基督徒不过是小小的麻烦，对教皇、异教徒和\n绝望却是莫大的恐怖。对老鼠和任何更小的动物，它几乎\n不构成危险。你可以把一只老鼠丢进千码深的矿井；\n它落到井底时只会稍稍一震，然后走开，前提\n是地面足够柔软。老鼠会摔死，人会摔碎，\n马会摔成一摊血水。”\n    -J.B.S. 霍尔丹，《论大小得当》。1926年3月。"},
+    {"the wise man bowed his head solemnly and spoke:\n “theres actually zero difference between good & bad things.\n you imbecile. you fucking moron”\n    -wint, @dril Twitter. June 1, 2014\n# https://twitter.com/dril/status/473265809079693312/", "智者郑重地低下头，说道：\n“好事物与坏事物之间其实根本没有区别。\n 你这蠢货。你这他妈的弱智”\n    -wint，@dril Twitter。2014年6月1日\n# https://twitter.com/dril/status/473265809079693312/"},
+    {"“We shall not cease from exploration\n And the end of all our exploring\n Will be to arrive where we started\n And know the place for the first time.”\n    -T.S. Eliot, “Little Gidding”, _Four Quartets_. 1943.", "“我们不会停止探索\n 而我们一切探索的终点\n 将是抵达我们出发的地方\n 并第一次认识那地方。”\n    -T.S. 艾略特，《小吉丁》，《四个四重奏》。1943年。"},
+    {"<Temple>", "<神殿>"},
+    {"<Elven Halls>", "<精灵殿堂>"},
+    {"<Orcish Mines>", "<兽人矿坑>"},
+    {"<Shoals>", "<浅滩>"},
+    {"“A tomb now suffices him for whom the world was not enough.”\n    -Alexander the Great's epitaph.", "“世界尚不足以容纳之人，如今一抔坟墓便已足够。”\n    -亚历山大大帝的墓志铭。"},
+    {"“The storm irresistibly propels him into the future to which his back is\n turned, while the pile of debris before him grows skyward. This storm is what\n we call progress.”\n    -Walter Benjamin, “Theses on the Philosophy of History”. 1940.\n   trans. Harry Zohn, 1969.", "“风暴不可抗拒地把他推向他背对着的未来，\n 而他面前的残骸堆却高耸入云。这场风暴，我们\n 称之为进步。”\n    -瓦尔特·本雅明，《历史哲学论纲》。1940年。\n   哈里·佐恩 译，1969年。"},
+    {"“I like trees because they seem more resigned to the way they have to live than\n other things do.”\n    -Willa Cather, _O Pioneers!_. 1913.\n########################################\n#\n# Dungeon branches\n#\n########################################", "“我喜欢树，因为它们似乎比别的生灵\n更能安于自己不得不过的生活方式。”\n    -薇拉·凯瑟，《啊，拓荒者！》。1913年。\n########################################\n#\n# Dungeon branches\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"“Out here in the zones, you never even knew when your bone marrow might book an\n exotic vacation and neglect to invite you along. Thinking square thoughts under\n a violet endomoon could redefine every third cell in your body as a verb. There\n were sneakers that ate feet, bifocals that ate eyeballs, strawberry yogurt that\n ate small intestines. Farther out there, _so_ much farther, there were\n wavelengths of something ancestral to light that could crawl into your past and\n eat the day you were born.\n\n Nobody so much as batted an eye or quivered a flange when these things happened\n to the unnaturally branchinated, the puzzling and comical tourists that blipped\n in and out from the forgettable grey. Things like herself, or Fern, or the\n plank maze loser were little more than _weird bugs_ to most entities, a quaint\n curiosity at best and more often an unwanted parasite. Infinite layers of\n reality, and it still felt, in the worst way, like she'd never left home.”\n    -Jonathan Wojcik, _Awful Hospital_. 2021.", "“在这片区域里，你根本不知道什么时候你的骨髓会订下\n一趟奇异的假期，却忘了邀请你同行。在紫色的内月之下思索\n方正的思想，能把你体内每三个细胞就重新定义为一个动词。这里\n有吃脚的球鞋，吃眼珠的眼镜，吃小肠的\n草莓酸奶。再往外，_远_得多的地方，还有\n某种比光更古老的东西的波长，能爬进你的过去，\n吃掉你出生的那一天。\n\n当这些事发生在那些天生多枝的人、那些从遗忘的灰色中\n忽隐忽现的古怪又滑稽的游客身上时，谁也不曾眨一下眼，或\n颤一下边沿。像她、像弗恩、或者那个\n木板迷宫输家这样的东西，在大多数实体眼里不过是_奇怪的虫子_，\n往好里说是新奇的稀奇物，更多时候是不受欢迎的寄生虫。现实有无穷的层次，\n而她仍然觉得，而且是以最糟的方式觉得，自己从未离开过家。”\n    -乔纳森·沃伊奇克，《糟糕医院》。2021年。"},
+    {"“In the salt mines\n I saw the salt\n in this shaker.\n I know you won’t believe me,\n but there\n it sings,\n the salt sings, the skin\n of the salt mines\n sings\n with a mouth choking\n on dirt.\n Alone\n when I heard\n the voice\n of salt,\n I trembled\n in the empty\n desert.\n Near Antafagusta\n the whole\n salted plain\n shouts out\n in its\n cracked\n voice\n a pitiful\n song.”\n    -Pablo Neruda, “Ode to Salt”, 1957.\n     trans. Philip Levine, published 2013", "“在盐矿里\n 我看见了盐\n 就在这盐罐中。\n 我知道你不会信我，\n 可它\n 在歌唱，\n 盐在歌唱，盐矿的\n 表皮\n 在歌唱\n 用一张被泥土\n 噎住的嘴。\n 独自一人\n 当我听见\n 盐的\n 声音，\n 我在空旷的\n 沙漠里\n 颤抖。\n 在安托法加斯塔附近\n 整片\n 盐碱平原\n 呼喊出\n 它那\n 嘶哑\n 的嗓音\n 一首可怜的\n 歌。”\n    -巴勃罗·聂鲁达，《盐颂》，1957年。\n     菲利普·莱文 译，2013年出版"},
+    {"“Elves are wonderful. They provoke wonder.\n Elves are marvelous. They cause marvels.\n Elves are fantastic. They create fantasies.\n Elves are glamorous. They project glamour.\n Elves are enchanting. They weave enchantment.\n Elves are terrific. They beget terror.\n The thing about words is that meanings can twist just like a snake, and if you\n want to find snakes look for them behind words that have changed their meaning.\n No one ever said elves are _nice_.\n Elves are _bad_.”\n    -Terry Pratchett, _Lords and Ladies_. 1992.", "“精灵是奇妙的。它们激起惊奇。\n精灵是惊人的。它们制造奇迹。\n精灵是奇幻的。它们创造幻象。\n精灵是迷人的。它们散播魅惑。\n精灵是令人着魔的。它们编织魔法。\n精灵是可怕的。它们孕育恐怖。\n词语这玩意儿，意义会像蛇一样扭曲，而如果你\n想找蛇，就去那些已经变了意思的词背后找。\n从来没人说过精灵是_善良_的。\n精灵是_坏_的。”\n    -特里·普拉切特，《贵族与淑女》。1992年。"},
+    {"“You load sixteen tons, what do you get\n Another day older and deeper in debt\n Saint Peter don't you call me 'cause I can't go\n I owe my soul to the company store.”\n    -Merle Travis, “Sixteen Tons”. 1946.", "“你装上十六吨煤，换来的是什么\n 不过是又老一天，债又欠得更深\n 圣彼得你别叫我，因为我走不了\n 我把灵魂抵押给了公司商店。”\n    -默尔·特拉维斯，《十六吨》。1946年。"},
+    {"“I often think about that old metaphor, the one that says we are all islands on\n a wide sea. Especially these days, now that things are more difficult than\n before and the world appears to be harsher than we once imagined it to be.\n\n We are all like islands, the philosopher said. Perhaps it's true. Yet I cannot\n help but remember an older saying scratched on a cave wall somewhere by a\n long- forgotten prophet: In the end the sea will claim everything.\n\n The ancient words crash into my mind like waves, waking me from sleep, filling\n me with feelings I cannot fully understand. We are like islands. Does it mean\n we are connected? Do we share a common origin? Or just the common fate of\n sinking?”\n    -“The Sea Will Claim Everything”. 2012.", "“我时常想起那个古老的比喻，它说我们都是广阔大海上的岛屿。尤其是在\n 如今，当事情比以往更加艰难，世界似乎也比我们曾经想象的\n 更加严酷之时。\n\n 那位哲学家说，我们就像岛屿。或许确实如此。然而我不禁想起一位被\n 遗忘已久的先知刻在某处洞壁上的更古老的话：最终，大海将\n 吞没一切。\n\n 那些古老的话语如浪潮般冲击我的脑海，将我从睡梦中唤醒，让我充满\n 我无法完全理解的情感。我们就像岛屿。这是否意味着我们彼此相连？\n 我们是否拥有共同的起源？还是只有沉没这共同的\n 命运？”\n    -“大海将吞没一切”。2012。"},
+    {"“There is a drear and lonely tract of hell\n From all the common gloom removed afar:\n A flat, sad land it is, where shadows are,\n Whose lorn estate my verse may never tell.”\n    -Edward Arlington Robinson, “Supremacy”. 1897.", "“地狱中有一片凄凉孤寂之地\n 远离一切寻常的幽暗：\n 那是一片平坦而悲伤的土地，阴影盘踞其间，\n 它凄凉的境况，我的诗篇永远无法诉说。”\n    -爱德华·阿灵顿·罗宾逊，《至高无上》。1897。"},
+    {"“And I heard a great voice out of the temple saying to the seven angels, Go\n your ways, and pour out the vials of the wrath of God upon the earth.”\n    -KJV Bible, Revelations 16:1.", "“我听见有大声音从殿中出来，向那七位天使说：你们去，把盛神大怒的\n七碗倒在地上。”\n    -钦定版圣经，启示录 16:1。"},
+    {"“In the depths of every heart, there is a tomb and a dungeon, though the\n lights, the music, and revelry above may cause us to forget their existence,\n and the buried ones, or prisoners whom they hide. But sometimes, and oftenest\n at midnight, those dark receptacles are flung wide open. In an hour like this,\n when the mind has a passive sensibility, but no active strength; when the\n imagination is a mirror, imparting vividness to all ideas, without the power\n of selecting or controlling them; then pray that your grieves may slumber, and\n the brotherhood of remorse not break their chain.”\n    -Nathaniel Hawthorne, “The Haunted Mind”. 1835.\n########################################\n#\n# Spells and abilities\n#\n########################################", "“在每颗心灵的深处，都有一座坟墓和一座地牢，\n 尽管上方的灯火、音乐与狂欢或许会让我们忘记它们的存在，\n 忘记它们所掩埋的逝者或囚徒。但有时，往往是在午夜，\n 那些黑暗的容器会大大敞开。在这样的时刻，当心灵只有\n 被动的感受力，而无主动的力量；当想象是一面镜子，\n 赋予一切念头以鲜活，却无力选择或控制它们；那么就\n 祈祷你的悲伤沉睡，\n 祈祷悔恨的同胞之情不会挣脱锁链。”\n    -纳撒尼尔·霍桑，《闹鬼的心灵》。1835。\n########################################\n#\n# 法术与能力\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"<Curse of Agony spell>", "<痛苦诅咒法术>"},
+    {"Despite its unwieldy appearance, it is widely considered Alistair's most popular\nwork; countless variations of this spell can be found in surface libraries,\ncustomised to prepare everything from floor polish to vegetable soup. In the\ndepths of the dungeon, however, most mages favor the combat utility of the\nclassic iteration.", "尽管其外表笨拙，它仍被广泛认为是阿利斯泰尔最受欢迎的\n作品；地表图书馆中能找到这法术的无数变体，被改造来制作\n从地板蜡到蔬菜汤的各种东西。然而在地牢深处，\n大多数法师更青睐经典版本\n在战斗中的实用性。"},
+    {"“Remember, I am not recording the vision of a madman. The sun does not more\n certainly shine in the heavens, than that which I now affirm is true. Some\n miracle might have produced it, yet the stages of the discovery were distinct\n and probable. After days and nights of incredible labour and fatigue, I\n succeeded in discovering the cause of generation and life; nay, more, I became\n myself capable of bestowing animation upon lifeless matter.”\n    -Mary Shelley, _Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus_,\n     Vol. I, Chapter 3. 1818 (1st Ed.)", "“请记住，我记录的并非疯子的幻象。我所断言的真实，绝不比\n 太阳在天空中照耀更不确定。或许是某种奇迹造就了它，但这一\n 发现的各个阶段都清晰而可信。在经历了无数日夜难以置信的\n 辛劳与疲惫之后，我成功发现了生殖与生命的成因；\n 不仅如此，我自己也变得能够赋予无生命之物\n 以活力。”\n    -玛丽·雪莱，《弗兰肯斯坦，或现代普罗米修斯》，\n     第一卷，第三章。1818（第一版）"},
+    {"\"A blacksmith shapes the heart of their steel just as much as any leader shapes\nthe hearts of their soldiers. If this armour of mine could speak, I know it\nwould say that we fight for the same cause.\"", "“铁匠塑造钢铁的核心，正如任何领袖塑造士兵的心灵。\n如果我这身盔甲会说话，我知道它\n会说我们为同样的事业而战。”"},
+    {" • Hel: [She died to] Infection! She's mine!\n — Thor: From a splinter that she got bravely fighting an elm!\n • Hel: Trees are inanimate plants, you buffoon!\n — Thor: Bravery knows no limits!\n    -Rich Burlew, _Order of the Stick_, #874.", " • 海拉：[她死于]感染！她是我的人！\n — 托尔：那是因为她在勇敢地与一棵榆树搏斗时扎了一根刺！\n • 海拉：树木是无生命的植物，你这蠢货！\n — 托尔：勇敢没有界限！\n    -里奇·伯利，《棍子骑士团》，#874。"},
+    {"“An immense river of oblivion is sweeping us away into a nameless abyss.”\n    -Ernest Renan, Souvenirs", "“一条巨大的遗忘之河正将我们卷入无名的深渊。”\n    -欧内斯特·勒南，《回忆录》"},
+    {"“The bats have left the bell tower\n The victims have been bled”\n    -Bauhaus, “Bela Lugosi's Dead”. 1979.", "“蝙蝠已离开钟楼\n 受害者已流尽鲜血”\n    -包豪斯乐队，《贝拉·卢戈西已死》。1979。"},
+    {"“It's astounding\n Time is fleeting\n Madness takes its toll”\n    -Richard O'Brien, “Time Warp”. 1975.", "“多么惊人\n 时光飞逝\n 疯狂自有其代价”\n    -理查德·奥布莱恩，《时间扭曲》。1975。"},
+    {"“Lister: I know what you want. It's pink and it's moist and it's in my head. And\n         that's where it's staying.”\n    -Red Dwarf, _Psirens_. 1993.", "“李斯特：我知道你想要什么。它是粉色的，湿漉漉的，就在我的脑子里。\n 而它哪儿也不去。”\n    -《红矮星号》，《灵能海妖》。1993。"},
+    {"“There seemed a strange stillness over everything. But as I listened, I heard\n as if from down below in the valley the howling of many wolves. The Count's\n eyes gleamed, and he said.\n\n ‘Listen to them, the children of the night. What music they make!’ Seeing, I\n suppose, some expression in my face strange to him, he added, ‘Ah, sir, you\n dwellers in the city cannot enter into the feelings of the hunter.’”\n    -Bram Stoker, _Dracula_. 1897.", "“一切似乎都笼罩在一种奇异的寂静之中。但当我侧耳倾听时，我仿佛听见\n 从下方山谷中传来许多狼的嚎叫。伯爵双目闪光，\n 说道。\n\n ‘听啊，夜之子民。它们奏出的何等音乐！’我想他大概是看见了\n 我脸上某种令他觉得陌生的表情，便补充道：‘啊，先生，\n 你们这些城市居民是无法体会猎人的感受的。’”\n    -布拉姆·斯托克，《德古拉》。1897。"},
+    {"“And when Miranda sang\n Everyone turned away\n Used to the noose, they obey”\n    -The Mars Volta, “Miranda That Ghost Just Isn't Holy Anymore”. 2005.", "“当米兰达歌唱\n 所有人都转过身去\n 习惯了绞索，他们服从”\n    -火星电压乐队，《米兰达那鬼魂再也不神圣了》。2005。"},
+    {"“The trouble ain't that there is too many fools,\n but that the lightning ain't distributed right.”\n    -traditionally attributed to Samuel Clemens.", "“问题不在于傻瓜太多，\n 而在于闪电分配得不均匀。”\n    -传统上归于塞缪尔·克莱门斯。"},
+    {"“He held up his hand, and they all stopped, and I thought he seemed to be\n saying, ‘All these lives will I give you, ay, and many more and greater,\n through countless ages, if you will fall down and worship me!’ And then a red\n cloud, like the colour of blood, seemed to close over my eyes, and before I\n knew what I was doing, I found myself opening the sash and saying to Him,\n ‘Come in, Lord and Master!’”\n    -Bram Stoker, _Dracula_. 1897.", "“他举起手，他们全都停了下来，我觉得他似乎正在说：\n ‘所有这些生命我都将赐予你，是的，还有更多更伟大的生命，\n 历经无数世代，只要你俯首崇拜我！’接着，一片血色的红云\n 似乎罩住了我的双眼，还没等我意识到自己在做什么，\n 我已发现自己打开了窗扇，对他说：\n ‘进来吧，主与主人！’”\n    -布拉姆·斯托克，《德古拉》。1897。"},
+    {"“I fumbled to the window to experience the world\n And to hear my Madness singing, sitting on the kerbstone\n [A blind old drunken man who sings and mutters,\n  With broken boot heels stained in many gutters]\n And as he sang the world began to fall apart . . .”\n    -T.S. Eliot, “Prufrock's Pervigilium”. 1912 (published 1996).", "“我摸索着走向窗户去体验这个世界\n 去听我的疯狂在路缘石上歌唱\n [一个失明的老醉汉，歌唱着，喃喃自语，\n  破靴子的后跟沾满了许多阴沟的污渍]\n 当他歌唱时，世界开始分崩离析……”\n    -T.S. 艾略特，《普鲁弗洛克的守夜》。1912（1996年出版）。"},
+    {"“Unbearable, isn't it? The suffering of strangers, the agony of friends. There\n is a secret song at the center of the world, Joey, and its sound is like\n razors through flesh.”\n    -Pinhead, _Hellraiser 3: Hell on Earth_. 1992.", "“难以忍受，不是吗？陌生人的苦难，朋友的痛苦。\n 乔伊，世界的中心有一首秘密之歌，它的声音就像\n 剃刀划过血肉。”\n    -钉子头，《养鬼吃人3：地狱人间》。1992。"},
+    {"“This is where the dragons went.\n They lie...\n Not dead, not asleep. Not waiting, because waiting implies expectation.\n Possibly the word we're looking for here is...\n ...dormant.\n And although the space they occupy isn't like normal space, nevertheless\n   they are packed in tightly. Not a cubic inch there but is filled by a claw,\n   a talon, a scale, the tip of a tail, so the effect is like one of those\n   trick drawings and your eyeballs eventually realise that the space between\n   each dragon is, in fact, another dragon.\n They could put you in mind of a can of sardines, if you thought sardines\n   were huge and scaly and proud and arrogant.\n And presumably, somewhere, there's the key.”\n    -Terry Pratchett, “Guards! Guards!”. 1990.", "“龙就是去了这里。\n 它们躺着……\n 没有死，没有睡。也没有等待，因为等待意味着期盼。\n 也许我们在这里要找的词是……\n ……休眠。\n 尽管它们占据的空间不像寻常空间，\n 但它们仍被紧紧地塞在一起。那里没有一立方英寸不被爪子、\n 利爪、鳞片、尾尖填满，效果就像那些错觉画，\n 你的眼球最终会意识到，每条龙之间的空隙，\n 实际上也是另一条龙。\n 它们可能会让你想起一罐沙丁鱼，前提是你认为沙丁鱼\n 是巨大、遍体鳞片、骄傲而傲慢的。\n 而想必，在某处，有那把钥匙。”\n    -特里·普拉切特，《卫兵！卫兵！》。1990。"},
+    {"DUEL, n. A formal ceremony preliminary to the reconciliation of two enemies.\nGreat skill is necessary to its satisfactory observance; if awkwardly\nperformed the most unexpected and deplorable consequences sometimes ensue.\nA long time ago, a man lost his life in a duel.\n    -Ambrose Bierce, _The Devil's Dictionary_, 1906.", "决斗，名词。两名敌人和解之前的一场正式仪式。\n要令人满意地完成它，需要高超的技巧；若笨拙地\n施行，有时会引发最出人意料、最可悲的后果。\n很久以前，有一个人在一场决斗中丧了命。\n    -安布罗斯·比尔斯，《魔鬼辞典》，1906。"},
+    {"“Sweet dreams are made of this; who am I to disagree?”\n    -Eurythmics, “Sweet Dreams (Are Made of This)”. 1983.", "“美梦由此铸成；我又凭什么反对？”\n    -韵律操乐队，《甜梦（由此铸成）》。1983。"},
+    {"A popular legend states that Eringya first demonstrated this spell as the\nclosing remark of a particularly acrimonious debate about wetlands conservation,\nbut most modern scholars consider this apocryphal.", "一则广为流传的传说称，埃林吉娅首次展示此法术，是在一场关于\n湿地保护的格外激烈的辩论中作为结束语；但大多数现代学者\n认为这不足为信。"},
+    {"“Some have said there is no subtlety to destruction. You know what? They're\n dead.”\n    -Jaya Ballard, task mage (Magic: the Gathering).", "“有人说毁灭毫无精妙可言。\n 你知道吗？他们都死了。”\n    -贾雅·巴拉德，任务法师（万智牌）。"},
+    {"”And as you cross the circle line\n Well, the ice wall creaks behind\n You're a rabbit on the run”\n\n    -Jethro Tull, “Skating Away (on the Thin Ice of a New Day)”.  1974.", "”当你穿过环线\n 冰墙在身后嘎吱作响\n 你是一只奔逃的兔子”\n\n    -杰思罗·塔尔，《溜走（在新一天的薄冰上）》。1974。"},
+    {"“Reflex in the sky warn you you're gonna die\n Storm coming, you'd better hide from the atomic tide\n Flashes in the sky turns houses into sties\n Turns people into clay, radiation minds decay”\n    -Black Sabbath, “Electric Funeral”. 1970.", "“天空中的反光警告你，你将要死去\n 风暴来临，你最好躲避这原子浪潮\n 天空中的闪光把房屋变成猪圈\n 把人变成泥土，辐射令心智衰败”\n    -黑色安息日，《电葬礼》。1970。"},
+    {"“Maxim 4: Close air support covereth a multitude of sins.”\n    -Howard Tayler, _The Seventy Maxims of Maximally Effective Mercenaries_,\n     in _Schlock Mercenary_. 2008.", "“格言4：近距空中支援能掩盖诸多罪过。”\n    -霍华德·泰勒，《最高效雇佣兵的七十条格言》，\n     出自《薛克雇佣兵》。2008。"},
+    {"“Now the house was full of men and women; and all the lords of the Philistines\n were there; and there were upon the roof about three thousand men and women,\n that beheld while Samson made sport.\n\n And Samson called unto the LORD, and said, O Lord GOD, remember me, I pray\n thee, and strengthen me, I pray thee, only this once, O God, that I may be at\n once avenged of the Philistines for my two eyes.\n\n And Samson took hold of the two middle pillars upon which the house stood, and\n on which it was borne up, of the one with his right hand, and of the other\n with his left.\n\n And Samson said, Let me die with the Philistines. And he bowed himself with\n all his might; and the house fell upon the lords, and upon all the people that\n were therein. So the dead which he slew at his death were more than they which\n he slew in his life.”\n    -KJV Bible, Judges 16:27-30.", "“那时房内充满了男女，非利士人的众首领也都在那里；\n 房的平顶上约有三千男女，\n 观看参孙戏耍。\n\n 参孙求告耶和华说：主耶和华啊，求你眷念我。\n 神啊，求你赐我这一次的力量，\n 使我在非利士人身上报那剜我双眼的仇。\n\n 参孙就抱住托房的那两根柱子，\n 左手抱一根，\n 右手抱一根，\n\n 说：我情愿与非利士人同死。于是他尽力屈身，\n 房子就倒塌，压住首领和房内的众人。\n 这样，参孙死时所杀的人，\n 比活着所杀的还多。”\n    -钦定版圣经，士师记 16:27-30。"},
+    {"“Wie die Mächtigen es schon seit jeher wissen,\n Es gibt immer einen Kniff;\n Denn als Guter bist du niemals so gerissen,\n Ein Zauberpfeil der immer trifft!\n\n [As the powerful have known all along,\n There is always a trick;\n Because as a good person, you'll never be so cunning,\n A magic arrow that always hits!]”\n    -ASP, \"Verwandlungen I-III\". 2008\n\n# translation by me, nicolae", "“Wie die Mächtigen es schon seit jeher wissen,\n Es gibt immer einen Kniff;\n Denn als Guter bist du niemals so gerissen,\n Ein Zauberpfeil der immer trifft!\n\n [正如权贵们自古以来就知道的，\n 总有一个诀窍；\n 因为作为好人，你永远不会那么狡猾，\n 一支永远命中的魔法之箭！]”\n    -ASP, \"Verwandlungen I-III\". 2008\n\n# translation by me, nicolae"},
+    {"“Go to, let us go down, and there confound their language, that they may not\n understand one another's speech. So the LORD scattered them abroad from thence\n upon the face of all the earth: and they left off to build the city.”\n    -KJV Bible, Genesis 11:7-8.", "“我们下去，在那里变乱他们的口音，使他们的言语彼此不通。\n 于是，耶和华使他们从那里分散在全地上；\n 他们就停工，不造那城了。”\n    -钦定版圣经，创世记 11:7-8。"},
+    {"While originally designed for use in industrial construction, enterprising\ncopycats were quick to discover a multitude of other problems that could be\nsolved by moving one object into another, very, very quickly.", "虽然最初是为工业建筑设计的，但富有进取心的\n模仿者们很快发现，还有许许多多其他的问题，只要\n把一个物体以非常非常快的速度射入另一个物体，就能迎刃而解。"},
+    {"“Seit mehreren Jahren schon hatte die indische Cholera eine verstärkte\n Neigung zur Ausbreitung und Wanderung an den Tag gelegt. Erzeugt aus\n den warmen Moraesten des Ganges-Deltas, aufgestiegen mit dem\n mephitischen Odem jener üppig-untauglichen, von Menschen gemiedenen\n Urwelt- und Inselwildnis, in deren Bambusdickichten der Tiger kauert,\n hatte die Seuche in ganz Hindustan andauernd und ungewöhnlich heftig\n gewütet, hatte östlich nach China, westlich nach Afghanistan und\n Persien übergegriffen und, den Hauptstraßen des Karawanenverkehrs\n folgend, ihre Schrecken bis Astrachan, ja selbst bis Moskau getragen.”\n    -Thomas Mann, _Der Tod in Venedig_, 1911.", "“多年来，印度霍乱已显示出日益增强的\n 扩散和游移倾向。它产生于\n 恒河三角洲温暖的沼泽，伴随着\n 那肥沃而有害、人迹罕至的\n 原始森林和岛屿荒野的瘴气升起，老虎蜷伏于其中的竹林；\n 这种瘟疫在整个印度斯坦持续而异常猛烈地\n 肆虐，向东蔓延到中国，向西蔓延到阿富汗和\n 波斯，并沿着商队的主要道路\n 把恐怖带到阿斯特拉罕，甚至莫斯科。”\n    -托马斯·曼，_威尼斯之死_，1911年。"},
+    {"“During the course of the treatise, each element is allowed time to tout its\n own virtues and usefulness to mankind, but when it comes time for Mercury,\n he announces that he presides over all of the beings that have come before\n him. Mercury is mother and father. He gives other metals their splendor and\n makes them useful to humanity. Without Mercury, the other metals would have no\n (use) value. Perhaps a seat on the throne would restrict him to a fixed\n position of sovereignty. He reigns, but he must be mobile.”\n    -Karen Pinkus, _Alchemical Mercury: A Theory of Ambivalance_, 2009", "“在论文的进程中，每种元素都有时间吹嘘其\n 自身的优点和对人类的用处，但当轮到墨丘利时，\n 他宣称自己统领着所有先于他存在的造物。\n 墨丘利既是母亲又是父亲。他赋予其他金属光辉，\n 使它们对人类有用。没有墨丘利，其他金属就没有\n （使用）价值。也许坐在王座上会把他限制在固定的\n 主权位置上。他统治，但必须保持流动。”\n    -卡伦·平库斯，_炼金术的墨丘利：一种矛盾心理理论_，2009年"},
+    {"Scholars long considered the hammer mere theatrics to mask the complex\nstructural enhancement magics which must underpin this spell, and yet every\nattempt to remove it from the incantation has caused the spell to lose its\neffectiveness. The implications of this remain hotly debated.", "学者们长期以来认为锤子只是戏剧效果，用来掩盖必然支撑此法术的复杂\n结构强化魔法，然而每一次\n试图从咒语中移除它的尝试都导致法术失去其\n效力。其含义至今仍备受争议。"},
+    {"“He says the best way out is always through.”\n    -Robert Frost, _A Servant to Servants_. 1915.", "“他说，最好的出路永远是穿过去。”\n    -罗伯特·弗罗斯特，_仆人的仆人_。1915年。"},
+    {"“Any pose held too long calcifies.\n A belief believed too long becomes dogma.\n Any expression held long enough will harden into a mask.\n And who is the _real_ us\n that hard exterior\n or the soft uncertain self we keep beneath?”\n    -Dorothy Gambrell, _Cat and Girl_, 2011.", "“任何姿势保持太久都会僵化。\n 一种信念相信太久就会变成教条。\n 任何表情保持足够久都会硬化成面具。\n 而哪一个才是_真正的_我们\n 是那坚硬的外表\n 还是我们藏在下面的柔软而不确定的自我？”\n    -多萝西·甘布雷尔，_猫与女孩_，2011年。"},
+    {"“I saw in a hall an arrow pointing the way and I thought that this inoffensive\n symbol had once been a thing of iron, an inescapable and fatal projectile that\n pierced the flesh of men and lions and clouded the sun at Thermopylae and gave\n Harald Sigurdarson six feet of English earth forever.”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, _Mutations_. 1960.\n     trans. Mildred Boyle", "“我看见大厅里有一个指路的箭头，我想这个不起眼的\n 符号曾经是铁制之物，一种无法逃避的致命投射物，\n 它刺穿人和狮子的血肉，在温泉关遮天蔽日，并让\n 哈拉尔·西居尔兹松永远得到六英尺英国土地。”\n    -豪尔赫·路易斯·博尔赫斯，_变异_。1960年。\n     米尔德里德·博伊尔 译"},
+    {"“Every thing is related in them which bears reference to my accursed origin;\n the whole detail of that series of disgusting circumstances which produced it\n is set in view; the minutest description of my odious and loathsome person is\n given, in language which painted your own horrors, and rendered mine\n ineffaceable. I sickened as I read. ‘Hateful day when I received life!’ I\n exclaimed in agony. ‘Cursed creator! Why did you form a monster so hideous that\n even you turned from me in disgust? God in pity made man beautiful and\n alluring, after his own image; but my form is a filthy type of yours, more\n horrid from its very resemblance. Satan had his companions, fellow-devils, to\n admire and encourage him; but I am solitary and detested.’”\n    -Mary Shelley, _Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus_,\n     Vol. II, Chapter 7. 1818 (1st Ed.)", "“其中每件与我那可诅咒的起源有关的事都被联系起来；\n 产生它的那一连串恶心情境的全部细节\n 都展现出来；对我那可憎可厌的形貌的最细微描述也\n 被给出，用的语言描绘出你自己的恐怖，并使我的\n 无法抹去。我读着读着就恶心起来。‘我获得生命的那天真可恨！’我\n 痛苦地呼喊。‘该死的造物主！你为什么造出一个如此丑陋的怪物，\n 连你自己都厌恶地转身离开？上帝出于怜悯把人造得美丽而\n 诱人，按他自己的形象；但我的形体是你形象的污秽变体，更\n 因其酷似而可怕。撒旦有他的同伴、同伙恶魔来\n 钦佩和鼓励他；而我却孤独并被憎恨。’”\n    -玛丽·雪莱，_弗兰肯斯坦，或现代普罗米修斯_，\n     第二卷，第七章。1818年（第一版）"},
+    {"“So the people shouted when the priests blew with the trumpets: and it came to\n pass, when the people heard the sound of the trumpet, and the people shouted\n with a great shout, that the wall fell down flat, so that the people went up\n into the city, every man straight before him, and they took the city.\n\n And they utterly destroyed all that was in the city, both man and woman, young\n and old, and ox, and sheep, and ass, with the edge of the sword.”\n    -KJV Bible, Joshua 6:20-21.", "“于是百姓呼喊，祭司也吹角。百姓听见角声，便\n 大声呼喊，城墙就塌陷，\n 百姓便上去\n 进城，各人往前直上，将城夺取。\n\n 又将城中所有的，不拘男女老少，牛羊和驴，\n 都用刀杀尽。”\n    -钦定版圣经，约书亚记 6:20-21。"},
+    {"“Rumack: It starts with a slight fever and dryness of the throat. When the\n virus penetrates the red blood cells, the victim becomes dizzy, begins to\n experience an itchy rash, then the poison goes to work on the central nervous\n system, severe muscle spasms followed by the inevitable drooling.\n [Oveur does all of the above as Rumack describes each one]\n Rumack: At this point, the entire digestive system collapses accompanied by\n uncontrollable flatulence.\n [Oveur begins to fart uncontrollably]\n Rumack: Until finally, the poor bastard is reduced to a quivering wasted piece\n of jelly.”\n    -Airplane! 1980.", "“鲁马克：开始时是轻微发烧和喉咙干燥。当\n 病毒侵入红细胞，受害者会头晕，开始\n 出现瘙痒的皮疹，然后毒素作用于中枢神经\n 系统，严重的肌肉痉挛，随后不可避免地流口水。\n [奥维尔在鲁马克描述每一项时都做出相应表现]\n 鲁马克：此时，整个消化系统崩溃，伴随\n 无法控制的胀气。\n [奥维尔开始无法控制地放屁]\n 鲁马克：直到最后，这可怜的家伙变成一滩颤抖的\n 果冻。”\n    -《空前绝后满天飞》 1980年。"},
+    {"“I remember doing the Time Warp\n Drinking those moments when\n The blackness would hit me\n And the void would be calling”\n    -Richard O'Brien, “Time Warp”. 1975.", "“我记得跳着时间扭曲\n 喝着那些时刻，当\n 黑暗击中我\n 虚空在呼唤”\n    -理查德·奥布莱恩，“Time Warp”。1975年。"},
+    {"“Give a man a fire and he's warm for a day, but set fire to him and he's warm\n for the rest of his life.”\n    -Terry Pratchett, “Jingo”. 1997.", "“给人一团火，他能暖和一天；但把他点着，他就能\n 暖和一辈子。”\n    -特里·普拉切特，“Jingo”。1997年。"},
+    {"“It's so dreamy\n Oh, fantasy free me\n So you can't see me\n No, not at all\n In another dimension”\n    -Richard O'Brien, “Time Warp”. 1975.", "“如此梦幻\n 哦，幻想让我自由\n 这样你就看不见我\n 不，完全看不见\n 在另一个维度”\n    -理查德·奥布莱恩，“Time Warp”。1975年。"},
+    {"“'Tis now the very witching time of night,\n When churchyards yawn and hell itself breathes out\n Contagion to this world.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark_, III, 2.", "“现在是夜里最阴森的巫魔时刻，\n 当教堂墓地张开大口，地狱本身也向\n 这世界呼出瘟疫。”\n    -威廉·莎士比亚，_丹麦王子哈姆雷特的悲剧_，第三幕，第二场。"},
+    {"“I am just a copy of a copy of a copy\n Everything I say has come\n before\n Assembled into something into something into something\n I don’t know for certain anymore\n\n I am just a shadow of a shadow of a shadow\n Always trying to catch up with myself\n I am just an echo of an echo of an echo\n Listening to someone’s cry for help\n\n Look what you had to start\n Why all the change of heart?\n You need to play your part\n A copy of a copy of a—”\n    -Nine Inch Nails, “Copy Of A”. 2013\n\n# Sadly, player illusions don't seem to work with the database, so I can't\n# associate this quote with player illusions themselves.", "“我只是一个副本的副本的副本\n 我说的每句话都曾经\n 出现过\n 组装成某个东西某个东西某个东西\n 我不再确定\n\n 我只是一个影子的影子的影子\n 总是试图追上我自己\n 我只是一个回声的回声的回声\n 听着某人的求助呼喊\n\n 看看你不得不开始什么\n 为什么态度全变了？\n 你需要扮演你的角色\n 一个副本的副本的——”\n    -九寸钉，“Copy Of A”。2013年\n\n# Sadly, player illusions don't seem to work with the database, so I can't\n# associate this quote with player illusions themselves."},
+    {"Zoologists famously considered this animal to have been extinct for centuries\nuntil copies of this spell were unearthed among the affairs of a misanthropic\nhermit who'd finally made the wrong noble's hair fall out.\n\nSeveral well-funded expeditions were launched in the wake of the discovery, but\ndespite (or perhaps because of) the sometimes-violent competitiveness these\nnaturalists had for each other, the location of the lizards' nesting grounds\nremains unknown to this day.", "动物学家们曾著名地认为这种动物已经灭绝了几个世纪，\n直到这个法术的副本在一名厌世隐士的遗物中被发现，\n他终于让一位不该惹的贵族头发掉光了。\n\n发现之后，人们发起了几次资金充足的探险，但\n尽管（或许是因为）这些博物学家彼此之间有时会\n发生暴力竞争，这种蜥蜴的筑巢地\n至今仍然不为人知。"},
+    {"“JUST WALK OUT\n you can leave!!!\n  work\n  social thing\n  movies\n  home\n  class\n  dentist\n  clothes shoppi\n  too fancy weed store\n  cops if your quick\n  friend ships\n IF IT SUCKS... HIT DA BRICKS!!\n real winners quit” [sic]\n   -Admin, @dasharez0ne Twitter. March 30, 2018\n# https://twitter.com/dasharez0ne/status/979810839749210112/", "“直接走开\n 你可以离开！！！\n  工作\n  社交活动\n  电影\n  家\n  上课\n  牙医\n  买衣服\n  太高档的大麻店\n  警察，如果你够快\n  友谊\n 如果它很烂……就砸了它！！\n 真正的赢家会退出” [sic]\n   -管理员，@dasharez0ne 推特。2018年3月30日\n# https://twitter.com/dasharez0ne/status/979810839749210112/"},
+    {"“With a bit of a mind flip\n You're into the time slip\n And nothing can ever be the same”\n    -Richard O'Brien, “Time Warp”. 1975.", "“随着一点思维翻转\n 你便进入时间滑移\n 从此一切都不再一样”\n    -理查德·奥布莱恩，“Time Warp”。1975年。"},
+    {"“Water dissolving\n And water removing\n There is water\n At the bottom of the ocean\n Under the water\n Carry the water\n Remove the water\n At the bottom of the ocean”\n    -Talking Heads, “Once in a Lifetime”. 1980.\n\n########################################\n#\n# Items\n#\n########################################", "“水在溶解\n 水在清除\n 有水\n 在海洋底部\n 在水下\n 带走水\n 清除水\n 在海洋底部”\n    -传声头像，“Once in a Lifetime”。1980年。\n\n########################################\n#\n# Items\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"“Gringoire put out his hand for the little bag, but she drew back. ‘Do not\n touch it! It is an amulet, and either you will do mischief to the charm, or it\n will hurt you.’”\n    -Victor Marie Hugo, _Notre Dame de Paris_, Book II, chapter VII “A Wedding\n     Night”. 1831.", "“格兰古瓦伸手去拿那个小袋子，但她缩了回去。‘别\n 碰它！这是一件护身符，你要么会破坏符咒，要么它\n 会伤到你。’”\n    -维克多·马里·雨果，_巴黎圣母院_，第二卷，第七章“婚礼\n     之夜”。1831年。"},
+    {"“He killed the noble Mudjokivis.\n Of the skin he made him mittens,\n Made them with the fur side inside,\n Made them with the skin side outside.\n He, to get the warm side inside,\n Put the inside skin side outside;\n He, to get the cold side outside,\n Put the warm side fur side inside.\n That's why he put the fur side inside,\n Why he put the skin side outside,\n Why he turned them inside outside.”\n    -Anonymous, in Wells' _A Parody Anthology_, p. 120. 1904.", "“他杀死了高贵的穆乔基维斯。\n 他用其皮给自己做连指手套，\n 做的时候把毛的一面朝里，\n 做的时候把皮的一面朝外。\n 他为了让暖和的一面朝里，\n 便把里面的皮面朝外；\n 他为了让寒冷的一面朝外，\n 便把暖和的一面毛面朝里。\n 这就是为什么他把毛面朝里，\n 为什么他把皮面朝外，\n 为什么他把它们里外翻转。”\n    -佚名，载于威尔斯的_模仿诗集_，第120页。1904年。"},
+    {"“(Tell enters with his crossbow)\n W. TELL:\n My precious jewel now, —my chiefest treasure—\n A mark I'll set thee, which the cry of grief\n Could never penetrate,—but thou shalt pierce it,—\n And thou, my trusty bowstring, that so oft\n For sport has served me faithfully and well,\n Desert me not in this dread hour of need,—\n Only be true this once, my own good cord,\n That hast so often wing'd the biting shaft:—\n For shouldst thou fly successless from my hand,\n I have no second to send after thee.”\n    -Friedrich Schiller, _Wilhelm Tell_, IV, iii. 1804.\n     trans. Sir Theodore Martin, 1898.", "“（退尔持弩上场）\n W. 退尔：\n 我珍贵的宝石啊，——我最珍视的宝物——\n 我要在你身上做个记号，悲痛的哭喊\n 也永远无法穿透它，——但你要将它射穿，——\n 还有你，我忠实的弓弦，你曾多少次\n 为消遣而忠实地为我效劳，\n 别在这危急的时刻抛弃我，——\n 只求你这一次忠诚，我自己的好弦，\n 你曾多少次让那噬人的箭矢插上翅膀：——\n 若你从我手中无功而返，\n 我便没有第二根弦可以追你而去。”\n    ——弗里德里希·席勒，《威廉·退尔》，IV, iii。1804年。\n     西奥多·马丁爵士 译，1898年。"},
+    {"“The republic always maintains seven or eight thousand regular troops on the\n frontiers, to prevent the incursions of the Tartars. The King does not\n maintain these troops; he only pays the Heydukes, the Semelles, and the\n Janizaries. The first-mentioned are dressed in blue, with large buttons and\n plates of tin, and have bonnets made of felt upon their heads. They have\n firelocks, and the bardiche, which they say is a very good weapon.”\n    -John Pinkerton, _A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting\n     Voyages and Travels in all parts of the World, many of which are now first\n     translated into English. Digested on a New Plan_. 1808.", "“共和国始终在边境维持七八千正规军，\n 以防鞑靼人入侵。国王并不\n 供养这些军队；他只支付海杜克、塞梅尔和\n 雅尼恰尔人的军饷。前者身穿蓝色制服，缀有大纽扣和锡片，\n 头戴毡帽。他们配备火枪，\n 还有巴尔迪切斧，据说那是一种非常好的武器。”\n    ——约翰·平克顿，《世界各地最佳最有趣的\n     游记与旅行总集，其中多篇今首次\n     译为英文。按新方案编订》。1808年。"},
+    {"“On Carian coins, indeed of quite late date, the labrys, set up on its long\n pillar-like handle, with two dependent fillets, has much the appearance of a\n cult image.”\n    -Sir Arthur John Evans, “Mycenaean tree and pillar cult and its\n     Mediterranean relations,” _Journal of Hellenic Studies_ XXI, p. 109. 1901.", "“在卡里亚的钱币上——确实年代相当晚——双刃斧立在其长\n 柱状的柄上，垂下两条束带，形貌极像一尊\n 祭祀神像。”\n    ——阿瑟·约翰·埃文斯爵士，《迈锡尼的树与柱崇拜及其\n     地中海联系》，《希腊研究杂志》XXI，第109页。1901年。"},
+    {"“On leaf of palm, on sedge-wrought roll;\n On plastic clay and leathern scroll,\n Man wrote his thoughts; the ages passed,\n And lo! the Press was found at last!”\n    -John Greenleaf Whittier, _The Library_, st. 4.", "“在棕榈叶上，在莎草编就的卷上；\n 在可塑的黏土和皮革卷轴上，\n 人写下自己的思想；岁月流转，\n 看哪！印刷机终被发现！”\n    ——约翰·格林利夫·惠蒂埃，《图书馆》，第4节。"},
+    {"“The weapon, thrown at 20 or 30 yards distance, twirled round in the air with\n astonishing velocity, and alighting on the right arm of one of his opponents,\n actually rebounded to a distance not less than 70 or 80 yards, leaving a\n horrible contusion behind, and exciting universal admiration.”\n    -The Sydney Gazette and New South Wales Advertiser, 23 December 1804", "“这件武器在二十或三十码外掷出，以惊人的速度在空中\n 旋转，落在他一名对手的右臂上，\n 竟又反弹到不下七十或八十码之外，留下一片\n 可怕的淤伤，令众人赞叹不已。”\n    ——《悉尼公报暨新南威尔士广告报》，1804年12月23日"},
+    {"“Weapon, shapely, naked, wan!\n Head from the mother's bowels drawn!\n Wooded flesh and metal bone! limb only one, and lip only one!\n Gray-blue leaf by red-heat grown! helve produced from a little seed sown!\n Resting the grass amid and upon,\n To be lean'd, and to lean on.”\n    -Walt Whitman, _Song of the Broad-Axe_, l. 1-6. 1867.", "“武器啊，匀称、赤裸、苍白！\n 头颅从母亲的脏腑中拽出！\n 木质的肉与金属的骨！只有一条肢体，只有一片唇！\n 因炽热而生的灰蓝叶刃！斧柄由一粒播下的种子长成！\n 歇息在草丛之中与草丛之上，\n 供人倚靠，也倚靠着人。”\n    ——沃尔特·惠特曼，《阔斧之歌》，第1-6行。1867年。"},
+    {"“Let who will boast their courage in the field,\n I find but little safety from my shield.\n Nature's, not honour's, law we must obey:\n This made me cast my useless shield away,\n And by a prudent flight and cunning save\n A life, which valour could not, from the grave.\n A better buckler I can soon regain;\n But who can get another life again?”\n    -Archilochos. 7th cent. B.C.\n     trans. William H. Goodwin, 1878.", "“谁愿在战场上夸耀勇气，随他去，\n 我的盾牌却护不了我几分安全。\n 我们须遵从自然的法则，而非荣誉的：\n 这让我丢下无用的盾牌，\n 以审慎的逃遁和机巧，\n 从坟墓中救回勇武救不回的性命。\n 更好的盾牌我很快能再得；\n 可谁又能再得一条性命？”\n    ——阿尔基洛科斯。公元前7世纪。\n     威廉·H·古德温 译，1878年。"},
+    {"“so long suckers! i rev up my motorcylce and create a huge cloud of smoke.\n when the cloud dissipates im lying completely dead on the pavement”\n    -wint, @dril Twitter. July 26, 2016\n# https://twitter.com/dril/status/757914951868485632/", "“再见啦，傻瓜们！我发动摩托车，卷起一大团烟雾。\n 等烟雾散去，我已经直挺挺地死在人行道上了”\n    ——wint，@dril Twitter。2016年7月26日\n# https://twitter.com/dril/status/757914951868485632/"},
+    {"“O Bell my wife, why dost thou flyte?\n   Now is now, and then was then:\n Seek now all the world throughout,\n   Thou kens not clowns from gentlemen:\n They are clad in black, green, yellow and blue,\n   So far above their own degree.\n Once in my life I'll take a view;\n   For I'll have a new cloak about me.”\n    -Anonymous, “The Old Cloak”. 16th Century.", "“哦，贝尔我的妻，你为何争吵？\n   现在是现在，那时是那时：\n 如今你寻遍整个世界，\n   也分不清乡巴佬和绅士：\n 他们穿着黑、绿、黄、蓝，\n   远远高过自己的身份。\n 我这一生也要瞧一瞧；\n   因为我要给自己披上一件新斗篷。”\n    ——佚名，《旧斗篷》。16世纪。"},
+    {"“I have always been fond of the West African proverb: ‘Speak softly and carry a\n big stick; you will go far.’ If I had not carried the big stick, the\n organization would not have gotten behind me, and if I had yelled and\n blustered, as Pankhurst and the similar dishonest lunatics desired, I would\n not have had ten votes.”\n    -Theodore Roosevelt, in a letter to Henry L. Sprague. January 26, 1900.", "“我一直很喜欢那句西非谚语：‘轻声说话，手握大棒；你便能走得远。’\n 倘若我没有握着大棒，组织就不会支持我；\n 倘若我像潘克赫斯特之流的不诚实疯子\n 所希望的那样大喊大叫、虚张声势，\n 我连十张选票都得不到。”\n    ——西奥多·罗斯福，致亨利·L·斯普拉格的信。1900年1月26日。"},
+    {"“He drew his dagger, that was sae sharp,\n   That was sae sharp and meet,\n And drave it into the nut-browne bride,\n   That fell deid at his feit.\n\n ‘Now stay for me, dear Annet,’ he sed,\n   ‘Now stay, my dear,’ he cry'd;\n Then strake the dagger untill his heart,\n   And fell deid by her side.”\n    -English traditional ballad, “Lord Thomas and Fair Annet”, circa 1650.", "“他拔出匕首，锋利无比，\n   锋利而合手，\n 将它刺入那栗色新娘，\n   她当即倒在他脚下死去。\n\n ‘等等我，亲爱的安妮特，’他说，\n   ‘等等我，我的爱人，’他喊道；\n 随即将匕首刺进自己的心，\n   倒在她身旁死去。”\n    ——英格兰传统歌谣，《托马斯勋爵与美丽的安妮特》，约1650年。"},
+    {"“This caliber should be appropriate.”\n    -prince Dajmiech, “Kajko and Kokosz: The Battle with Dajmiech”, Janusz\nChrista", "“这个口径应该合适。”\n    ——达伊米赫王子，《卡伊科与科科什：与达伊米赫之战》，雅努什\n赫里斯塔"},
+    {"“Quemadmodum gladius neminem occidit: occidentis telum est.”\n “A sword by itself does not slay; it is merely the weapon used by the slayer.”\n    -Lucius Annaeus Seneca, _Epistulae Morales ad Lucilium_, Letter LXXXVII:\n     Some arguments in favor of the simple life, l. 30. ca. 65 A.D.\n     trans. Richard Mott Gummere, 1917.", "“Quemadmodum gladius neminem occidit: occidentis telum est.”\n “剑本身并不杀人；它不过是杀人者所用的武器。”\n    ——卢基乌斯·安内乌斯·塞内卡，《致卢基里乌斯的道德书信集》，第LXXXVII封：\n     支持简朴生活的一些论证，第30行。约公元65年。\n     理查德·莫特·古默里 译，1917年。"},
+    {"“At these words he started up, and beheld—not his Sophia—no, nor a Circassian\n maid richly and elegantly attired for the grand Signior's seraglio. No;\n without a gown, in a shift that was somewhat of the coarsest, and none of the\n cleanest, bedewed likewise with some odoriferous effluvia, the produce of the\n day's labour, with a pitchfork in her hand, Molly Seagrim approached.”\n    -Henry Fielding, _The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling_, Book V, ch. X.\n     1749.", "“听到这番话，他猛地起身，却见到的——不是他的索菲娅——不，也不是\n 一位为苏丹后宫盛装华服的切尔克斯少女。不；\n 她没穿长裙，只着一件粗陋之极、也不太干净的\n 衬衣，还沾满了带味的汗气——那是白日劳作的产物——\n 手里握着干草叉，莫莉·西格里姆就这样走来。”\n    ——亨利·菲尔丁，《弃儿汤姆·琼斯传》，第五卷，第十章。\n     1749年。"},
+    {"“With a terrible cry the Balrog fell forward, and its shadow plunged down and\n vanished. But even as it fell it swung its whip, and the thongs lashed and\n curled about the wizard's knees, dragging him to the brink. He staggered, and\n fell, grasped vainly at the stone, and slid into the abyss.”\n    -J.R.R. Tolkien, _The Fellowship of the Ring_. II, 5, “The Bridge of\n     Khazad-dûm”. 1954.", "“伴随着一声可怕的嚎叫，炎魔向前倒下，它的阴影坠落下去，\n 消失不见。但就在倒下之际，它仍挥动长鞭，鞭梢抽打\n 缠绕住巫师的膝盖，将他拖向崖边。他踉跄了一下，\n 倒了下去，徒劳地想抓住岩石，最终滑入了深渊。”\n    ——J.R.R. 托尔金，《魔戒现身》。第二卷第5章，《卡扎督姆之桥》。\n     1954年。"},
+    {"“‘Ah! ah! ah!’ laughed his two men, ‘how the Norman villains will be humbled\nwhen they see their doughty knight's skull beaten in by our brave countryman.’”\n    -_Tales of Chivalry; or, Perils by Flood and Field_. 1830.", "“‘啊！啊！啊！’他的两名手下笑道，‘等那些诺曼恶棍看到他们勇武骑士的脑袋被我们勇敢的同胞\n打扁时，他们会多么灰头土脸啊。’”\n    ——《骑士传奇；或，水陆历险记》。1830年。"},
+    {"“I never knew, I never thought,\n Such bliss as this could fill me with a love divine,\n I'm afraid I'll wake and find it was only in my mind,\n Was it a dream, or are you really mine?”\n    -Dick Powell, \"Was It a Dream?\". 1928.", "“我从未知晓，我从未想过，\n 这样的幸福能以上天的爱意充满我，\n 我害怕醒来后发现那只是我的想象，\n 这是一场梦，还是你真的属于我？”\n    ——迪克·鲍威尔，《这是一场梦吗？》。1928年。"},
+    {"“It is said to have been the favourite weapon of the Norman priest, who,\n objecting to the shedding of blood, had no scruple about the dashing out of\n brains.”\n    -T. M. Allison, “The Flail and Kindred Tools (from a historical and\n     literary standpoint)”, _Archaeologia Aeliana_, Third Series, vol. IV.\n     1908.", "“据说它曾是那位诺曼神父最钟爱的武器；他\n 反对流血，却对砸出\n 脑浆毫无顾忌。”\n    ——T. M. 阿利森，《连枷及其同类工具（从历史与\n     文学角度）》，《阿埃利亚考古学》，第三辑，第IV卷。\n     1908年。"},
+    {"“‘But I am frightened of what is dark, urZah.’\n\n ‘With reason,’ the urRu replied. ‘Darkness imprisons the light. Darkness\n  destroys all beings, covets all energy. It is evil.’\n\n ‘What is evil?’ Jen asked.\n\n ‘Evil does not exist,’ urZah answered. ‘Evil is disharmony between existences.\n  Now go, Gelfling, with your questions.’”\n    -A. C. H. Smith, _Jim Henson’s “The Dark Crystal”: The Novelization_. 1982.", "“‘可我害怕黑暗，urZah。’\n\n ‘有理由如此，’urRu 答道。‘黑暗囚禁光明。黑暗\n 摧毁一切生灵，觊觎一切能量。它是邪恶的。’\n\n ‘什么是邪恶？’Jen 问道。\n\n ‘邪恶并不存在，’urZah 回答。‘邪恶是存在之间的不和谐。\n 现在走吧，Gelfling，带着你的问题。’”\n    ——A. C. H. 史密斯，《吉姆·亨森的〈黑水晶〉：小说化》。1982年。"},
+    {"“She danced, and was compelled to dance—to dance in the dark night. The shoes\n carried her on over thorn and brier; she scratched herself till she bled; she\n danced away across the heath to a little lonely house. Here she knew the\n executioner dwelt; and she tapped with her fingers on the panes, and\n called,—‘Come out, come out! I cannot come in, for I must dance!’\n\n And the Executioner said,—‘You probably don't know who I am? I cut off the bad\n people's heads with my axe, and mark how my axe rings!’\n\n ‘Do not strike off my head,’ said Karen, ‘for if you do I cannot repent of my\n sin. But strike off my feet with the red shoes?’\n\n And then she confessed all her sin, and the Executioner cut off her feet with\n the red shoes; but the shoes danced away with the little feet over the fields\n and into the deep forest.\n\n And he cut her a pair of wooden feet, with crutches, and taught her a psalm,\n which the criminals always sing; and she kissed the hand that had held the\n axe, and went away across the heath.”\n    -Hans Christian Andersen, “The Red Shoes”, _Nye Eventyr. Første Bind.\n     Tredie Samling._. 1845.", "“她跳着舞，被迫跳着舞——在漆黑的夜里跳舞。鞋子\n 带着她越过荆棘和蔷薇；她把自己抓得流血；她\n 一路跳着穿过荒原，来到一座孤零零的小屋。她知道\n 刽子手就住在这里；她用手指敲着窗玻璃，\n 喊道——‘出来，出来！我进不去，因为我必须跳舞！’\n\n 刽子手说——‘你大概不知道我是谁吧？我用斧子砍下坏\n 人的头，你听听我的斧子响得多亮！’\n\n ‘别砍下我的头，’卡伦说，‘因为那样我就无法为我的\n 罪悔悟了。不如用这双红鞋砍下我的脚吧？’\n\n 于是她忏悔了自己所有的罪，刽子手用红鞋砍下了她的脚；\n 可那双鞋带着那对小脚跳着穿过田野，\n 一直跳进深林。\n\n 他给她削了一对木脚，配上一副拐杖，又教了她一首圣诗，\n 那是囚犯们常唱的；她亲吻了那只曾握斧的\n 手，然后穿过荒原离去。”\n    ——汉斯·克里斯蒂安·安徒生，《红鞋》，《新童话集。第一册。\n     第三辑》。1845年。"},
+    {"“I have seen the day, with my good biting falchion\n I would have made them skip: I am old now,\n And these same crosses spoil me.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _King Lear_, V, iii. 1608.", "“我见过那样的日子，凭我这把锋利好咬的弯刀\n 我能叫他们蹦跳逃窜：如今我老了，\n 而这些磨难又折损了我。”\n    ——威廉·莎士比亚，《李尔王》，V, iii。1608年。"},
+    {"“Even after forcing their way, with great effort and loss, through this double\n defence, [the Germans] still found themselves at a disadvantage; for their\n armour scarce enabled them to contend on equal terms with the uncouth but\n formidable weapons of their adversaries. The Bohemians were armed with long\n iron flails, which they swung with prodigious force. They seldom failed to\n hit, and when they did so, the flail crashed through brazen helmet, skull and\n all.”\n    -James A. Wylie, _The History of Protestantism_, vol. I, book 3, ch. 15\n     “John Huss and the Hussite Wars”. 1878.", "“即使[德国人]费尽力气、付出巨大损失，强行突破了这道双重\n 防线，他们仍发现自己处于劣势；因为他们的\n 甲胄几乎无法让他们与敌人那粗陋却\n 可怕的武器平等抗衡。波希米亚人装备着长长的\n 铁连枷，挥舞时力大无穷。他们很少失手，\n 一旦击中，连枷便砸穿黄铜头盔，连头骨\n 一并击碎。”\n    ——詹姆斯·A·怀利，《新教史》，第I卷，第3部，第15章\n     《约翰·胡斯与胡斯战争》。1878年。"},
+    {"“Revenge is a dish best served cold.”\n    -Klingon proverb, _Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan_. 1982", "“复仇这道菜，冷着吃最好。”\n    ——克林贡谚语，《星际迷航II：可汗怒吼》。1982年"},
+    {"“Therewith the gyant buckled him to fight,\n Inflamd with scornefull wrath and high disdaine,\n And lifting up his dreadful club on hight,\n All armed with ragged snubbes and knottie graine,\n Him thought at first encounter to have slaine.”\n    -Edmund Spenser, _The Faerie Queene_, Book I, “The Legend of the Knight of\n     the Red Crosse”, Canto VIII, stanza vii, l. 55-9. 1590.", "“于是那巨人束紧铠甲准备战斗，\n 满怀轻蔑的怒火与高傲的鄙夷，\n 高高举起他可怖的棍棒，\n 上面满是嶙峋的节瘤和扭曲的木纹，\n 他心想头一回合便能将对方杀死。”\n    ——埃德蒙·斯宾塞，《仙后》，第一卷，《红十字骑士传奇》，\n     第八章，第7节，第55-9行。1590年。"},
+    {" oni-ni-kanabō\n “oni with a spiked iron club”\n    -Japanese proverb indicating overwhelming power", " oni-ni-kanabō\n “手持狼牙铁棒的鬼”\n    ——日本谚语，意指压倒性的力量"},
+    {"“To know the perfect length of your ſhort ſtaffe, or half Pike, Forreſt bil,\n Partiſan or Gleue, or ſuch like weapons of vantage and perfect lengths, you\n ſhall ſtand vpright, holding the ſtaffe vpright cloſe by your body, with your\n left hãd, reaching with your right hand your ſtaffe as high as you can, and\n then allow to that length a ſpace to ſet both your hands, when you come to\n fight, wherein you may conueniently ſtrike, thruſt and ward, & that is the\n iuſt length according to you ſtature. And this note, that theſe lengths will\n commonly fall out to be eight or nine foot long, and will fit, although not\n iuſt, the ſtatures of all men, without any hindrance at all vnto them in their\n fight, becauſe in any weapon wherin the hands may be remoued, and at libertie,\n to make the weapon lõger or ſhorter in fight at his pleaſure, a foot of the\n ſtaffe behind the backmoſt hand doth no harme.”\n    -George Silver,_Paradoxes of Defence_.1599.", "“要想知道你短杖、半长矛、林用钩镰、\n 长矛或战戟之类占优势且长度合宜的武器的完美长度，你\n 应站直，左手将杖竖直紧贴身体，\n 用右手尽量高地够到杖，\n 然后在这一长度上留出双手握持的空间，当你临战\n 之时，便可方便地劈、刺和格挡，那便是\n 适合你身高的恰当长度。还要注意，这些长度\n 通常会是八到九英尺，纵然并非\n 恰好合身，也能适用于所有人的身高，而丝毫不妨碍他们在\n 作战中；因为在任何可以移动双手、随意\n 将武器于战斗中变长或变短的武器上，\n 最靠后那只手之后的一英尺杖身并无害处。”\n    ——乔治·西尔弗，《防御悖论》。1599年。"},
+    {"“Here it was that the ambassadors of the Samnites, finding him boiling turnips\n in the chimney corner, offered him a present of gold; but he sent them away\n with this saying; that he, who was content with such a supper, had no need of\n gold; and that he thought it more honourable to conquer those who possessed\n the gold, than to possess the gold itself.”\n    -Plutarch, “Marcus Cato”, _Lives_. 75 AD.\n    trans. John Dryden, 1683.", "“正是在这里，萨姆尼特人的使者发现他正在壁炉旁煮萝卜，\n 便献上黄金作为礼物；但他打发他们走，并说了这番话：\n 一个满足于这样一顿晚餐的人，不需要黄金；他认为\n 征服那些拥有黄金的人，比拥有黄金本身\n 更光荣。”\n    ——普鲁塔克，《马库斯·加图》，《名人传》。公元75年。\n     约翰·德莱顿 译，1683年。"},
+    {"“There will arise one named Feridoun, who shall inherit thy throne\n and reverse thy fortunes, and strike thee down with a cow-headed mace.”\n    -Firdausi, _Shahnameh_. ca. 1000 A.D.\n     trans. Helen Zimmern, 1883.", "“将会出现一个名叫费雷敦的人，他将继承你的王位，\n 扭转你的命运，用牛头锤将你击倒。”\n    ——菲尔多西，《列王纪》。约公元1000年。\n     海伦·齐默恩 译，1883年。"},
+    {"“And Diarmid oh, Diarmid he perished in the strife;\n His head it was spiked upon a halberd high;\n His colours they were trampled: he had no chance of life\n If the Lord God Himself stood by!—\n  Och, ochone!”\n    -James Clarence Mangan , _A Farewell to Patrick Sarsfield, Earl of Lucan_.\n     ca. 1840.", "“迪尔米德啊，迪尔米德，他死于争斗之中；\n 他的头颅被钉在高高的戟尖之上；\n 他的旗帜被践踏：他毫无生还之望\n 即便上帝亲自站在他身旁！——\n  呜呼，哀哉！”\n    ——詹姆斯·克拉伦斯·曼根，《告别帕特里克·萨斯菲尔德，卢肯伯爵》。\n     约1840年。"},
+    {"“The hammah that John Henry swung,\n It weighed over nine poun',\n He broke a rib in his left han' side,\n And his intrels fell on the groun',\n And his intrels fell on the groun'.”\n    -Onah L. Spencer, _John Henry_. Early 20th cent.", "“约翰·亨利抡起的那把大锤，\n 重逾九磅，\n 他左侧肋骨被震断，\n 他的肠子落在地上，\n 他的肠子落在地上。”\n    ——奥娜·L·斯宾塞，《约翰·亨利》。20世纪初。"},
+    {"“Lizzie Borden took an axe\n And gave her mother forty whacks.\n When she saw what she had done\n She gave her father forty-one.”\n    -A popular skipping-rope rhyme, after 1893.", "“莉齐·博登拿起斧头\n 砍了她母亲四十下。\n 当她看到自己所做之事\n 又砍了她父亲四十一下。”\n    ——一首流行的跳绳歌谣，1893年后。"},
+    {"“I do not aim with my hand; he who aims with his hand has forgotten the face of\n his father.\n I aim with my eye.\n I do not shoot with my hand; he who shoots with his hand has forgotten the face\n of his father.\n I shoot with my mind.\n I do not kill with my gun; he who kills with his gun has forgotten the face of\n his father.\n I kill with my heart.”\n    —Stephen King, “The Dark Tower III: The Waste Lands”", "“我不用手瞄准；用手瞄准的人已经忘记了他\n 父亲的脸。\n 我用眼睛瞄准。\n 我不用手射击；用手射击的人已经忘记了他\n 父亲的脸。\n 我用心灵射击。\n 我不用枪杀戮；用枪杀戮的人已经忘记了他\n 父亲的脸。\n 我用心杀戮。”\n    ——斯蒂芬·金，《黑暗塔III：荒原》"},
+    {"“So Joshua called together the priests and said, “Take up the Ark of the Lord's\n Covenant, and assign seven priests to walk in front of it, each carrying a\n ram's horn.\n\n When the people heard the sound of the rams’ horns, they shouted as loud as\n they could. Suddenly, the walls of Jericho collapsed, and the Israelites\n charged straight into the town and captured it. They completely destroyed\n everything in it with their swords—men and women, young and old, cattle,\n sheep, goats, and donkeys.”\n    -Joshua 6:6,20-21, New Living Translation", "“于是约书亚召集众祭司，说，“你们抬起耶和华的\n 约柜，派七位祭司走在约柜前，各人拿着\n 羊角。\n\n 百姓听见羊角的响声，就尽力\n 大声呼喊。耶利哥的城墙忽然倒塌，以色列人\n 直冲进城，将其攻取。他们用刀剑\n 将城中一切尽行毁灭——男女老少、牛\n 羊、山羊和驴。”\n    ——约书亚记 6:6,20-21，新普及译本"},
+    {"\"You're made of metal, but you have feelings, and you think about things,\n and that means you have a soul. And souls don't die.\"\n    -_The Iron Giant_. 1999", "\"你由金属制成，但你有感情，你会思考，\n 这意味着你有灵魂。而灵魂不会消亡。\"\n    ——《钢铁巨人》。1999年"},
+    {"“Suppose you found your brother in bed with your wife, and put a javelin\n through both of them, you would be justified, and they would atone for their\n sins, and be received into the kingdom of God.”\n    -Brigham Young, _Journal of Discourses_, 3:247. 1856.", "“假设你发现你的兄弟与你的妻子同床，用标枪\n 将他们两人刺穿，你也是正当的，而他们将为他们的\n 罪赎罪，并被接纳进入神的国度。”\n    ——杨百翰，《讲道日志》，3:247。1856年。"},
+    {"“...for the shield may be as important for victory, as the sword or spear.”\n    -Charles Darwin, “The Origin of Species”. 1859.", "“……因为盾牌对胜利的重要性，可能不亚于剑或矛。”\n    ——查尔斯·达尔文，《物种起源》。1859年。"},
+    {"“A weapon that comes down as still\n As snowflakes fall upon the sod;\n But executes a freeman's will,\n As lightning does the will of God.”\n    -John Pierpont, “The Ballot”. ca. 1850.", "“一件落下的武器，寂静无声\n 如雪花落在草地上；\n 却执行着自由人的意志，\n 如闪电执行上帝的意志。”\n    ——约翰·皮尔庞特，《选票》。约1850年。"},
+    {"“Well, I run to the rock and I hide my face\n The rock cried out, No hiding place\n There's no hiding place down here.”\n    -Negro spiritual. 19th cent.", "“哦，我跑向岩石，把脸藏起\n 岩石呼喊，无处可藏\n 这里无处可藏。”\n    ——黑人灵歌。19世纪。"},
+    {"“Nought can Deform the Human race\n Like to the Armours iron brace”\n    -William Blake, “Auguries of Innocence”, 99-100. 1803.", "“没有什么能比盔甲的\n 铁箍更扭曲人类”\n    ——威廉·布莱克，《天真的预言》，99-100。1803年。"},
+    {"“While we were at grips with this great army and their dreadful broadswords\n (maquahuitl [made of obsidian]), many of the most powerful among the enemy\n seem to have decided to capture a horse. They began with a furious attack, and\n laid hands on a good mare well trained both for sport and battle. Her rider,\n Pedro de Moron, was a fine horseman; and as he charged with three other\n horsemen into the enemy ranks—they had been instructed to charge together for\n mutual support—some of them seized his lance so he could not use it, and\n others slashed at him with their broadswords (maquahuitl), wounding him\n severely. Then they slashed at his mare, cutting her head at the neck so that\n it only hung by the skin. The mare fell dead, and if his mounted comrades had\n not come to Moron's rescue, he would probably have been killed also.”\n    -Bernal Díaz del Castillo, _The Conquest of New Spain_. 1623.\n     trans. J.M.Cohen, 1963.", "“当我们与这支大军和他们可怕的大砍刀\n （马夸维特尔[由黑曜石制成]）搏斗时，敌人中最强壮的许多人\n 似乎决意要擒获一匹马。他们发起猛攻，\n 抓住了一匹训练有素、既堪比赛又堪战斗的良马。它的骑手\n 佩德罗·德·莫龙是位出色的骑手；当他与另外三名\n 骑手冲入敌阵时——他们奉命一同冲锋以相互\n 支援——其中一些人抓住他的长矛使他无法使用，\n 另一些人则用大砍刀（马夸维特尔）向他猛砍，将他\n 重伤。接着他们又砍他的母马，在脖子处砍中马头，\n 只连着一点皮。母马倒地而亡，若不是他的骑马同伴\n 赶来救莫龙，他大概也会被杀。”\n    ——贝尔纳尔·迪亚斯·德尔·卡斯蒂略，《征服新西班牙》。1623年。\n     J.M.科恩 译，1963年。"},
+    {"“Robyn bent a full goode bowe,\n An arrowe he drowe at wyll;\n He hit so the proud sherife\n Upon the grounde he lay full still.”\n    -_A Gest of Robyn Hode_ Sixth Fytte, l. 120-123. ca. 1450.", "“罗宾拉满一张上好的弓，\n 随心所欲地射出一箭；\n 他射中了那傲慢的郡守\n 他便直挺挺地倒在地上。”\n    ——《罗宾汉轶事》第六篇，第120-123行。约1450年。"},
+    {"“[My plan] does not propose to fill your lobby with squabbling colony agents,\n who will require the interposition of your mace at every instant to keep the\n peace among them.”\n    -Edmund Burke, “On Conciliation with America”, speech in Parliament. 1775.", "“[我的计划]并不打算用争吵不休的殖民地代理人塞满你们的议事厅，\n 他们随时都需要你们权杖的干预来维持\n 他们之间的和平。”\n    ——埃德蒙·伯克，《关于与美洲和解》，议会演讲。1775年。"},
+    {"“Manuals have their uses... but they are not to be confused with living.”\n    -Robert Fulghum", "“手册自有其用处……但不能把它们与生活混为一谈。”\n    ——罗伯特·富尔格姆"},
+    {"“Little did then his pomp of plumes bestead\n The Azteca, or glittering pride of gold.\n Against the tempered sword; little his casque,\n Cay with its feathery coronal, or drest\n In graven terrors, when the Briton's hand\n Drove in through helm and head the spiked mace;\n Or swung its iron weights with shattering sway.\n Which, where they fell, destroyed.”\n    -Robert Southey, _Madoc_. 1805.", "“那时他的华丽羽饰对阿兹特克人\n 毫无用处，或闪耀的金饰之傲，\n 面对淬炼的刀剑时亦然；他的头盔，\n 饰有羽毛冠冕的凯，或装点着\n 雕琢可怖之纹饰，皆无济于事，当不列颠人之手\n 连盔带头将带钉的锤子砸入；\n 或挥动其铁锤以摧枯拉朽之势，\n 所落之处，尽皆毁灭。”\n    ——罗伯特·骚塞，《马多克》。1805年。"},
+    {"“But soon as early Dawn appeared, the rosy-fingered, then gathered the folk\n about the pyre of glorious Hector.”\n    -Homer, _The Iliad_, XXIV, 776.\n     trans. A. T. Murray, 1857.", "“但当那玫瑰色手指的黎明刚一出现，人们便聚集在\n 光荣的赫克托耳的火葬堆旁。”\n    ——荷马，《伊利亚特》，XXIV，776。\n     A. T. 默里 译，1857年。"},
+    {"“Down at that far end of the hallway, the mirror hovered, shadowing us. We\n discovered (very late at night such a discovery is inevitable) that there is\n something monstrous about mirrors. That was when Bioy remembered a saying by\n one of the heresiarchs of Uqbar: ‘Mirrors and copulation are abominable,\n for they multiply the number of mankind.’”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, _Tlön, Uqbar, Orbis Tertius_. 1947\n      trans. Andrew Hurley, 1998.", "“在走廊遥远的尽头，那面镜子悬在那里，将我们笼罩在阴影中。我们\n 发现（在深夜里，这样的发现是不可避免的）镜子有\n 某种怪异之处。就在这时，比奥伊想起了乌克巴尔某个\n 异端首领的一句话：‘镜子和交媾是令人憎恶的，\n 因为它们使人间的数目倍增。’”\n    ——豪尔赫·路易斯·博尔赫斯，《特隆、乌克巴尔、奥比斯·特蒂乌斯》。1947年\n      安德鲁·赫尔利 译，1998年。"},
+    {"“Then gave I her, — so tutor'd by my art, —\n A sleeping potion; which so took effect\n As I intended, for it wrought on her\n The form of death: meantime I writ to Romeo\n That he should hither come as this dire night,\n To help to take her from her borrow'd grave,\n Being the time the potion's force should cease.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _Romeo and Juliet_", "“于是我给她，——经我技艺调教，——\n 一瓶安眠药剂；它果然如我所愿\n 起了作用，在她身上显出\n 死亡的样子：同时我写信给罗密欧\n 要他在这可怕之夜前来此处，\n 帮她从借来的坟墓中脱身，\n 那正是药力该消退之时。”\n    ——威廉·莎士比亚，《罗密欧与朱丽叶》"},
+    {"“But if when you say ‘whiskey’ you mean the oil of conversation,\n the philosophic wine, the ale that is consumed when good fellows\n get together, that puts a song in their hearts and laughter on\n their lips, and the warm glow of contentment in their eyes...\n then certainly I am for it.”\n    -Noah S. Sweat, Jr. “If By Whiskey”. 1952", "“但如果你说的‘威士忌’是指谈话的润滑油，\n 那哲理之酒，是好伙伴们聚在一起时\n 畅饮的麦芽酒，它让歌声充满他们的心，笑容挂在\n 他们唇边，满足的暖光映在他们眼中……\n 那我当然赞成。”\n    ——小诺亚·S·斯威特，《如果以威士忌而言》。1952年"},
+    {"“Let go your earthly tether. Enter the void. Empty, and become wind.”\n    -_The Legend of Korra_. 2014", "“放开你尘世的羁绊。进入虚空。清空自我，化为风。”\n    ——《降世神通：科拉传奇》。2014年"},
+    {"“I shall never forget that dawn, and the strange horror of seeing that my hands\n had become as clouded glass, and watching them grow clearer and thinner as the\n day went by, until at last I could see the sickly disorder of my room through\n them, though I closed my transparent eyelids. My limbs became glassy, the bones\n and arteries faded, vanished, and the little white nerves went last. I gritted\n my teeth and stayed there to the end. At last only the dead tips of the\n fingernails remained, pallid and white, and the brown stain of some acid, upon\n my fingers.”\n    -H.G. Wells, _The Invisible Man_, Chapter 20. 1897", "“我永远不会忘记那个黎明，以及看到自己双手\n 变得如云雾般的玻璃那般，心中涌起的奇异恐惧，看着它们随着\n 白日推移变得越来越清晰、越来越稀薄，直到最后我能透过\n 它们看见我房间里那令人作呕的凌乱，尽管我闭上了透明的眼睑。我的四肢变得如玻璃般，骨头\n 和血管逐渐淡去、消失，最后消失的是那些白色的小神经。我咬紧\n 牙关，一直坚持到最后。最后只剩下指甲\n 死去的尖端，苍白而惨白，以及我手指上\n 某种酸留下的褐色污渍。”\n    ——H.G.威尔斯，《隐形人》，第20章。1897年"},
+    {"“Before her prayer was ended, torpor seized\n on all her body, and a thin bark closed\n around her gentle bosom, and her hair\n became as moving leaves; her arms were changed\n to waving branches, and her active feet\n as clinging roots were fastened to the ground—\n her face was hidden with encircling leaves.”\n    -Ovid, _Metamorphoses_, Book I:253-259, trans. Brookes More. 1922", "“她的祈祷尚未结束，麻木便攫住了\n 她整个身体，一层薄薄的树皮合拢\n 在她温柔的胸前，她的头发\n 化为飘动的树叶；她的双臂变成了\n 摇曳的枝条，她敏捷的双脚\n 如缠绕的树根般扎入大地——\n 她的脸庞被环绕的树叶遮住。”\n    ——奥维德，《变形记》，第一卷:253-259，布鲁克斯·莫尔 译。1922年"},
+    {"“If when you say ‘whiskey’ you mean the devil's brew, the poison\n scourge, the bloody monster, that defiles innocence, dethrones\n reason, destroys the home, creates misery and poverty, yea,\n literally takes the bread from the mouths of little children...\n then certainly I am against it.”\n    -Noah S. Sweat, Jr. “If By Whiskey”. 1952", "“如果你说‘威士忌’是指魔鬼的酿物、那毒害\n 的祸根、那血腥的怪物，它玷污清白、颠覆\n 理智、毁坏家庭、制造苦难与贫穷，是的，\n 甚至真真切切地从幼童口中夺走面包……\n 那我当然反对它。”\n    -小诺亚·S·斯韦特，《若论威士忌》。1952年"},
+    {"“Aha! will fate play tricks upon me? Will the elements lay plots against me?\n Shall fire, air, and water make a combined attack against me? Well, they\n shall know what a determined man can do. I will not yield. I will not stir\n a single foot backwards, and it will be seen whether man or nature is to\n have the upper hand!”\n    -Jules Verne, _Journey into the Interior of the Earth_. 1864\n     trans. F.A. Malleson, 1877", "“啊哈！命运要戏弄我吗？元素要密谋对付我吗？\n 火、气、水要联合起来攻击我吗？好，它们\n 会知道一个坚定的人能做什么。我绝不屈服。我绝不\n 后退一步，且看究竟是人还是自然\n 占上风！”\n    -儒勒·凡尔纳，_地心游记_。1864年\n     译 F.A. 马勒森，1877年"},
+    {"“Then Robin he unbuckled his belt,\n And laid down his bow so long;\n He took up a staff of another oak graff,\n That was both stiff and strong.\n ‘But let me measure,’ said jolly Robin,\n ‘Before we begin our fray;\n For I'll not have mine to be longer than thine,\n For that will be counted foul play.’\n ‘I pass not for length,’ bold Arthur replied,\n ‘My staff is of oak so free;\n Eight foot and a half it will knock down a calf,\n And I hope it will knock down thee.’\n Then Robin could no longer forbear,\n He gave him such a knock,\n Quickly and soon the blood came down,\n Before it was ten o'clock.\n About and about and about they went,\n Like two wild boars in a chase,\n Striving to aim each other to maim,\n Leg, arm, or any other place.\n And knock for knock they hastily dealt,\n Which held for two hours and more;\n That all the wood rang at every bang,\n They plied their work so sore.”\n    -Anonymous, “Robin Hood and the Tanner”.", "“于是罗宾解下了他的腰带，\n 放下了他的长弓；\n 他拿起一根别的橡木杖，\n 又硬又结实。\n ‘不过让我先量一量，’快活的罗宾说，\n ‘趁我们还没开始打斗；\n 我可不许我的比你的长，\n 那样会被算作犯规。’\n ‘长度我不计较，’大胆的亚瑟答道，\n ‘我的杖是上好的橡木；\n 八英尺半，能打翻一头小牛，\n 我希望它也能打翻你。’\n 于是罗宾再也按捺不住，\n 他给了他那么一下，\n 又急又快，鲜血就流了下来，\n 还没到十点钟。\n 他们转来转去，转来转去，\n 像追逐中的两头野猪，\n 一心要击中对方致残，\n 腿、臂，或任何别的地方。\n 他们匆忙地对打，一下还一下，\n 持续了两个多小时；\n 每一下都震得整片树林作响，\n 他们打得如此猛烈。”\n    -佚名，《罗宾汉与制革匠》。"},
+    {"“He hath loosed the fateful lightning of His terrible swift sword...”\n    -Julia Ward Howe, “The Battle Hymn of the Republic”. 1861.", "“他已释放出他那可怖迅疾之剑的致命闪电……”\n    -朱莉娅·沃德·豪，《共和国战歌》。1861年"},
+    {"“Who was the first that forged the deadly blade?\n Of rugged steel his savage soul was made...”\n    -Albius Tibullus, _Elegies_ I, xi. ca. 25 B.C.\n     trans. James Grainger, 1822.", "“是谁最先锻造出那致命的刀刃？\n 他野性的灵魂由粗糙的钢铁铸成……”\n    -阿尔比乌斯·提布鲁斯，_哀歌集_ I, xi。约公元前25年\n     译 詹姆斯·格兰杰，1822年"},
+    {"“A ring of gold and a milk-white dove\n Are goodly gifts for thee,\n And a hempen rope for your own love\n To hang upon a tree.”\n    -Oscar Wilde, “Chanson”. 1881.", "“一枚金戒指和一只乳白的鸽子\n 是给你的美好礼物，\n 还有一条麻绳，给你心上人\n 吊在树上用。”\n    -奥斯卡·王尔德，《香颂》。1881年"},
+    {"“A mermaid who fancied to sing,\n Roused lust in the Octopus King.\n Her squiggly suitor,\n Tried hard to woo her,\n But she couldn't wear all his rings.”", "“有位美人鱼爱歌唱，\n 惹得章鱼王心痒痒。\n 她那蠕曲的追求者，\n 拼命想讨她欢心，\n 可他的戒指她戴不完。”"},
+    {"“Love is a burning thing\n And it makes a fiery ring”\n    -June Carter and Merle Kilgore, “Ring of Fire”. 1963.", "“爱是燃烧之物\n 它造就一圈烈焰之环”\n    -琼·卡特与默尔·基尔戈，《火环》。1963年"},
+    {"“What surprised him the most, however, was the logic of his wings. They seemed\n so natural on that completely human organism that he couldn't understand why\n other men didn't have them too.”\n    -Gabriel Garcia Marquez, _A Very Old Man with Enormous Wings_. 1955.\n     trans. Gregory Rabassa. 1972", "“然而最令他惊讶的，是他那对翅膀的逻辑。它们长在\n 那样一副完全属于人的躯体上显得如此自然，以至于他不明白为什么\n 别的男人没有翅膀。”\n    -加夫列尔·加西亚·马尔克斯，_巨翅老人_。1955年\n     译 格雷戈里·拉巴萨。1972年"},
+    {"“Some say the world will end in fire;\n Some say in ice.\n From what I've tasted of desire\n I hold with those who favor fire.\n But if it had to perish twice,\n I think I know enough of hate\n To say that for destruction ice\n Is also great\n And would suffice.”\n    -Robert Frost, “Fire and Ice”. 1920.", "“有人说世界将终结于火；\n 有人说终结于冰。\n 尝过我心中欲望的滋味，\n 我赞同那些偏爱火的人。\n 但若它非得毁灭两次，\n 我想我对恨了解得够多，\n 足以说冰之于毁灭\n 也同样伟大\n 且已足够。”\n    -罗伯特·弗罗斯特，《火与冰》。1920年"},
+    {"“Hobbes: Did it work?\n Calvin: I think so.\n         I feel smarter already.”\n    -Bill Watterson, _Calvin and Hobbes_. November 19, 1993.", "“霍布斯：有用吗？\n 卡尔文：我想是的。\n         我已经觉得自己更聪明了。”\n    -比尔·沃特森，_卡尔文与霍布斯_。1993年11月19日"},
+    {" Buttercup:    “And to think, all that time it was your cup that was poisoned.”\n Man in Black: “They were both poisoned. I spent the last few years building\n                up an immunity to iocane powder.”\n    -_The Princess Bride_. 1987", " 巴特卡普：    “真想不到，一直以来被下毒的竟是你的杯子。”\n 黑衣人： “两杯都被下毒了。我花了过去几年时间培养\n                对艾欧卡因粉末的免疫力。”\n    -_公主新娘_。1987年"},
+    {"“And these three men, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, fell down bound into the\n midst of the burning fiery furnace.\n\n Then Nebuchadnezzar the king was astonished, and rose up in haste, and spake,\n and said unto his counsellors, Did not we cast three men bound into the midst\n of the fire? They answered and said unto the king, True, O king.\n\n He answered and said, Lo, I see four men loose, walking in the midst of the\n fire, and they have no hurt; and the form of the fourth is like the Son of God.\n\n Then Nebuchadnezzar came near to the mouth of the burning fiery furnace, and\n spake, and said, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, ye servants of the most high\n God, come forth, and come hither. Then Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, came\n forth of the midst of the fire.\n\n And the princes, governors, and captains, and the king’s counsellors, being\n gathered together, saw these men, upon whose bodies the fire had no power, nor\n was an hair of their head singed, neither were their coats changed, nor the\n smell of fire had passed on them.”\n    -KJV Bible, Daniel 3:23-27.", "“这三个人，沙得拉、米煞、亚伯尼歌，都被捆绑着落在\n 烈火的窑中。\n\n 那时尼布甲尼撒王惊奇，急忙起来，说：\n 我们捆起来扔在火里的不是三个人吗？\n 他们回答王说：王啊，是。\n\n 王回答说：看哪，我见有四个人捆绑松开，在火中行走，\n 也没有受伤；那第四个人的相貌好像神子。\n\n 于是尼布甲尼撒就近烈火窑门，\n 说：至高神的仆人沙得拉、米煞、亚伯尼歌，\n 出来，到这里来。沙得拉、米煞、亚伯尼歌，\n 就从火中出来了。\n\n 那些总督、钦差、巡抚和王的谋士，一同聚集，\n 看这三个人，火无力伤他们的身体，\n 他们的头发也没有烧焦，衣裳也没有变色，\n 也没有火燎的气味。”\n    -钦定版圣经《但以理书》3:23-27."},
+    {"“Here is my secret. It is very simple: It is only with the heart that one can\n see rightly; what is essential is invisible to the eye.”\n    -Antoine de Saint Exupéry, _The Little Prince_. 1943.", "“这就是我的秘密。它很简单：只有用心才能\n 看得正确；本质的东西用眼睛是看不见的。”\n    -安托万·德·圣埃克苏佩里，_小王子_。1943年"},
+    {"“Life is too short to occupy oneself with the slaying of the slain more than\n once.”\n    -Thomas Huxley", "“人生太短暂，不值得为杀死已被杀死者的事\n 费心超过一次。”\n    -托马斯·赫胥黎"},
+    {"“Then as part of the game\n She completely forgot where she'd hidden herself\n And she spent the rest of her time\n Trying to find the parts”\n    -Howard Jones, “Hide and Seek”. 1984.", "“于是作为游戏的一部分\n 她完全忘了自己藏在哪里\n 而她把余下的时间\n 都用来寻找那些部件”\n    -霍华德·琼斯，《捉迷藏》。1984年"},
+    {"“That which does not kill us makes us stronger.”\n    -Friedrich Nietzsche", "“凡杀不死我们的，必使我们更强大。”\n    -弗里德里希·尼采"},
+    {"“I am, indeed, a king, because I know how to rule myself.”\n    -Pietro Aretino, letter to Mr. Agostino Ricchi, May 10th, 1537. from\n      _The Works of Aretino, Translated into English from the original Italian_,\n      trans. Samuel Putnam. 1926", "“我确实是位国王，因为我知道如何统治我自己。”\n    -彼得罗·阿雷蒂诺，致阿戈斯蒂诺·里基先生的书信，1537年5月10日。摘自\n      _阿雷蒂诺作品集：由意大利文原文译成英文_，\n      译 塞缪尔·帕特南。1926年"},
+    {"“CLEOPATRA: Give me my robe, put on my crown; I have\n Immortal longings in me”\n    -William Shakespeare, _Anthony & Cleopatra_, V, ii. ca. 1605.", "“克莉奥佩特拉：给我穿上长袍，戴上王冠；我心中有\n 不死的渴望”\n    -威廉·莎士比亚，_安东尼与克莉奥佩特拉_，V, ii。约1605年"},
+    {"“The museum-cabinet and huge library arrogated to themselves the entire lower\n floor — there were the controversial and incompatible books that are somehow\n the history of the nineteenth century; there were scimitars from Nishapur, in\n whose frozen crescents the wind and violence of battle seemed to be living\n on.”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, _The Form of the Sword_. 1953.\n     trans. Andrew Hurley.", "“博物馆陈列柜和巨大的藏书室占据了整个底\n 层——那里有那些彼此矛盾、互不相容的书籍，它们不知怎的\n 就是十九世纪的历史；还有来自尼沙普尔的弯刀，在\n 它们冰冷的月牙刃上，战斗的风与暴力仿佛仍存活着\n 一般。”\n    -豪尔赫·路易斯·博尔赫斯，_剑的形状_。1953年\n     译 安德鲁·赫尔利。"},
+    {"“To drift with every passion till my soul\n Is a stringed lute on which all winds can play,\n Is it for this that I have given away\n Mine ancient wisdom, and austere control?\n Methinks my life is a twice-written scroll\n Scrawled over on some boyish holiday.”\n    -Oscar Wilde, _Helas_. 1881.", "“任由每一种激情驱使，直到我的灵魂\n 成为一把弦琴，任所有风儿弹奏，\n 难道就是为了这个，我舍弃了\n 古老的智慧，和严峻的克制？\n 我想我的生命是一卷重复书写的卷轴\n 在某个稚气的假日里被胡乱涂改。”\n    -奥斯卡·王尔德，_赫拉斯_。1881年。"},
+    {"He had stopped trying to think. He just repeated his litany over and over:\n“I am an animal, you see that. I don’t have the words, they didn’t teach\nme the words. I don’t know how to think, the bastards didn’t let me learn\nhow to think. But if you really are... all-powerful... all-knowing... then you\nfigure it out! Look into my heart. I know that everything you need is in there.\nIt has to be. I never sold my soul to anyone! It’s mine, it’s human! You\ntake from me what it is I want... it just can’t be that I would want something\nbad! Damn it all, I can’t think of anything, except those words of his...\n‘HAPPINESS FOR EVERYBODY, FREE, AND NO ONE WILL GO AWAY UNSATISFIED!’”\n    -Arkady and Boris Strugatsky, “Roadside Picnic”. 1972.\n    trans. Antonina W. Bouis, 1977.", "他已经不再尝试思考。只是反复念叨着他的连祷文：\n“我是个动物，你看得出来。我没有那些词语，他们没教\n我词语。我不知道怎么思考，那些混蛋不让我学会\n怎么思考。但如果你真的是... 全能的... 全知的... 那么你\n自己弄明白！看看我的心。我知道你需要的一切都在那里。\n一定是这样。我从未把灵魂卖给任何人！它是我的，它是人类的！你\n从我这拿走我想要的东西... 不可能我会想要什么\n坏东西！该死的，我什么也想不出来，除了他那些话...\n‘人人幸福，免费，无人会不满而归！’”\n    -阿尔卡季和鲍里斯·斯特鲁加茨基，《路边野餐》。1972年。\n    安东妮娜·W·布伊丝 译，1977年。"},
+    {"“But revenge is hollow. I'd prefer amnesia.”\n    -Tera Lynn Childs", "“但复仇是空虚的。我宁愿失忆。”\n    -泰拉·琳·查尔兹"},
+    {"“It used to be thought that the events that changed the world were things like\n big bombs, maniac politicians, huge earthquakes, or vast population movements,\n but it has now been realized that this is a very old-fashioned view held by\n people totally out of touch with modern thought. The things that really change\n the world, according to Chaos theory, are the tiny things. A butterfly flaps\n its wings in the Amazonian jungle, and subsequently a storm ravages half of\n Europe...”\n    -Neil Gaiman and Terry Pratchett, _Good Omens: The Nice And Accurate\n     Prophecies of Agnes Nutter, Witch_. 1990.", "“过去人们认为，改变世界的事件是诸如\n 大炸弹、疯狂政客、大地震，或大规模人口迁徙，\n 但现在人们认识到，这是一种非常过时的观点，持有者\n 完全脱离现代思想。根据混沌理论，真正改变\n 世界的是微小的事物。一只蝴蝶在\n 亚马逊丛林中扇动翅膀，随后一场风暴肆虐了半个\n 欧洲……”\n    -尼尔·盖曼和特里·普拉切特，_好兆头：女巫阿格妮丝·纳特\n     的精准预言_。1990年。"},
+    {"“I will revenge my injuries: if I cannot inspire love, I will cause fear.”\n    -Mary Shelley, _Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus_,\n     Vol. II, Chapter 9. 1818 (1st Ed.)", "“我要报复我的伤害：如果我不能激发爱，我就制造恐惧。”\n    -玛丽·雪莱，_弗兰肯斯坦，或现代普罗米修斯_，\n     第二卷，第九章。1818年（初版）"},
+    {"“Calvin: I used to hate writing assignments, but now I enjoy them. I realized\n         that the purpose of writing is to inflate weak ideas, obscure poor\n         reasoning, and inhibit clarity. With a little practice, writing can be\n         an intimidating and impenetrable fog!”\n    -Bill Watterson, _Calvin and Hobbes_. February 11th, 1993.", "“卡尔文：我过去讨厌写作作业，但现在我喜欢它们。我意识到\n         写作的目的是夸大薄弱的想法，模糊糟糕的\n         推理，并阻碍清晰。稍加练习，写作就能成为\n         一片令人生畏且难以穿透的迷雾！”\n    -比尔·沃特森，_卡尔文与霍布斯_。1993年2月11日。"},
+    {"“This, what is it in itself, and by itself, according to its proper\n constitution? What is the substance of it? What is the matter, or\n proper use? What is the form or efficient cause? What is it for in\n this world, and how long will it abide? Thus must thou examine all\n things, that present themselves unto thee.”\n    -Marcus Aurelius, _Marcus Aurelius Antoninus - His Meditations concerning\n     himselfe_, Book VIII, X. trans. Meric Casaubon, 1634.", "“这东西本身是什么，按其固有的\n 构造？它的实质是什么？它的质料，或\n 正当用途是什么？它的形式或动力因是什么？它在这\n 世界中是为了什么，又能持续多久？因此你必须审视所有\n 呈现于你面前的事物。”\n    -马可·奥勒留，_马可·奥勒留·安东尼努斯——关于\n     他自己的沉思录_，第八卷，第十节。梅里克·卡索邦 译，1634年。"},
+    {"“‘What a useful thing a pocket-map is!’ I remarked.\n ‘That’s another thing we’ve learned from your Nation,’ said Mein Herr,\n    ‘map-making. But we’ve carried it much further than you. What do you\n    consider the largest map that would be really useful?’\n ‘About six inches to the mile.’\n ‘Only six inches!’ exclaimed Mein Herr. ‘We very soon got to six yards to the\n     mile. Then we tried a hundred yards to the mile. And then came the grandest\n     idea of all! We actually made a map of the country, on the scale of a mile\n     to the mile!’\n ‘Have you used it much?’ I enquired.\n ‘It has never been spread out, yet,’ said Mein Herr: ‘the farmers objected:\n     they said it would cover the whole country, and shut out the sunlight! So\n     we now use the country itself, as its own map, and I assure you it does\n     nearly as well.’”\n    -Lewis Carroll, _Sylvie and Bruno Concluded_, Chapter XI. 1895", "“‘一张口袋地图是多么有用的东西啊！’我说道。\n ‘那是我们从你们国家学到的另一件事，’迈恩赫尔说，\n    ‘地图绘制。但我们比你们走得远得多。你们认为\n    真正有用的最大地图是多大？’\n ‘大约六英寸比一英里。’\n ‘只有六英寸！’迈恩赫尔叫道。‘我们很快就到了六码比一\n     英里。然后我们尝试了一百码比一英里。接着出现了最宏大的\n     想法！我们实际上制作了一张这个国家的地图，比例是一英里\n     比一英里！’\n ‘你们常用它吗？’我问道。\n ‘它还没有被展开过，’迈恩赫尔说：‘农民们反对：\n     他们说它会覆盖整个国家，挡住阳光！所以\n     我们现在用国家本身作为它自己的地图，我向你保证它\n     差不多一样好。’”\n    -刘易斯·卡罗尔，_西尔维与布鲁诺续篇_，第十一章。1895年"},
+    {"“The nature of the place where the Library was founded—a sanatorium rumored to\n harbor the pitiably deformed—had proven a lure to people with no desire at all\n for ‘regular human communication.’ I remember the case of a man who was normal\n in every respect, in words, in reasoning, in the practice of his profession,\n but strayed from this ‘normalcy’ in only one way: his inexplicable need to fill\n thousands of sheets of foolscap paper with seemingly meaningless words,\n phonemes close to wailing sounds, cries of fury and pain in relentless\n successions, fragments of sentences and pleas addressed to God-knows-whom.”\n    -Giorgio de Maria, _The Twenty Days of Turin_, 1975.\n     trans. Ramon Glazov, 2016", "“图书馆创建之地的性质——一个据传收容\n 可怜畸形者的疗养院——对完全不想‘正常人际交流’\n 的人证明是一种诱惑。我记得有一个人，他在各方面\n 都很正常，在言语上，在推理上，在职业实践中，\n 但只在一点上偏离了这种‘正常’：他莫名地需要填满\n 数千张大页纸，上面写着看似无意义的词语，\n 接近哀嚎的音素，狂怒与痛苦的呼喊，接连\n 不断，句子片段和向天知道是谁的恳求。”\n    -乔治·德·玛丽亚，_都灵的二十天_，1975年。\n     拉蒙·格拉佐夫 译，2016年"},
+    {"“And in the naked light I saw\n   Ten thousand people, maybe more.\n People talking without speaking,\n   People hearing without listening;\n People writing songs\n   That voices never shared:\n No one dared\n   Disturb the sound\n   Of silence.”\n    -Simon & Garfunkel, _The Sound of Silence_. 1964.", "“在赤裸的灯光下我看见\n   一万个人，也许更多。\n 人们交谈却无言，\n   人们倾听却未听；\n 人们写着歌\n   歌声从未被分享：\n 无人敢\n   打破那声音\n   那寂静之声。”\n    -西蒙与加芬克尔，_寂静之声_。1964年。"},
+    {"“When thou attended gloriously from heaven, Shalt in the sky appear, and from\n thee send Thy summoning archangels to proclaim Thy dread tribunal.”\n    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book III, 1667.", "“当你荣耀地从天界降临，要在天空中显现，并从\n 你那里派遣召唤的大天使，宣告你可怖的审判。”\n    -约翰·弥尔顿，_失乐园_，第三卷，1667年。"},
+    {"“Eiris sazun idisi\n sazun hera duoder\n suma hapt heptidun\n suma heri lezidun\n suma clubodun\n umbi cuoniouuidi\n insprinc haptbandun\n inuar uigandun\n\n [Once sat women,\n they sat here then there.\n some fastened bonds,\n some impeded an army,\n some unraveled fetters:\n Escape the bonds,\n flee the enemy!]”\n    -First Merseburg incantation, trans. Patricia Giangrosso, 2001", "“Eiris sazun idisi\n sazun hera duoder\n suma hapt heptidun\n suma heri lezidun\n suma clubodun\n umbi cuoniouuidi\n insprinc haptbandun\n inuar uigandun\n\n [从前坐着女人们，\n 她们坐在这里又坐那里。\n 一些人缚住枷锁，\n 一些人阻挡军队，\n 一些人解开镣铐：\n 逃离枷锁，\n 逃离敌人！]”\n    -第一梅泽堡咒语，帕特丽夏·姜格罗索 译，2001年"},
+    {"“It was instinct. Illogical as lightning striking and not hurting. Each day the\n grain must be cut. It had to be cut. Why? Well, it just did, that was all. He\n laughed at the scythe in his big hands. Then, whistling, he took it out to the\n ripe and waiting field and did the work. He thought himself a little mad.\n Hell, it was an ordinary-enough wheat field, really, wasn't it?”\n    -Ray Bradbury, _The Scythe_. 1943.", "“那是本能。不合逻辑，就像闪电击中却不伤人。每一天\n 麦子必须被割。必须割。为什么？嗯，就是这样，仅此而已。他\n 对着大手中的镰刀笑了。然后，吹着口哨，他把它拿到\n 成熟而等待的田里，干起了活。他觉得自己有点疯。\n 见鬼，那不过是一块再普通不过的麦田，真的，不是吗？”\n    -雷·布拉德伯里，_镰刀_。1943年。"},
+    {"“‘Hurrah! my boys,’ says I, my shillelagh I let fly.\n Some Galway boys were by, they saw I was a hobbling;\n Then with a loud ‘hurrah!’ they joined me in the fray.\n Faugh-a-ballagh! Clear the way! for the rocky road to Dublin!”\n\n    -D.K. Gavan, _Rocky Road to Dublin_. 19th Century.", "“‘乌拉！我的孩子们，’我说，我的橡木棍飞了出去。\n 一些戈尔韦男孩在旁边，他们看见我步履蹒跚；\n 然后随着一声响亮的‘乌拉！’他们加入了战斗。\n Faugh-a-ballagh！让开！为了通往都柏林的崎岖之路！”\n\n    -D.K.加文，_通往都柏林的崎岖之路_。19世纪。"},
+    {"“You are the bows from which your children as living arrows are sent forth.”\n    -Khalil Ghibran, _The Prophet_, “On Children”. 1923.", "“你们是弓，你们的孩子是从弦上射出的生命之箭。”\n    -纪伯伦，_先知_，“论孩子”。1923年。"},
+    {"“Who was the first that forged the deadly blade?\n Of rugged steel his savage soul was made”\n    -Albius Tibullus, _Elegies_ I, xi. ca. 25 B.C.\n     trans. James Grainger, 1822.", "“是谁最先锻造出致命的刀刃？\n 他那野蛮的灵魂由粗钢铸成”\n    -阿尔比乌斯·提布鲁斯，_哀歌集_ I, xi. 约公元前25年。\n     詹姆斯·格兰杰 译，1822年。"},
+    {"“And David put his hand in his bag, and took thence a stone, and slang it, and\n smote the Philistine in his forehead, that the stone sunk into his forehead;\n and he fell upon his face to the earth.”\n    -KJV Bible, 1 Samuel 17:49.", "“大卫把手伸进囊中，从那里掏出一块石头，甩了出去，\n 击中非利士人的额头，石头陷入他的额头；\n 他面朝地倒下了。”\n    -钦定版圣经，撒母耳记上 17:49。"},
+    {"“Thrice whirling round his head\n The whistling thong, Mezentius took his aim.\n Clean through his temples hissed the molten lead,\n And prostrate in the dust, the gallant youth lay dead.”\n    -Virgil, Aeneid 9\n     trans. E. Fairfax Taylor", "“三次在头顶旋转\n 呼啸的皮索，梅曾提乌斯瞄准了目标。\n 熔铅嘶嘶作响，径直穿透他的太阳穴，\n 那勇敢的青年趴倒在尘土中，死了。”\n    -维吉尔，埃涅阿斯纪 9\n     E·费尔法克斯·泰勒 译"},
+    {"“Or burst the vanish’d Hero’s lofty mound;\n Far on the solitary shore he sleeps:\n He fell, and falling nations mourn’d around;\n But now not one of saddening thousands weeps,\n Nor warlike worshipper his vigil keeps\n Where demi-gods appear’d, as records tell.\n Remove yon skull from out the scatter’d heaps:\n Is that a temple where a God may dwell?\n Why ev’n the worm at last disdains her shatter’d cell!\n\n Look on its broken arch, its ruin’d wall,\n Its chambers desolate, and portals foul:\n Yes, this was once Ambition’s airy hall,\n The dome of Thought, the palace of the Soul:\n Behold through each lack-lustre, eyeless hole,\n The gay recess of Wisdom and of Wit\n And Passion’s host, that never brook’d control:\n Can all saint, sage, or sophist ever writ,\n People this lonely tower, this tenement refit?”\n    -Lord Byron, _Childe Harold's Pilgrimage_, Canto the Second, Stanzas V-VI", "“或者，那消失的英雄的高耸坟冢是否已崩塌；\n 他长眠在遥远的孤寂海岸：\n 他倒下了，周围倒下的民族为他哀悼；\n 但如今，那成千上万悲伤的人中无一哭泣，\n 也没有尚武的崇拜者为他守夜\n 在据记载曾有半神显现的地方。\n 从那散乱的尸骨堆中移开那骷髅：\n 那是一座可供神灵居住的庙宇吗？\n 为何连虫子最终也鄙视她那破碎的牢房！\n\n 看那断裂的拱门，那残破的墙壁，\n 那荒凉的房间，和污秽的门廊：\n 是的，这里曾是野心飘渺的殿堂，\n 思想的穹顶，灵魂的宫殿：\n 透过每一个无光、无眼的孔洞，观看\n 智慧和才智的欢乐幽所\n 以及从不忍受控制的激情之众：\n 所有圣徒、贤哲或智者所写的一切，\n 能让人住进这孤独的塔楼，重新装修这住所吗？”\n    -拜伦勋爵，_恰尔德·哈罗德游记_，第二篇，第五至六节"},
+    {"“The halberd is inferior to the spear on the battlefield. With the spear you\n can take the initiative; the halberd is defensive. In the hands of one of two\n men of equal ability, the spear gives a little extra strength.”\n    -Miyamoto Musashi, _The Book of Five Rings_. 1645.", "“在战场上，戟不如枪。用枪你可以\n 采取主动；戟是防御性的。在两个能力相当的人\n 手中，枪会多给一点力量。”\n    -宫本武藏，_五轮书_。1645年。"},
+    {"“Bashō Osho said to his disciples, ‘If you have a staff, I will give you a\n staff. If you have no staff, I will take it from you.’\n\n Mumon's Comment\n\n It helps me wade across a river when the bridge is down. It accompanies me to\n the village on a moonless night. If you call it a staff, you will enter hell\n like an arrow.”\n    -Mumon Ekai, _The Gateless Gate_, case 44. 1228.\n     trans. Katsuki Sekida", "“芭蕉和尚对弟子们说：‘如果你有拄杖，我就给你\n 拄杖。如果你没有拄杖，我就从你那里夺走。’\n\n 无门评唱\n\n 桥断时，它助我涉水过河。无月之夜，它伴我前往\n 村庄。如果你称之为拄杖，你就会像箭一样\n 堕入地狱。”\n    -无门慧开，_无门关_，第四十四则。1228年。\n     关田胜树 译"},
+    {"“'I am Aed Abaid of Ess Rúaid, that is, the good god of wizardry of the Tuatha\n Dé Danann, and the Rúad Rofhessa, and Eochaid Ollathair are my three names.’\n\n And thus he was, with Cermait Milbél, one of his sons, on his back, who had\n fallen in fight and combat by Lug, son of Cian, High King of Ireland. The\n Dagda betook himself to his knowledge and learning, and therefore frankincense\n and myrrh and herbs were put around the body of Cermait, and he lifted Cermait\n on his back, and bearing Cermait he searched the world, and came to the great\n eastern world.\n\n He met three men going the road and the way with their father's treasures. The\n Dagda asked news of them, and they said ‘We are three sons of one father and\n mother, and we are sharing our father's treasures.’\n ‘What have ye?’ said the Dagda.\n ‘A shirt and a staff and a cloak,’ said they.\n ‘What virtues have these?’ said the Dagda.\n ‘This great staff that thou seest,’ said he, ‘has a smooth end and a rough\n end. One end slays the living, and the other end brings the dead to life.’”\n    -Osborn Bergin, “How the Dagda Got His Magic Staff”, _Medieval Studies in\n     Memory of Gertrude Schoepperle Loomis_. 1927.", "“'我是埃斯鲁阿德的艾德·阿拜德，也就是图阿萨\n 德·达南的魔法善神，而鲁阿德·罗菲萨和埃奥凯德·奥拉泰尔是我的三个名字。’\n\n 于是他就这样背着他的一个儿子切尔迈特·米尔贝尔，他在战斗交锋中\n 被爱尔兰至高王、基安之子卢格击倒。达格达求助于自己的知识与学问，\n 因此人们在切尔迈特的尸身旁放上乳香、没药和草药，\n 他把切尔迈特背到背上，背负着切尔迈特遍寻世界，\n 来到了伟大的\n 东方世界。\n\n 他遇到三个人带着他们父亲的财宝赶路。达格达\n 向他们打听消息，他们说：‘我们是一父一母所生的三兄弟，\n 正在分我们父亲的财宝。’\n ‘你们有什么？’达格达说。\n ‘一件衬衫、一根法杖和一件斗篷。’他们说。\n ‘这些有什么用处？’达格达说。\n ‘你所见的这根大法杖，’他说，‘一头光滑，一头\n 粗糙。一头杀死生者，另一头让死者复生。’”\n    -Osborn Bergin, “How the Dagda Got His Magic Staff”, _Medieval Studies in\n     Memory of Gertrude Schoepperle Loomis_. 1927."},
+    {"“The wizard suddenly remembered the words of the god. He remembered that of all\n the creatures that people the earth, Fire was the only one who knew his son to\n be a phantom. This memory, which at first calmed him, ended by tormenting him.\n He feared lest his son should meditate on this abnormal privilege and by some\n means find out he was a mere simulacrum. Not to be a man, to be a projection\n of another man's dreams—what an incomparable humiliation, what madness!”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, _The Circular Ruins_. 1940.\n    trans. Anthony Bonner, 1962.", "“巫师忽然想起了神的那些话。他想起，在栖居大地的一切生灵中，\n 唯有火知道他儿子是个幻影。这段记忆起初令他平静，\n 最终却折磨着他。他生怕儿子会思索这反常的特权，\n 并设法发现自己不过是个幻象。不是人，\n 而是他人梦境的投射——这是何等无可比拟的羞辱，\n 何等疯狂的念头！”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, _The Circular Ruins_. 1940.\n    trans. Anthony Bonner, 1962."},
+    {"“His scales are his pride, shut up together as with a close seal. One is so\n near to another, that no air can come between them. They are joined one to\n another, they stick together, that they cannot be sundered.”\n    -KJV Bible, Job 41:15-17.", "“他的鳞甲是他的骄傲，紧紧合拢如同封闭的印记。鳞片彼此\n 如此相近，连空气也不能透入其间。它们彼此相连，\n 黏合在一起，无法分开。”\n    -KJV Bible, Job 41:15-17."},
+    {"“How happy is the little Stone\n That rambles in the Road alone,\n And doesn't care about Careers\n And Exigencies never fears —\n Whose Coat of elemental Brown\n A passing Universe put on,\n And independent as the Sun\n Associates or glows alone,\n Fulfilling absolute Decree\n In casual simplicity —”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “How happy is the little Stone”. ca. 1865.", "“小小石头多么幸福\n 独自在路上漫步，\n 不在乎什么前程\n 也从不畏惧急难——\n 它披着一件元素之褐的外衣\n 由一个路过的宇宙为它穿上，\n 独立如太阳，\n 或结伴或独自发光，\n 在偶然的单纯中\n 履行绝对的旨意——”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “How happy is the little Stone”. ca. 1865."},
+    {"“Stormform is said to cause\n A tempest of winds and showers,\n Beware its powers, beware its powers.\n Though its coming brings the gods their night,\n It obliges a bloodred spren.\n Beware its end, beware its end.”\n    -Brandon Sanderson, “Words of Radiance”. 2014.", "“据说风暴形态会引发\n 风与雨的狂风暴雨，\n 当心它的力量，当心它的力量。\n 尽管它的降临为众神带来黑夜，\n 它却催生出血红色的灵。\n 当心它的结局，当心它的结局。”\n    -Brandon Sanderson, “Words of Radiance”. 2014."},
+    {"“Don’t sing if you want to live long\n They have no use for your song\n You’re dead, you’re dead, you’re dead\n You’re dead and outta this world\n\n You’ll never get a second chance\n Plan all your moves in advance\n Stay dead, stay dead, stay dead\n Stay dead and outta this world”\n    -Norma Tanega, “You’re Dead”. 1966", "“若想活得长久，就别唱歌\n 他们用不着你的歌\n 你死了，你死了，你死了\n 你死了，已不在这个世界\n\n 你再也得不到第二次机会\n 提前谋划好你所有的行动\n 保持死亡，保持死亡，保持死亡\n 保持死亡，已不在这个世界”\n    -Norma Tanega, “You’re Dead”. 1966"},
+    {"“The look of a scared thing\n Sitting in a net!”\n    -Edna St. Vincent Millay, “When the Year Grows Old”. 1917.", "“受惊之物的神情——\n 坐在网中！”\n    -Edna St. Vincent Millay, “When the Year Grows Old”. 1917."},
+    {"“Don't try to rise above it, it's better below\n When life is going by you, you'll never know\n When everything is simple, it's really sublime\n Don't try to rise above it, below it is fine”\n    -Daryl Hall & John Oates, “No Brain, No Pain”. 1979.", "“别想着超脱其上，身在其下更好\n 当生活从你身边流逝，你永远也不会明白\n 当一切都很简单，那真是无比美妙\n 别想着超脱其上，身在其下就很好”\n    -Daryl Hall & John Oates, “No Brain, No Pain”. 1979."},
+    {"“Without noticing the occupations of an intervening day or two, which, as they\n consisted of the ordinary sylvan amusements of shooting and coursing, have\n nothing sufficiently interesting to detain the reader, we pass to one in some\n degree peculiar to Scotland, which may be called a sort of salmon-hunting.\n This chase, in which the fish is pursued and struck with barbed spears, or a\n sort of long shafted trident, called a waster, is much practised at the mouth\n of the Esk, and in the other salmon rivers of Scotland.”\n    -Sir Walter Scott, _Guy Mannering_, ch. XXVI. 1815.", "“未曾留意中间这一两天里发生的事情，由于那不过是些寻常的\n 林间消遣——打猎与追猎——没有什么足以留住读者的趣味，\n 我们便转而讲述一件在苏格兰有些独特的事，\n 或许可称之为一种猎鲑。这种追逐是用带倒钩的矛，\n 或一种叫作 waster 的长柄三叉戟来追赶并刺击鱼，\n 在埃斯克河口以及苏格兰其他鲑鱼河中\n 十分流行。”\n    -Sir Walter Scott, _Guy Mannering_, ch. XXVI. 1815."},
+    {"“Energy may be likened to the bending of a crossbow;\n decision, to the releasing of a trigger.”\n    -Sun Tzu", "“能量可比作拉开的弩弓；\n 决断，则如扣动扳机。”\n    -Sun Tzu"},
+    {"“THE TROLL KING: Now, listen, Prince Peer, and give way to reason!\n You're cut out for a Troll. Why, look, already\n You bear yourself quite in a Troll-like fashion!\n And you want to become one, don't you?\n PEER GYNT: Of course.\n In return for a bride and a well-found kingdom\n I'm not unwilling to sacrifice something;\n But all things have their natural limit.\n I have taken a tail, it is true; but, then,\n I can undo the knots that our friend has tied\n And take the thing off. I have shed my breeches;\n They were old and patched; but that won't prevent me\n From putting them on if I have a mind to.\n I shall probably find it just as easy\n To deal with your Trollish way of living.\n I can easily swear that a cow's a maiden;\n An oath's not a difficult thing to swallow.\n But to know that one never can get one's freedom —\n Not even to die as a human being —\n To end one's days as a Troll of the mountains —\n Never go back, as you tell me plainly —\n That is a thing that I'll not submit to.”\n    -Henrik Ibsen, _Peer Gynt_ . 1867.", "“巨魔之王：喏，听着，培尔王子，讲讲道理吧！\n 你天生就是当巨魔的料。瞧，你早就\n 一举一动都颇有巨魔的样子了！\n 你难道不想变成一个吗？\n 培尔·金特：当然想。\n 为了一位新娘和一个富足安稳的王国\n 我不介意牺牲点什么；\n 但凡事都有其天然的限度。\n 我确实长了条尾巴；可是，话说回来，\n 我能解开咱们这位朋友打的结，\n 把它取下来。我脱掉了自己的马裤；\n 它们又旧又打满补丁；可那也不妨碍我\n 想穿就再穿上。\n 我想应对你们那种巨魔的生活方式\n 大概也一样容易。\n 我可以轻易发誓说一头母牛是少女；\n 发个誓不是什么难以下咽的事。\n 但要知道人永远无法重获自由——\n 甚至不能作为人死去——\n 以山中巨魔的身份了此一生——\n 正如你明明白白告诉我的，再也回不去——\n 这种事我决不答应。”\n    -Henrik Ibsen, _Peer Gynt_ . 1867."},
+    {"“[The principle of selection] is the magician's wand, by means of which he may\n summon into life whatever form and mould he pleases.”\n    -William Youatt, _Sheep: their breeds, management, and diseases; to which\n     is added the Mountain Shepherd's Manual_, ch. III. 1837.", "“[选择的原则]是魔术师的魔杖，凭借它，他可以随心所欲地\n 召唤出任何形态与模子，赋予其生命。”\n    -William Youatt, _Sheep: their breeds, management, and diseases; to which\n     is added the Mountain Shepherd's Manual_, ch. III. 1837."},
+    {"“But it isn't charm as veneer, as facilitating ooze or unguent,\n but charm that identifies and goes out to meet itself in others\n wherever it may be met with.”\n    -Michael Hofmann, “Hell, he'll be frozen stiff!”, London Review of Books,\n     2022.\n\n“He held up his hand, and they all stopped, and I thought he seemed to be\n saying, ‘All these lives will I give you, ay, and many more and greater,\n through countless ages, if you will fall down and worship me!’ And then a red\n cloud, like the colour of blood, seemed to close over my eyes, and before I\n knew what I was doing, I found myself opening the sash and saying to Him,\n ‘Come in, Lord and Master!’”\n    -Bram Stoker, _Dracula_. 1897.", "“但这不是作为虚饰的魅力，不是便利的渗流或软膏，\n 而是那种能辨明自身、并在他人身上迎遇自身的魅力，\n 无论在哪里遇见它。”\n    -Michael Hofmann, “Hell, he'll be frozen stiff!”, London Review of Books,\n     2022.\n\n“他举起手，他们都停了下来，我觉得他仿佛在说：\n ‘只要你肯俯身敬拜我，这一切生命我都赐给你，是的，还有更多更伟大的，\n 历经无数世代！’随后，一片红色的\n 云，犹如血的颜色，似乎合拢在我眼前，我还没\n 弄清自己在做什么，就已经发现自己打开窗扇，\n 对他说：‘进来吧，主与主人！’”\n    -Bram Stoker, _Dracula_. 1897."},
+    {"“Great holes secretly are digged where earth’s pores ought to suffice, and\n things have learnt to walk that ought to crawl.”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft, “The Festival”. 1925", "“大洞在暗地里被掘出，那些本应足够的地之孔隙，\n 而本该爬行的东西学会了行走。”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft, “The Festival”. 1925"},
+    {"“Als Gregor Samsa eines Morgens aus unruhigen Träumen erwachte, fand er sich in\n seinem Bett zu einem ungeheueren Ungeziefer verwandelt.”\n    -Franz Kafka, _Die Verwandlung_. 1915", "“当格里高尔·萨姆沙一天早晨从不安的梦中醒来，发现自己\n 在床上变成了一只巨大的甲虫。”\n    -Franz Kafka, _Die Verwandlung_. 1915"},
+    {"“‘God speed the kiss,’ said Max, and Katie sigh'd,\n With pray'rful palms close seal'd, ‘God speed the axe!’”\n    -Isabella Valancey Crawford, “Malcolm's Katie: A Love Story”, Part I, _Old\n     Spookses' Pass, Malcolm's Katie and Other Poems_. 1884.", "“愿上帝成全这一吻，”马克斯说，凯蒂叹息道，\n 双手虔诚地紧紧合十，“愿上帝成全这斧头！”\n    ——伊莎贝拉·瓦伦西·克劳福德，《马尔科姆的凯蒂：一个爱情故事》，第一部，收入《老\n     幽灵隘口、马尔科姆的凯蒂及其他诗篇》。1884年。"},
+    {"“Not with a Club, the Heart is broken\n Nor with a Stone —\n A Whip so small you could not see it\n I've known\n To lash the Magic Creature\n Till it fell,\n Yet that Whip's Name\n Too noble then to tell.”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “Not with a Club, the Heart is broken”. ca. 1865.\n########################################\n#\n# Unique monsters\n#\n########################################", "“不是以棍棒，心被击碎\n 也不是以石头——\n 一根细小得你无法看见的鞭子\n 我早已知晓\n 它能抽打那神奇的生灵\n 直到它倒下，\n 然而那鞭子的名字\n 彼时太过高贵，不可言说。”\n    ——艾米莉·狄金森，《不是以棍棒，心被击碎》。约1865年。\n########################################\n#\n# Unique monsters\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"Wasn't it beautiful running wild 'til you fell asleep\nBefore the monsters caught up to you?\n    -Taylor Swift, _Innocent_, 2010.", "肆意撒野，直到你沉沉睡去，难道不美吗\n在怪物追赶上你之前？\n    ——泰勒·斯威夫特，《清白》，2010年。"},
+    {"“The fact is that I am unique. What a man can pass unto others does not\n interest me; like the philosopher, I think nothing is communicated by the art\n of writing. Annoying and trivial minutiae have no place in my spirit, a spirit\n which is receptive only to whatsoever is grand.”\n  -Jorge Luis Borges, “The House of Asterion”. 1947.\n   trans. Antonios Sarhanis, 2008.", "“事实是，我是独一无二的。一个人能传给别人的东西，我并不\n 感兴趣；和那位哲学家一样，我认为写作这门艺术传达不了任何东西。\n 琐碎恼人的细枝末节在我的灵魂中没有位置，我的灵魂\n 只接纳一切宏大之物。”\n  ——豪尔赫·路易斯·博尔赫斯，《阿斯特里昂之家》。1947年。\n   安东尼奥斯·萨哈尼斯 译，2008年。"},
+    {"“That country was then ruled by Antaeus, son of Poseidon, who used to kill\n strangers by forcing them to wrestle. Being forced to wrestle with him,\n Hercules hugged him, lifted him aloft, broke and killed him; for when he\n touched earth so it was that he waxed stronger, wherefore some said that he\n was a son of Earth.”\n    -Pseudo-Apollodorus , _Library and Epitome_, 2.5.11. ca. 150 BC.\n    trans. Sir James George Frazer, 1913.\n\n“The head foreman in the mine, called Antaeus,\n was an obese giant with a thick black beard\n who actually seemed to draw his strength from Mother Earth.”\n    -Primo Levi, “The Periodic Table”. 1975.", "“那片国土当时由波塞冬之子安泰俄斯统治，他惯于强迫陌生人\n 与他摔跤，借此杀死他们。赫拉克勒斯被迫与他摔跤时，\n 将他抱住，高高举起，折断并杀死了他；因为他一旦\n 触到大地，便会变得更加健壮，因此有人说\n 他是大地之子。”\n    ——伪阿波罗多洛斯，《书库与摘要》，2.5.11。约公元前150年。\n    詹姆斯·乔治·弗雷泽爵士 译，1913年。\n\n“矿上的总工头名叫安泰俄斯，\n 是个肥胖的巨人，长着浓密的黑胡子，\n 他似乎真能从大地母亲那里汲取力量。”\n    ——普里莫·莱维，《元素周期表》。1975年。"},
+    {"“For myself, I have other occupations:  I make absurd matches; I marry\n greybeards with minors, masters with servants, girls with small fortunes with\n tender lovers who have none. It is I who introduced into this world luxury,\n debauchery, games of chance, and chemistry. I am the author of the first\n cookery book, the inventor of festivals, of dancing, music, plays, and of the\n newest fashions; in a word, I am ASMODEUS, surnamed The Devil on Two Sticks.”\n    -Alain René Le Sage, _Asmodeus: Or, The Devil on Two Sticks_. 1707.", "“就我而言，我另有营生：我撮合荒唐的姻缘；我让\n 老头与少女、主人与仆人、有些许嫁妆的姑娘与\n 身无分文的温柔情人结为夫妻。是我把奢华、\n 放荡、赌博与炼金术引入了这个世界。我是第一部\n 烹饪书的作者，是节庆、舞蹈、音乐、戏剧以及\n 最新时尚的发明者；一言以蔽之，我便是阿斯莫德，绰号‘双杖魔鬼’。”\n    ——阿兰·勒内·勒萨日，《阿斯莫德：或双杖魔鬼》。1707年。"},
+    {"“And Allah said,\n ‘As thou the deed hast done, so now the office shall be thine, O Azrael,\n  to gather up for me the souls of men and women when their time has come;\n  the souls of saints and sinners, of beggars and of princes,\n  of the old or young, whate'er befall; and even though friends weep,\n  and hearts of loved ones ache with sorrow and with anguish,\n  when bereft of those they love.’\n So Azrael became the messenger of Death.”\n    -J. E. Hanauer, _Folk-lore of the Holy Land, Moslem, Christian and Jewish_.\n1907.", "“真主说道，\n ‘既然你已行了此事，那么如今这职分便归你，阿兹拉尔啊，\n  待时辰来临之时，为我收取男女的灵魂；\n  圣徒与罪人的灵魂，乞丐与王子的灵魂，\n  无论老幼，无论遭遇何事；纵使亲友哭泣，\n  挚爱之人的心因悲伤与痛苦而绞痛，\n  当他们失去所爱之人时。’\n 于是阿兹拉尔成了死亡的使者。”\n    ——J. E. 哈瑙尔，《圣地的民间传说：穆斯林、基督徒与犹太人》。\n1907年。"},
+    {"“There was an Old Man with a beard,\n Who said, ‘It is just as I feared!\n Two Owls and a Hen,\n Four Larks and a Wren,\n Have all built their nests in my beard!’”\n    -Edward Lear, _A Book of Nonsense [No. 1]_. 1846.", "“有位老人留着一把胡子，\n 他说：‘这正是我所担心的！\n 两只猫头鹰和一只母鸡，\n 四只云雀和一只鹪鹩，\n 都在我的胡子里筑了巢！’”\n    ——爱德华·李尔，《荒诞集[第1首]》。1846年。"},
+    {"“Hoc idem magis ostendit antiquius Iovis nomen: nam olim Diovis et Diespiter\n dictus, id est dies pater; a quo dei dicti qui inde, et dius et divum, unde\n sub divo, Dius Fidius. Itaque inde eius perforatum tectum, ut ea videatur\n divum, id est caelum. Quidam negant sub tecto per hunc deierare oportere.\n Aelius Dium Fidium dicebat Diovis filium, ut Graeci Dioskopon Castorem, et\n putabat hunc esse Sancum ab Sabina lingua et Herculem a Graeca. Idem hic Dis\n pater dicitur infimus, qui est coniunctus terrae, ubi omnia ut oriuntur ita\n aboriuntur; quorum quod finis ortuum, Orcus dictus.”\n    -Marcus Terentius Varro, _De Lingua Latina_, Liber V, circa 40 BC.", "“这同一事更能说明朱庇特那个更古老的名字：从前他被称为 Diovis 和 Diespiter，\n 意即“白日之父”；由此，凡源于他者皆称 dei，又有 dius 和 divum，故有\n sub divo、Dius Fidius 之说。因此他的庙顶开有孔洞，以便得见\n divum，即天空。有人说不应在屋顶之下以他的名义起誓。\n 埃利乌斯说 Dius Fidius 是 Diovis 之子，正如希腊人称卡斯托耳为 Dioskopos，\n 并认为此人即萨宾语所称的 Sancus、希腊语所称的赫拉克勒斯。同一位他\n 又被称为 Dis pater，居于最下，与大地相连，万物在那里生而复灭；\n 其生之终点，遂被称为 Orcus。”\n    ——马库斯·特伦提乌斯·瓦罗，《论拉丁语》第五卷，约公元前40年。"},
+    {"“Skill and grace, the twin brother and sister, are dancing playfully on your\n finger tips.”\n    -Rabindranath Tagore, _Chitra_, Act I, Scene iv. 1914.", "“技艺与优雅，这对孪生的兄妹，正在你的\n 指尖上嬉戏起舞。”\n    ——拉宾德拉纳特·泰戈尔，《齐德拉》，第一幕第四场。1914年。"},
+    {"“Twin children: the Girl, she was plain;\n The Brother was handsome & vain;\n ‘Let him brag of his looks,’\n Father said; ‘mind your books!\n The best beauty is bred in the brain.’”\n    -Aesop & Walter Crane, _The Baby's Own Aesop_, 36: “Brother & Sister”.\n1887.", "“孪生兄妹：那女孩，相貌平平；\n 那男孩，英俊而虚荣；\n ‘让他吹嘘自己的容貌吧，’\n 父亲说；‘专心读你的书！\n 最美的美，孕育于头脑之中。’”\n    ——伊索与沃尔特·克莱恩，《宝宝自己的伊索寓言》，第36篇：“兄妹”。\n1887年。"},
+    {"“And my invention thrive, Edmund the base\n Shall top the legitimate. I grow; I prosper.—\n Now, gods, stand up for bastards!”\n    -William Shakespeare, _King Lear_, I, ii. 1606.\n\n“When the forces stood in array Edmund proposed to decide their claims by\n single combat; but Canute saying that he, a man of small stature, would have\n little chance against the tall athletic Edmund, proposed, on the contrary, for\n them to divide the realm as their fathers had done.”\n    -Thomas Keightley, _The History of England_. 1839.", "“我的计谋得逞，卑贱的埃德蒙\n 将凌驾于合法子嗣之上。我成长；我兴旺。——\n 如今，诸神啊，为私生子撑腰吧！”\n    ——威廉·莎士比亚，《李尔王》，第一幕第二场。1606年。\n\n“当两军列阵对峙时，埃德蒙提议以\n 单打独斗来裁决他们的王位主张；但卡努特说，他一个\n 身材矮小之人，面对高大健壮的埃德蒙胜算渺茫，于是\n 反过来提议，让他们像父辈那样瓜分王国。”\n    ——托马斯·凯特利，《英格兰史》。1839年。"},
+    {"“I thoroughly disapprove of duels. I consider them unwise and I know they are\n dangerous. Also, sinful. If a man should challenge me, I would take him kindly\n and forgivingly by the hand and lead him to a quiet retired spot and kill\n him.”\n    -Mark Twain, _Autobiography of Mark Twain_. 1924.", "“我完全反对决斗。我认为它不明智，也知道它很\n 危险。而且，它还有罪。若有人向我挑战，我会友善\n 而宽容地牵起他的手，领他到一处安静的僻静之地，把\n 他杀掉。”\n    ——马克·吐温，《马克·吐温自传》。1924年。"},
+    {"“Khrysaor, married to Kallirhoe, daughter of glorious Okeanos, was father to\n the triple-headed Geryon, but Geryon was killed by the great strength of\n Herakles at sea-circled Erytheis beside his own shambling cattle on that day\n when Herakles drove those broad-faced cattle toward holy Tiryns, when he\n crossed the stream of Okeanos and had killed Orthos and the oxherd Eurytion\n out in the gloomy meadow beyond fabulous Okeanos.”\n    -Hesiod, _Theogony_, circa 700 BCE.", "“克律萨俄耳娶了光荣的俄刻阿诺斯之女卡利罗厄，生下了\n 三头革律翁，但革律翁被赫拉克勒斯的巨力所杀，\n 就在环海的厄律忒亚，在他自己蹒跚的牛群旁，那一天\n 赫拉克勒斯赶着这些宽脸牛群前往神圣的提林斯，\n 当他渡过俄刻阿诺斯的流水，杀死了俄耳托斯和牛倌欧律提翁，\n 就在那神话般的俄刻阿诺斯之外幽暗的草地上。”\n    ——赫西俄德，《神谱》，约公元前700年。"},
+    {"“One, you lock the target\n Two, you bait the line\n Three, you slowly spread the net\n And four, you catch the man\n\n You catch the man\n You catch the man\n I’m looking for this man to sell him to other men\n To sell him to other men at ten times the price at least”\n    —Front 242, “Headhunter”", "“一，你锁定目标\n 二，你布下诱饵\n 三，你慢慢撒开罗网\n 四，你抓住那人\n\n 你抓住那人\n 你抓住那人\n 我在找这个人，好把他卖给其他人\n 至少以十倍的价格卖给其他人”\n    ——Front 242，《猎头者》"},
+    {"“We have lingered in the chambers of the sea\n By sea-girls wreathed with seaweed red and brown\n Till human voices wake us, and we drown.”\n    -T.S. Eliot, _The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock_. lines 129-131. 1915.", "“我们曾在海之幽室里徘徊\n 身边是海藻缠身的红褐海女\n 直到人声将我们唤醒，我们便溺亡。”\n    ——T.S. 艾略特，《J. 阿尔弗雷德·普鲁弗洛克的情歌》。第129-131行。1915年。"},
+    {"“And then I looked farther, beyond the pallid line of the sands, and I saw a\n Pyramid of gold, the wonder Khufu had built. As a golden wonder it saluted me,\n as a golden thing it greeted me, as a golden miracle I shall remember it.”\n    -Robert Hichens, _The Spell of Egypt_. 1910.", "“随后我又向远方望去，越过沙滩苍白的分界线，我看见了\n 一座黄金金字塔，那是胡夫所建的奇迹。它如黄金般的奇迹向我致意，\n 如黄金般的事物迎接我，如黄金般的奇迹我将铭记于心。”\n    ——罗伯特·希钦斯，《埃及的魔力》。1910年。"},
+    {"“Lo, thy comrades yonder in the house of Kirke are penned like swine in\n close-barred sties. And art thou come to release them? Nay, I tell thee, thou\n shalt not thyself return, but shalt remain there with the others.”\n    -Homer, Odysseia", "“看哪，你的同伴就在那边基尔克的宅邸里，像猪一样被关在\n 紧闭的猪栏中。你是来释放他们的吗？不，我告诉你，你\n 自己也不能返回，而将和其他人一同留在那里。”\n    ——荷马，《奥德赛》"},
+    {"“The second Labour which he undertook was the slaying of the Lernaian Hydra,\n springing from whose single body were fashioned a hundred necks, each bearing\n the head of a serpent. And when one head was cut off, the place where it was\n severed put forth two others; for this reason it was considered to be\n invincible, and with good reason, since the part of it which was subdued sent\n forth a two-fold assistance in its place. Against a thing so difficult to\n manage as this Herakles devised an ingenious scheme and commanded Iolaos to\n sear with a burning brand the part which had been severed, in order to check\n the flow of the blood.”\n    -Diodorus Siculus, _Bibliotheca Historica_. [4. 11. 5.]. c. 100.", "“他所承担的第二项功业，是杀死勒耳那的九头蛇，\n 从它单一的身躯中长出上百条脖颈，每一根都顶着一颗\n 蛇头。每当砍下一颗头，断口处便会生出\n 另外两颗；因此它被认为不可战胜，\n 而且理由充分，因为它被制服的部分会在原处生出\n 双倍的助力。面对如此难以对付之物，\n 赫拉克勒斯想出了一条妙计，命伊俄拉俄斯\n 用燃烧的火把灼烧断口，\n 以止住血液的流淌。”\n    ——狄奥多罗斯·西库鲁斯，《历史丛书》。[4. 11. 5.]。约公元100年。"},
+    {"“Cat:  But who can say what a thing is worth.\n Girl: The free market, supposedly.\n Cat:  What _price_ do we put on a _soul_?\n Girl: $7.25 an hour.”\n    -Dorothy Gambrell, _Cat and Girl_, 2010.", "“猫：  可谁能说清一样东西值多少。\n 女孩：自由市场，大概是吧。\n 猫：  我们给一个_灵魂_标上_多少价_？\n 女孩：每小时7.25美元。”\n    ——多萝西·甘布雷尔，《猫与女孩》，2010年。"},
+    {"“This night the Lord of Illusion passed among you, Mara, mighty among dreamers,\n mighty for ill. He did come upon another who may work with the stuff of dreams\n in a different way. He did meet with Dharma, who may expel a dreamer from his\n dream. They did struggle, and the Lord Mara is no more. Why did they struggle,\n deathgod against illusionist? You say their ways are incomprehensible, being\n the ways of gods. This is not the answer.”\n    -Roger Zelazny, “Lord of Light”. 1967.\n\n“He who lives looking for pleasures only,\n his senses uncontrolled, immoderate in his food,\n idle, and weak, Mara will certainly overthrow him,\n as the wind throws down a weak tree.”\n    -The Buddha, _Dhammapada_, 1:7.\n     trans. F. Max Muller", "“今夜，幻象之王从你们中间走过，摩罗，在梦中人之间强大无比，\n 在作恶上强大无比。他确实遇上了另一位能以不同方式\n 操控梦境之材的人。他确实见到了达摩，那位能将做梦者\n 逐出其梦境的人。他们确实争斗了，而摩罗王已不复存在。为何他们要争斗，\n 死神对幻象师？你说他们的行事之道不可理解，因为\n 那是神之道。这不是答案。”\n    ——罗杰·泽拉兹尼，《光明王》。1967年。\n\n“唯求享乐而活者，\n 诸根不制，饮食无度，\n 懈怠而软弱，摩罗必将其击倒，\n 如风吹倒弱树。”\n    ——佛陀，《法句经》，1:7。\n     F. 马克斯·缪勒 译"},
+    {"“‘Stop thief! Stop thief!’ There is a magic in the sound. The tradesman leaves\n his counter, and the car-man his waggon; the butcher throws down his tray; the\n baker his basket; the milkman his pail; the errand-boy his parcels; the\n school-boy his marbles; the paviour his pickaxe; the child his battledore.\n Away they run, pell-mell, helter-skelter, slap-dash: tearing, yelling,\n screaming, knocking down the passengers as they turn the corners, rousing up\n the dogs, and astonishing the fowls: and streets, squares, and courts, re-echo\n with the sound.”\n    -Charles Dickens, _Oliver Twist_. 1838.", "“‘抓贼啊！抓贼啊！’这喊声有种魔力。商人放下\n 柜台，车夫丢下马车；屠夫扔下托盘；\n 面包师扔下篮子；送奶工扔下奶桶；跑腿的男孩扔下包裹；\n 上学的孩子扔下弹珠；铺路工扔下镐头；小孩扔下\n 球拍。他们撒腿就跑，乱作一团，慌里慌张，劈里啪啦：撕扯着、号叫着、\n 尖叫着，转过街角时撞倒行人，惊起群狗，\n 吓坏家禽：街道、广场和庭院都回荡着\n 这喊声。”\n    ——查尔斯·狄更斯，《雾都孤儿》。1838年。"},
+    {"“Ye men of Egypt, ye have heard your king!\n I go, and I return not. But the will\n Of the great Gods is plain; and ye must bring\n Ill deeds, ill passions, zealous to fulfil\n Their pleasure, to their feet; and reap their praise,\n The praise of Gods, rich boon! and length of days.”\n    -Matthew Arnold, _Mycerinus_", "“埃及的子民啊，你们已听见你们王的旨意！\n 我去了，不再回来。但伟大的\n 众神之意昭然；你们必须将\n 恶行、恶念，热切地献上以遂\n 其欢心，跪伏在他们脚边；并收获他们的赞美，\n 众神的赞美，丰厚的恩赐！以及长寿。”\n    ——马修·阿诺德，《迈西里努斯》"},
+    {"“Look behind you! A three-headed monkey!”\n    -Guybrush Threepwood, _The Secret of Monkey Island_", "“看你身后！一只三头猴子！”\n    ——盖布拉什·斯里普伍德，《猴岛小英雄》"},
+    {"“It dooth appéere that there is in Cats as in all other kindes of beasts, a\n certaine reason and language wherby they vnderstand one another. But as\n touching this Grimmalkin: I take rather to be an Hagat or a VVitch then a Cat.\n For witches haue gone often in that likenes, And therof hath come the prouerb\n as trew as common, that a Cat hath nine liues, that is to say, a witch may\n take on her a Cats body nine times.”\n    -William Baldwin, “Beware the Cat”, 1584", "“由此可见，猫与所有其他种类的野兽一样，\n 有着某种理性和语言，借此彼此理解。\n 但至于这只格里马尔金：我宁可认为她是个巫婆或女巫，而非一只猫。\n 因为巫婆常常化作那种模样，由此便有了那句\n 既真实又寻常的谚语：猫有九条命，也就是说，一个巫婆可以\n 九次披上猫的躯体。”\n    ——威廉·鲍德温，“当心那只猫”，1584"},
+    {"“One can prophesy with a Daniel's confidence that skilled electricians will\n settle the battles of the near future.”\n    -Nikola Tesla, “The Transmission of Electrical Energy Without Wires As a\nMeans for Furthering Peace”, _Electrical World and Engineer_. January 7, 1905.", "“人们可以怀着但以理般的自信预言，技艺娴熟的电气技师\n 将决定不久将来的战役。”\n    ——尼古拉·特斯拉，《以无线方式传输电能\n以促进和平》，载于《电气世界与工程师》。1905年1月7日。"},
+    {"“Death and death alone gives meaning to life and this meaning is entirely\nnegative.”\n    -Georges Poulet", "“死亡，唯有死亡赋予生命以意义，而这意义完全是\n消极的。”\n    ——乔治·普莱"},
+    {"“What are you going to do with that big bat? Gonna hit me? Better make it\n count. Better make it hurt. Better kill me in one shot.”\n   -Mr. Baseball, @MisterBaseball5 Twitter. 2017", "“你打算拿那根大棒子干什么？要打我吗？最好\n 打准点。最好打疼点。最好一击把我打死。”\n   ——棒球先生，@MisterBaseball5 推特。2017年"},
+    {"“...as soon as he had got through with all his work, he clutched up two more of\n my men, and began eating them for his morning's meal. Presently, with the\n utmost ease, he rolled the stone away from the door and drove out his sheep,\n but he at once put it back again—as easily as though he were merely clapping\n the lid on to a quiver full of arrows.”\n    -Homer, _The Odyssey_, Book IX.\n    trans. Samuel Butler, 1900.", "“……他刚干完所有的活，就一把抓起我的\n 两个人，当作早餐吃了起来。不一会儿，他无比轻松地\n 将石头从门口滚开，赶出他的羊群，\n 但随即又把它推回原处——轻松得仿佛只是给\n 装满箭矢的箭袋盖上盖子。”\n    ——荷马，《奥德赛》，第九章。\n    塞缪尔·巴特勒 译，1900年。"},
+    {"“Princess! youngest princess!\n Open the door for me!\n Dost thou not know what thou saidst to me\n\n Yesterday by the cool waters of the fountain?\n Princess, youngest princess!\n Open the door for me!”\n    -Brothers Grimm (Margaret Hunt), _The Frog King, or Iron Henry_", "“公主！最小的公主！\n 为我开门吧！\n 难道你不记得你曾对我说过的话吗\n\n 在水泉清凉的水边？\n 公主，最小的公主！\n 为我开门吧！”\n    ——格林兄弟（玛格丽特·亨特 译），《青蛙王子，或铁亨利》"},
+    {"“But Sigmund turned him about, and he said: ‘What aileth thee, son?\n Shall our life-days never be merry, and our labour never be done?’\n\n But Sinfiotli said: ‘I have looked, and lo, there is death in the cup.’\n\n And the song, and the tinkling of harp-strings to the roof-tree winded up;\n And Sigmund was dreamy with wine and the wearing of many a year;\n And the noise and the glee of the people as the sound of the wild woods were\n And the blossoming boughs of the Branstock were the wild trees waving about;\n\n So he said: ‘Well seen, my fosterling; let the lip then strain it out.’”\n    -William Morris, _The Story of Sigurd the Volsung and the Fall of the\nNiblungs_. 1891.", "“但西格蒙德转过身来，说道：‘孩子，你有何烦恼？\n 难道我们的岁月永无欢愉，我们的劳作永无止境？’\n\n 但辛菲奥特利说：‘我已看过，瞧，杯中藏着死亡。’\n\n 歌声与竖琴的叮咚声缭绕着升上屋栋；\n 西格蒙德沉醉于美酒与多年的风霜；\n 人群的喧闹与欢腾如同莽林的声响；\n 布兰斯托克盛放的花枝恰似摇曳的野树；\n\n 于是他说：‘好眼力，我的养子；且让双唇将它滤尽。’”\n    -威廉·莫里斯，《西格德·沃尔松的故事与\n尼伯龙根的沦亡》。1891年。"},
+    {"“He saith that Tiamat our mother hath conceived a hatred for us,\n With all her force she rageth, full of wrath.\n All the gods have turned to her,\n With those, whom ye created, they go at her side.\n They are banded together, and at the side of Tiamat they advance;\n They are furious, they devise mischief without resting night and day.\n They prepare for battle, fuming and raging;\n They have joined their forces and are making war.\n Tiamat who formed all things,\n Made in addition weapons invincible; she spawned monster-serpents,\n Sharp of tooth, and merciless of fang;\n With poison, instead of blood, she filled their bodies.\n Fierce monster-vipers she clothed with terror,\n With splendor she decked them, she made them of lofty stature.\n Whoever beheld them, terror overcame him,\n Their bodies reared up and none could withstand their attack.”\n    -Enuma Elish, Third Tablet. circa 668 BCE.", "“他说，我们的母亲提亚马特已对我们怀有憎恨，\n 她怒火中烧，倾尽全力大发雷霆。\n 众神都已转向她，\n 与你所创造的那些一道，他们站在她那边。\n 他们结成一伙，紧随着提亚马特前进；\n 他们狂怒，日夜不休地图谋恶事。\n 他们怒气冲冲，准备开战；\n 他们合兵一处，发动战争。\n 创造万物的提亚马特，\n 又造出无可匹敌的兵器；她生下巨蛇怪，\n 牙齿锋利，獠牙无情；\n 她以毒液代替血液，充满它们的躯体。\n 她让凶猛的巨蛇披上恐怖，\n 以威严装点它们，使它们身躯高耸。\n 凡看见它们的，都被恐怖攫住，\n 它们昂起身躯，无人能抵挡它们的攻击。”\n    -《埃努玛·埃利什》，第三块泥板。约公元前668年。"},
+    {"“_Urug_, tumbled down, fallen by crumbling down. Slipped down, as earth from a\n hill-side, stones which have been piled up or the like. To fall as water at a\n cascade. To fill up a hollow by putting earth into it. To lay gravel or\n materials on a road. A landslip.\n\n _Ururugan_, apparently a plural form of 'Urug'. To go with a number of men to\n any work. To make war, to attack with an army. To set upon in numbers, as it\n were to tumble upon in masses.”\n   -Jonathan Rigg, A dictionary of the Sunda language of Java. 1862.", "“_Urug_，滚落，因崩塌而坠落。如土石从山坡滑落，\n 如堆积的石头之类。如水在瀑布处泻落。用泥土填满凹陷。\n 在路上铺砾石或材料。\n 一场山崩。\n\n _Ururugan_，显然是‘Urug’的复数形式。带一批人去干某种活计。\n 开战，率军进攻。成群扑上，\n 仿佛成批滚落一般。”\n   -乔纳森·里格，《爪哇巽他语词典》。1862年。"},
+    {"“Beside the man stands a woman - of sorts. Her emulation of the human gender is\n only superficial, a courtesy. Likewise the loose drapelike dress she wears is\n not cloth. She has simply shaped a portion of her stiff substance to suit the\n preferences of the fragile, mortal creatures among whom she currently moves.\n From a distance the illusion would work to pass her off as a woman standing\n still...\n\n When she turns to the man - slowly; stone eaters are slow aboveground, except\n when they aren't - this movement pushes her beyond artful beauty into something\n altogether different. The man has grown used to it, but even so, he does not\n look at her. He does not want revulsion to spoil the moment.”\n    -N.K. Jemisin, “The Fifth Season”. 2015.\n\n########################################\n#\n# Vault monsters\n#\n########################################", "“男人身旁站着一个女人——勉强算是。\n 她对人类性别的模仿只是表面的，是一种礼节。同样，\n 她身上那件宽松如袍的衣裙也并非布料。她只是将自身\n 坚硬材质的一部分塑成形状，以迎合她眼下交往的那些\n 脆弱凡人的喜好。远看之下，这幻象足以让人把她当作\n 一个静立的女人……\n\n 当她转向那男人时——很慢；石食者在地面上很慢，\n 除非它们不是——这动作使她超越了精巧的美，成为\n 全然不同的东西。男人已经习惯了，但即便如此，\n 他也不看她。他不愿厌恶之感破坏此刻。”\n    -N·K·杰米辛，《第五季》。2015年。\n\n########################################\n#\n# Vault monsters\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"“I beheld the wretch — the miserable monster whom I had\n created. He held up the curtain of the bed; and his eyes, if\n eyes they may be called, were fixed on me. His jaws opened,\n and he muttered some inarticulate sounds, while a grin\n wrinkled his cheeks.”\n    -Mary Shelley, _Frankenstein_. 1818.", "“我看见那怪物——我亲手创造的悲惨怪物。\n 他掀起床帷；他的眼睛，如果那也能叫眼睛的话，\n 死死盯着我。他张开下颚，\n 嘟囔着一些含糊不清的声音，\n 同时一个笑容在他的脸颊上皱起。”\n    -玛丽·雪莱，《弗兰肯斯坦》。1818年。"},
+    {"“And when\n His master plan is unfurled\n There stands a handsome bid\n On the weather systems of the world”\n    -Andrew Bird, “Banking on a Myth”. 2005.", "“而当\n 他的宏伟蓝图展开\n 便有一位英俊的竞标者\n 立于世界的天气系统之上”\n    -安德鲁·伯德，《押注神话》。2005年。"},
+    {"“And when you look long into an abyss, the abyss also looks into you.”\n    -Friedrich Nietzche, “Beyond Good and Evil”. 1886.", "“当你长久凝视深渊时，深渊也在凝视着你。”\n    -弗里德里希·尼采，《善恶的彼岸》。1886年。"},
+    {"“Blood, sweat, and tea, sister! That's what it takes to achieve all great and\n terrible things.”\n    -Emory R. Frie, _Wonderland_. 2016.", "“鲜血、汗水与茶，姐妹！要成就一切伟大而\n 可怕的事业，靠的就是这些。”\n    -埃默里·R·弗莱，《仙境》。2016年。"},
+    {"“Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live.”\n    -KJV Bible, Exodus 22:18.\n########################################\n#\n# Monster glyphs\n#\n########################################", "“行邪术的女人，不可容她存活。”\n    -钦定版圣经，出埃及记22:18。\n########################################\n#\n# Monster glyphs\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"“When Peleus, some distance away, saw him torn apart by the frightful wound he\n shouted: ‘Accept this tribute to the dead, at least, Crantor, dearest of\n youths’, and with his powerful arm, he hurled his ash spear, at full strength,\n at Demoleon. It ruptured the ribcage, and stuck quivering in the bone. The\n centaur pulled out the shaft minus its head (he tried with difficulty to reach\n that also) but the head was caught in his lung. The pain itself strengthened\n his will: wounded, he reared up at his enemy and beat the hero down with his\n hooves. Peleus received the resounding blows on helmet and shield, and\n defending his upper arms, and controlling the weapon he held out, with one\n blow through the arm he pierced the bi-formed breast.”\n    -Ovid, _Metamorphoses_, XII, 330. 8 AD.", "“当佩琉斯远远望见克然托尔被那可怕的伤口撕裂时，\n 他喊道：‘克然托尔，最亲爱的青年，至少请接受这份\n 献给死者的祭礼’，随即他用有力的手臂，\n 将白蜡木长矛全力掷向德摩勒翁。长矛刺穿肋腔，\n 颤动着扎进骨头。半人马拔出无头的矛杆\n （他也费力地想去够那矛头），但矛头已嵌入他的肺中。\n 伤痛本身反倒坚定了他的意志：受伤的他挺起身扑向敌人，\n 用蹄子将这位英雄击倒。佩琉斯以头盔和盾牌承受轰响的打击，\n 一面护住上臂，一面控制着手中的兵器，\n 一击刺穿手臂，贯穿了那兼具两形的胸膛。”\n    -奥维德，《变形记》，第十二卷，330行。公元8年。"},
+    {"“Trogdor was a man!\n I mean, he was a dragon-man!\n Or... maybe he was just a dragon.\n ...\n And the Trogdor comes in the night!”\n    -The Brothers Chaps, “Trogdor”. 2003.", "“特罗格多是个人！\n 我是说，他是个龙人！\n 或者……也许他就是条龙。\n ……\n 而特罗格多在夜里降临！”\n    -查普斯兄弟，《特罗格多》。2003年。"},
+    {"HAMLET [Drawing his sword.]: How now! a rat? Dead, for a ducat, dead\n       [Stabs through the arras.]\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark_, III, 4.\n1603.", "哈姆雷特［拔剑］：怎么！有老鼠？赌一个金币，它死定了，死定了\n       ［隔着帷幕刺去。］\n    -威廉·莎士比亚，《丹麦王子哈姆雷特的悲剧》，第三幕，第四场。\n1603年。"},
+    {"“On the other hand, Confucius is made to say to his disciples, ‘I know how\n birds can fly, how fishes can swim, and how animals can run. But the runner\n may be snared, the swimmer may be hooked, and the flyer may be shot by the\n arrow. But there is the dragon. I cannot tell how he mounts on the wind\n through the clouds, and rises to heaven. Today I have seen Lao-tsze, and can\n only compare him to the dragon.’”\n    -Life of Confucius\n\n“This Dragon had Two furious Wings\n   Each one upon each Shoulder;\n With a Sting in his Tail as long as a Flail,\n   Which made him bolder and bolder.\n He had long Claws, and in his Jaws\n   Four and forty Teeth of Iron;\n With a Hide as tough, as any Buff,\n   Which did him round environ.”\n\n    -“An Excellent Ballad of a most dreadful Combat, fought between Moore of\nMoore-Hall, and the Dragon of Wantley”, retold by Ambrose Philips, _A\nCollection of Old Ballads. Corrected from the Best and Most Ancient Copies\nExtant. With Introductions Historical, Critical, Or Humorous_. 1723.", "“另一方面，据说孔子曾对弟子说：\n ‘我知道鸟能飞，鱼能游，兽能跑。但跑者\n 可以被网住，游者可以被钓起，飞者可以被箭射落。\n 唯有龙，我不知道它如何乘风云而上天。\n 今日我见到了老子，\n 只能将他比作龙。’”\n    -《孔子传》\n\n“这条龙生有两只狂暴的翅膀\n   一边肩膀各一只；\n 尾上有一根如连枷般长的毒刺，\n   令他愈发胆大。\n 他生有长爪，他的嘴里\n   长着四十四颗铁牙；\n 他的皮坚韧如牛皮，\n   将他周身包裹。”\n\n    -《关于摩尔厅的摩尔与旺特利的龙之间一场最可怕战斗的\n绝妙歌谣》，安布罗斯·菲利普斯重述，《古歌谣集。\n从现存最佳最古抄本校订。附历史、批评或诙谐的导言》。\n1723年。"},
+    {"“The Parts Septentrionall are with these Sp'ryts Much haunted.. About the\n places where they dig for Oare. The Greekes and Germans call them Cobali.”\n    -Thomas Heywood, _The Hierarchy of the Blessed Angels_, Book IX, l. 568.\n1635.", "“北方诸地多有这些精灵出没……在人们挖掘矿石之处附近。\n 希腊人和日耳曼人称它们为科巴利。”\n    -托马斯·海伍德，《蒙福天使的品级》，第九卷，第568行。\n1635年。"},
+    {"“The latter lived in the country, and before his house there was an oak, in\n which there was a lair of snakes. His servants killed the snakes, but Melampus\n gathered wood and burnt the reptiles, and reared the young ones. And when the\n young were full grown, they stood beside him at each of his shoulders as he\n slept, and they purged his ears with their tongues. He started up in a great\n fright, but understood the voices of the birds flying overhead, and from what\n he learned from them he foretold to men what should come to pass.”\n    -Pseudo-Apollodorus , _Library and Epitome_, 1.9.11. circa 150 BC.\n    trans. Sir James George Frazer, 1913.\n\n“A snake, with mottles rare,\n Surveyed my chamber floor,\n In feature as the worm before,\n But ringed with power.”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “In Winter In My Room”. circa 1860.", "“后者住在乡间，屋前有一棵橡树，树里有一窝蛇。\n 他的仆人杀死了那些蛇，但墨兰普斯收集木柴焚烧这些\n 爬虫，并养大了幼蛇。当幼蛇长成后，在他睡觉时\n 分别立在他两肩旁，用舌头清理他的耳朵。\n 他大惊而起，却听懂了头顶飞鸟的声音，\n 并凭借从它们那里学到的东西，向人们预言\n 未来将发生之事。”\n    -伪阿波罗多洛斯，《书库与摘要》，1.9.11。约公元前150年。\n    詹姆斯·乔治·弗雷泽爵士译，1913年。\n\n“一条蛇，斑纹珍奇，\n 巡视我卧房的地板，\n 形貌一如先前那条虫，\n 却缠着力量之环。”\n    -艾米莉·狄金森，《冬日，在我房中》。约1860年。"},
+    {"“Buckshank bold and Elfinstone,\n And more than I can mention here,\n They caused to be built so stout a ship,\n And unto Iceland they would steer.\n\n They launched the ship upon the main,\n Which bellowed like a wrathful bear;\n Down to the bottom the vessel sank,\n A laidly Trold has dragged it there.”\n    -George Borrow, _Lavengro: The Scholar, the Gypsy, the Priest_. 1851.\n########################################\n#\n# Non-unique monsters\n#\n########################################", "“勇武的巴克斯汉克与埃尔芬斯通，\n 还有更多我在此难以尽述之人，\n 他们命人造出一艘如此坚固的船，\n 要驶向冰岛。\n\n 他们把船推入大海，\n 大海如怒熊般咆哮；\n 那船沉入了海底，\n 一只可怕的巨魔将它拖了下去。”\n    -乔治·博罗，《拉文格罗：学者、吉普赛人与牧师》。1851年。\n########################################\n#\n# Non-unique monsters\n#\n########################################"},
+    {"ADDER, n. A species of snake. So called from its habit of adding funeral outlays\nto the other expenses of living.\n    -Ambrose Bierce, _The Devil's Dictionary_, 1906.", "蝰蛇（ADDER），名词。一种蛇。得名于它惯于在生活的其他开支之外，\n再追加一笔丧葬费用。\n    -安布罗斯·比尔斯，《魔鬼词典》，1906年。"},
+    {"““My father, my father, he seizes me fast,\n Full sorely the Erl-King has hurt me at last.”\n\n The father now gallops, with terror half wild,\n He grasps in his arms the poor shuddering child;\n He reaches his courtyard with toil and with dread,\n The child in his arms finds he motionless, dead.”\n    -Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, _Erlkönig_, 1782.\n    trans. Edgar Alfred Bowring, 1853.", "““我的父亲，我的父亲，他紧紧地抓住我，\n 那魔王已经把我伤得好重。”\n\n 父亲如今纵马飞奔，惊骇得近乎疯狂，\n 他把颤抖的可怜孩子紧抱在怀中；\n 他历尽艰辛、满心恐惧地抵达庭院，\n 却发现怀中的孩子已一动不动，死了。”\n    -约翰·沃尔夫冈·冯·歌德，《魔王》，1782年。\n    埃德加·阿尔弗雷德·鲍林译，1853年。"},
+    {"“Alligators commit errors of diet.”\n    -Bennet Bowler, M.D., _Contributions to the Natural History of the\n     Alligator, (Crocodilus Mississipiensis), with a Microscopic Addendum_,\n     p. 17. 1846.", "“短吻鳄会犯下饮食上的错误。”\n    -医学博士贝内特·鲍勒，《对短吻鳄（密西西比鳄）自然史的贡献，\n     附显微镜附录》，\n     第17页。1846年。"},
+    {"“I should have been a pair of ragged claws\n scuttling across the floors of silent seas.”\n    -T.S. Eliot, _The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock_. 1915.", "“我本应是一双破烂的钳爪\n 在寂静的海底匆匆爬行。”\n    -T·S·艾略特，《J·阿尔弗雷德·普鲁弗洛克的情歌》。1915年。"},
+    {"“Be thou like the imperial Basilisk\n Killing thy foe with unapparent wounds!\n Gaze on Oppression, till at that dread risk\n Aghast she pass from the Earth's disk:\n Fear not, but gaze—for freemen mightier grow,\n And slaves more feeble, gazing on their foe:—\n If Hope, and Truth, and Justice may avail,\n Thou shalt be great—All hail!”\n    -Percy Bysshe Shelley, “Posthumous Poems”. 1824.", "“愿你如那至尊的蛇怪，\n 以无形之伤杀死你的仇敌！\n 凝视压迫，直到她冒着那骇人的风险\n 惊惶地从大地的圆盘上消逝：\n 不要畏惧，只管凝视——因为自由人愈凝视仇敌便愈强大，\n 而奴隶愈凝视则愈软弱：——\n 若希望、真理与正义尚能奏效，\n 你必将伟大——万福！”\n    -珀西·比希·雪莱，《遗诗集》。1824年。"},
+    {"“The sun was set; the night came on apace,\n And falling dews bewet around the place;\n The bat takes airy rounds on leathern wings,\n And the hoarse owl his woeful dirges sings.”\n    -John Gay, Shepherd's Week, Wednesday; or, The Dumps.\n\n“Ere the bat hath flown\n His cloister'd flight.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _Macbeth_, III, 2, line 40. 1605.", "“太阳西沉，黑夜迅速降临，\n 落下的露水浸湿了四周；\n 蝙蝠抖着皮翼在空中盘旋，\n 嘶哑的猫头鹰唱着哀伤的挽歌。”\n    ——约翰·盖伊，《牧人周历》，星期三；又名《忧郁》。\n\n“早在蝙蝠飞完\n 它那幽闭的航程之前。”\n    ——威廉·莎士比亚，_麦克白_，第三幕，第二场，第40行。1605年。"},
+    {"“I'm your only friend\n I'm not your only friend\n But I'm a little glowing friend\n But really I'm not actually your friend\n But I am”\n    -They Might Be Giants, “Birdhouse in Your Soul”. 1989.", "“我是你唯一的朋友\n 我并非你唯一的朋友\n 但我是个小发光的朋友\n 但其实我实际上不是你的朋友\n 但我是”\n    ——They Might Be Giants，《Birdhouse in Your Soul》。1989年。"},
+    {"# would prefer a more prose-like passage translating Teoh's Bes Hyang Dney or\n# Werner's Jah-hět of Malaysia, Art and Culture, but until then:\n“The Bès Kěmwar, “maggot spirit”, is one of the many _bès_ or disease spirits\n known to the Jah Hut of Malaysia. A polyvalent spirit, it is depicted as a\n maggot or caterpillar, with variants distinguished by hair, thorns, wings, and\n other details. It eats rice, vegetables, and other crops. It is responsible for\n aches in bones, joints, and muscles. If its caterpillar hair falls into water\n and that water is drunk, it causes coughing and bleeding in the throat. Maggot\n spirits are also known to live in rotten tree trunks and feed in them. Anyone\n who approaches the fallen tree will be bitten in the leg by the spirit, causing\n redness, swelling, and itching. All the toenails fall off but the swelling will\n be gone by two or three months.”\n    - _A Book of Creatures_, 2020.", "# would prefer a more prose-like passage translating Teoh's Bes Hyang Dney or\n# Werner's Jah-hět of Malaysia, Art and Culture, but until then:\n“Bès Kěmwar，‘蛆虫之灵’，是马来西亚雅胡特（Jah Hut）人\n 所知的众多 _bès_ 或疾病之灵之一。它是一种多相之灵，\n 被描绘成蛆虫或毛虫，其变体以毛发、尖刺、翅膀和其他\n 细节相区分。它吃稻米、蔬菜和其他作物。它会导致骨骼、\n 关节和肌肉的疼痛。如果它的毛虫毛发落入水中，而那水\n 被人喝下，就会引起咳嗽和喉咙出血。蛆虫之灵也被认为\n 栖息在腐烂的树干中，并以其为食。任何走近那棵倒下的\n 树的人，都会被这灵咬到腿，引起红肿和瘙痒。\n 脚趾甲会全部脱落，\n 但肿胀会在两三个月内消退。”\n    —— _造物之书_，2020年。"},
+    {"“He thinks every bush a boggart.”\n    -John Ray, _A Compleat Collection of English Proverbs_. 1768.\n\n“A BOGGART intruded himself, upon what pretext or by what authority is unknown,\n into the house of a quiet, inoffensive, and laborious farmer; and, when once\n it had taken possession it disputed the right of domicile with the legal\n mortal tenant, in a very unneighbourly and arbitrary manner. In particular, it\n seemed to have a great aversion to children. As there is no point on which a\n parent feels more acutely than that of the maltreatment of his offspring, the\n feelings of the father and more particularly of his good dame, were daily, ay,\n and nightly, harrowed up by the malice of this malignant and invisible\n boggart.”\n    -C.J.T., _Folk-lore and Legends: English_  1890.", "“他把每丛灌木都当成博格特（boggart）。”\n    ——约翰·雷，_英格兰谚语大全_。1768年。\n\n“一个博格特擅自闯入——\n 以何借口或凭何权柄，无人知晓——住进了一位\n 安静、无害而勤劳的农夫家中；而一旦它\n 占据了那里，便以一种极不友善、蛮横专断的方式，\n 与合法的凡人住户争夺居住权。它似乎尤其\n 厌恶孩子。父母最痛心之事莫过于子女受虐，\n 因此这位父亲，尤其是他那善良妻子的心情，\n 日日夜夜，无论昼夜，都被这恶毒而隐形的\n 博格特的恶意所折磨。”\n    ——C.J.T.，_民间传说与传奇：英格兰篇_  1890年。"},
+    {"“Hello, my baby\n Hello, my honey\n Hello, my ragtime gal\n\n Send me a kiss by wire\n Baby, my hearts on fire\n\n If you refuse me\n Honey, you'll lose me\n Then you'll be left alone\n\n Oh baby, telephone\n And tell me I'm your own.”\n    -Ida Emerson and Joseph E. Howard, “Hello My Baby!”", "“你好，我的宝贝\n 你好，我的甜心\n 你好，我的拉格泰姆姑娘\n\n 用电波送我一个吻\n 宝贝，我的心着了火\n\n 若你拒绝我\n 甜心，你就会失去我\n 然后你将孤身一人\n\n 哦宝贝，打个电话\n 告诉我我属于你。”\n    ——艾达·爱默生与约瑟夫·E. 霍华德，《你好，我的宝贝！》"},
+    {"“And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him in a flame of fire out of the\n midst of a bush: and he looked, and, behold, the bush burned with fire, and\n the bush was not consumed.”\n    -KJV Bible, Exodus 3:2.", "“耶和华的使者从荆棘中的火焰里向他显现：\n 他观看，见荆棘被火烧着，\n 却没有烧毁。”\n    ——钦定版圣经，《出埃及记》3:2。"},
+    {"“Happiness is a butterfly, which when pursued, is always just beyond your\n grasp, but which, if you will sit down quietly, may alight upon you.”\n    -Nathaniel Hawthorne", "“幸福是一只蝴蝶，你追逐它时，它总在你触及不到的地方；\n 但若你安静地坐下，它或许会落在你身上。”\n    ——纳撒尼尔·霍桑"},
+    {"“We'll call him Cacodemon, with his black Gib there, his Succuba, his Devil's\n Seed, his Spawn of Phlegethon, that o’ my Consience was bred o’ the Spume of\n Cocytus.”\n    -John Fletcher, _The Knight of Malta_. 1647.", "“我们且称他为卡科德蒙（Cacodemon），还有他那里黑色的猫精、他的女魅、他的魔鬼之种、\n 他的弗勒革同之裔；凭我的良心，它是从\n 科基托斯的浮沫中滋生出来的。”\n    ——约翰·弗莱彻，_马耳他骑士_。1647年。"},
+    {"“So passed he over into the island, taking with him the two brothers of\n Anaxius; where he found the forsaken knight attired in his own livery, as\n black as sorrow itself could see itself in the blackest glass: his ornaments\n of the same hue, but formd into the figures of ravens which seemed to gape for\n carrion: only his reins were snakes, which finely wrapping themselves one\n within the other, their heads came together to the cheeks and bosses of the\n bit, where they might seem to bite at the horse, and the horse, as he champed\n the bit, to bite at them, and that the white foam was engendered by the\n poisonous fury of the combat. His impresa was a Catoblepta, which so long lies\n dead as the moon (whereto it hath so natural a sympathy) wants her light. The\n word signified, that the moon wanted not the light, but the poor beast wanted\n the moon's light.”\n    -Sir Philip Sidney", "“于是他带着阿纳克西俄斯的两兄弟渡海来到岛上；\n 在那里，他发现那位被遗弃的骑士穿着他自己的号衣，\n 黑得如同悲伤本身能在最黑的镜中照见自己：\n 他的饰物也是同样的色调，却塑成乌鸦的形状，\n 仿佛正张着喙等待腐尸：只有他的缰绳是蛇，\n 它们优美地相互缠绕，蛇头聚到马嚼子的颊侧和凸起处，\n 仿佛要去咬马，而马在咀嚼嚼子时也仿佛要去咬它们，\n 那白色的唾沫便是由这有毒的搏斗狂热所生。\n 他的徽记是一头卡托布莱帕斯（Catoblepta），\n 它与月亮如此天生交感，只要月亮缺了光，\n 它便一直躺倒如死。铭文之意是：月亮并不缺光，\n 缺光的是那可怜的野兽。”\n    ——菲利普·锡德尼爵士"},
+    {"“The glory of Yahweh mounted up from the cherub, and stood over the threshold\n of the house; and the house was filled with the cloud, and the court was full\n of the brightness of Yahweh's glory.\n\n The sound of the wings of the cherubim was heard even to the outer court, as\n the voice of God Almighty when he speaks.”\n    -WEB Bible, Ezekiel 10:4-5", "“耶和华的荣耀从基路伯那里上升，停在殿的门槛以上；\n 殿内充满了云彩，院宇也充满了\n 耶和华荣耀的光辉。\n\n 基路伯翅膀的响声直到外院都能听见，\n 好像全能神说话的声音。”\n    ——世界英文圣经，《以西结书》10:4-5"},
+    {"# would prefer a more prose-like passage translating Aguirre's Mitos De Chile or\n# Coluccio's Diccionario Folklórico Argentino, but until then:\n“In its simplest form it is a bird of the night, an owl (_Strix rufipes_) that\n flies on silent wings during the night to announce illness, death, or some\n other unwelcome event. When its croaking is heard it is advised to throw ash\n into the air and utter a few prayers in the hopes of turning away its evil.\n\n The chonchón is also said to be a _calcu_ or evil sorcerer in disguise. It\n resemble a human head, with oversized wing-like ears that allow it to fly.\n During the night, these sorcerers’ heads detach themselves from their bodies\n and fly around to cause mischief, invisible to most, their ominous _tué, tué,\n tué_ call announcing misfortune. They have the same powers as sorcerers do,\n and have been known to suck the blood of sleepers.”\n    - _A Book of Creatures_, 2015.", "# would prefer a more prose-like passage translating Aguirre's Mitos De Chile or\n# Coluccio's Diccionario Folklórico Argentino, but until then:\n“它最简单的形态是夜间的鸟，一只猫头鹰（_Strix rufipes_），\n 在夜间以无声的翅膀飞行，预示疾病、死亡或其他\n 不受欢迎的事件。当听到它的呱呱叫声时，人们被建议向空中撒灰\n 并念诵几句祷告，以盼能驱走它的邪祟。\n\n 据说琼琼（chonchón）也是一种 _calcu_，即伪装起来的邪恶巫师。它\n 形似一颗人头，长着一对过大的、翼状的耳朵，使它能够飞行。\n 到了夜间，这些巫师的头颅会脱离身体，\n 四处飞行作祟，大多数人看不见它们，它们那不祥的 _tué, tué,\n tué_ 叫声预示着灾祸。它们拥有与巫师相同的力量，\n 并且据说会吸食熟睡者的血。”\n    —— _造物之书_，2015年。"},
+    {"“The Devil, too, sometimes steals human children; it is not infrequent for him\n to carry away infants within the first six weeks after birth, and to\n substitute in their place imps.”\n    -Martin Luther", "“魔鬼有时也会偷走人类的孩子；他常常\n 在婴儿出生后的头六个星期内将其掳走，并\n 换上小魔鬼来顶替他们。”\n    ——马丁·路德"},
+    {"“How doth the little crocodile\n  Improve his shining tail,\n And pour the waters of the Nile\n  On every golden scale!\n\n How cheerfully he seems to grin,\n  How neatly spreads his claws,\n And welcomes little fishes in\n  With gently smiling jaws!”\n    -Lewis Carroll, _Alice's Adventures in Wonderland_, 1865.", "“小小的鳄鱼多么用心\n  修饰它闪亮的尾巴，\n 把尼罗河的水倾倒在\n  每一片金色的鳞甲！\n\n 它笑得多欢畅，\n  爪子张得多灵巧，\n 用它温柔微笑的下颚\n  把小鱼儿们迎进来！”\n    ——刘易斯·卡罗尔，_爱丽丝漫游奇境记_，1865年。"},
+    {"“Good friend for Jesus’ sake forbear,\n To dig the dust enclosed here.\n Blessed be the man that spares these stones,\n And cursed be he that moves my bones.”\n    -Epitaph on William Shakespeare's grave", "“好朋友，看在耶稣的份上，请住手，\n 莫掘这围护在此的尘土。\n 祝福那保全这些石块的人，\n 诅咒那移动我尸骨的人。”\n    ——威廉·莎士比亚墓志铭"},
+    {"“Between these twain the Daevas also chose not aright, for infatuation came\n upon them as they took counsel together, so that they chose the Worst Thought.\n Then they rushed together to Violence, that they might enfeeble the world of\n men.”\n    -the Avesta, Yasna XXX, 6, Ahunavaiti Gatha.\n    trans. Christian Bartholomae, 1951.", "“在这两者之间，众达埃瓦（Daevas）也未能做出正确的选择，因为迷妄\n 在他们共同商议时降临于他们，使他们选择了最恶之念。\n 于是他们一同扑向暴力，妄图削弱\n 人类的世界。”\n    ——《阿维斯陀》，亚斯纳第三十章第6节，阿胡纳瓦伊提·伽塔。\n    克里斯蒂安·巴托洛梅译，1951年。"},
+    {"“Xinchub: At least you're conceding that I've got a soul.\n   Petey: Yes, but it is battered, twisted, and drenched in the blood of the\n          innocent.\n Xinchub: Mmm... yeah.\n   Petey: When you stop being pleased by that we'll know you're making\n          progress.”\n    -Howard Tayler, _Schlock Mercenary_. February 8, 2008.", "“Xinchub：至少你承认了我有灵魂。\n   Petey：是的，但它千疮百孔、扭曲变形，还浸透了无辜者\n          的鲜血。\n Xinchub：嗯……是啊。\n   Petey：等你不再为此感到满意时，我们就知道你有\n          进步了。”\n    ——霍华德·泰勒，_Schlock Mercenary_。2008年2月8日。"},
+    {"“As one example of the meanings inherent in a form, let us examine a particular\n form of vertical stone marker, variously called stele, obelisk, standing-stone,\n and memorial column. These have been historically and commonly used to\n commemorate honored phenomena. So, when a people wish to remember an important\n relationship, event or personage, a location is dedicated to it and often\n marked with an enduring and aspiring vertical form or sets of them. In natural\n language, a vertical stone means: an aspiring connector between us (on earth)\n and an ideal (up there); that we ‘stand up’ with pride about this honored\n phenomenon. The marker is a symbolic inhabitation of the place it occupies. Its\n size and workmanship is a sacrifice of much work and resources to a memory. It\n is a strong suggestion (because we left it to them and it is of durable\n material) that future people also give honor to the memorialized phenomenon.\n When we use this particular physical form of the vertical marker, both its\n historic use as an honorific and its meanings in natural language may well send\n a message that this is an honored place, a place about a ‘good’ both in our\n culture and in the culture of future observers.”\n    -“Expert Judgment on Markers to Deter Inadvertent Human Intrusion into the\n      Waste Isolation Pilot Plant”, Sandia National Laboratories report. 1993", "“为举例说明一种形式所固有的含义，让我们来考察一种特定的\n 竖向石碑——它有不同的名称：石碑、方尖碑、立石\n 和纪念柱。历史上，人们常以它们来\n 纪念受尊崇的事物。因此，当一个民族希望记住一段重要的\n 关系、事件或人物时，就会为此选定一处地点，并常常\n 以持久而高耸的竖向形制或成组的形制加以标记。用自然\n 语言来说，一块竖立的石头意味着：一个连接我们（在地上）\n 与理想（在上方）的向上之纽带；意味着我们为这一受尊崇的\n 事物而自豪地‘挺身而立’。这标记是它所在之地的一种象征性栖居。它的\n 体量与工艺，是为一份记忆而献出的诸多劳力和资源。它\n 强烈地暗示（因为我们把它留给了后人，且它材质耐久）：\n 未来的人们也应当尊崇这被纪念的事物。\n 当我们采用这种特定的竖向标记物质形制时，它\n 作为敬示物的历史用途与其在自然语言中的含义，很可能共同传达\n 出这样一个信息：这是一处受尊崇的地方，一处在我们\n 文化中和未来观者文化中都属于‘善’的地方。”\n    ——《关于阻止无意间人类闯入废物隔离试验厂\n      （Waste Isolation Pilot Plant）的标记的专家判断》，桑迪亚国家实验室报告。1993年"},
+    {"“Standing over Hugo, and plucking at his throat, there stood a foul thing, a\n great, black beast, shaped like a hound, yet larger than any hound that ever\n mortal eye has rested upon. And even as they looked the thing tore the throat\n out of Hugo Baskerville...”\n    -Arthur Conan Doyle, _The Hound of the Baskervilles_. 1902.", "“站在雨果身上、撕扯着他喉咙的，是一头丑恶的东西，\n 一头巨大的黑色野兽，形似猎犬，却比任何凡人眼睛见过的猎犬都要大。\n 就在众人注视之际，那东西将雨果·巴斯克维尔的喉咙\n 撕了出来……”\n    ——阿瑟·柯南·道尔，_巴斯克维尔的猎犬_。1902年。"},
+    {"“How undisturbed, the sleep of the foolish.”\n    -Philip K. Dick", "“愚人的睡眠，何其安宁。”\n    ——菲利普·K·迪克"},
+    {" • Roy Quixote: Wait, what's my beef with clean energy again?\n — Durkon Pansa: Dunno, but if'n ye prefer, I know a grove o'peach trees tha've\n   been gettin' fresh wit tha locals.\n    -Rich Burlew, “Haleo and Julean”", " • 罗伊·吉诃德：等等，我到底为什么又反对清洁能源来着？\n — 杜尔孔·潘萨：不晓得，不过要是你乐意，我知道一片桃树林，那儿\n   可有人正跟当地人打得火热呢。\n    ——里奇·伯利，《Haleo and Julean》"},
+    {"“When the hoopoe returned to Solomon (he told him the news), and he responded\n (to Sheba's people): “Are you giving me money? What GOD has given me is far\n better than what He has given you. You are the ones to rejoice in such gifts.”\n (To the hoopoe, he said), “Go back to them (and let them know that) we will\n come to them with forces they cannot imagine. We will evict them, humiliated\n and debased.” He said, “O you elders, which of you can bring me her mansion,\n before they arrive here as submitters?” One afrit from the jinns said, “I can\n bring it to you before you stand up. I am powerful enough to do this.”\n    -The Quran, Sura 27 Al-Naml", "“当戴胜鸟回到所罗门那里（向他报告了消息），他回应\n （示巴的民众）说：“你们是要给我钱财吗？真主赐予我的，远\n 胜过他赐予你们的。你们才是该为这样的礼物而高兴的人。”\n （他对戴胜鸟说：）“回到他们那里去（让他们知道），我们将\n 带着他们无法想象的军队前来。我们将把他们驱逐出去，令他们蒙羞\n 受辱。”他说：“诸位长老啊，你们谁能把她的宫殿带来给我，\n 趁他们还未到这里来归顺？”一个镇尼中的伊夫利特说：“我能在\n 你站起之前把它带给你。我有足够的力量做这件事。”\n    ——《古兰经》，第27章《蚂蚁》"},
+    {"“No sooner had sleep caught him, dissolving all his grief\n as mists of refreshing slumber poured around him there—\n his powerful frame was bone-weary from charging Hector\n straight and hard to the walls of windswept Troy—\n than the ghost of stricken Patroclus drifted up...\n He was like the man to the life, every feature,\n the same tall build and the fine eyes and voice\n and the very robes that used to clothe his body.\n Hovering at his head the phantom rose and spoke:\n ‘Sleeping, Achilles? You’ve forgotten me, my friend.\n You never neglected me in life, only now in death.\n Bury me, quickly—let me pass the Gates of Hades.\n They hold me off at a distance, all the souls,\n the shades of the burnt-out, breathless dead,\n never to let me cross the river, mingle with them...\n They leave me to wander up and down, abandoned, lost\n at the House of Death with the all-embracing gates.’”\n    -Homer, _The Iliad_, XXIII, 72-88.\n     trans. Robert Fagles, 1990.", "“睡意刚一攫住他，将他的一切悲伤化解，\n 清新的沉睡之雾在他四周倾泻而下——\n 他那强健的身躯因径直猛攻赫克托尔、\n 冲向多风的特洛伊城墙而骨疲力竭——\n 这时，被击倒的帕特罗克洛斯的鬼魂飘然升起……\n 他活像那个人，每一个特征，\n 同样的高大身形、俊美的眼睛与声音，\n 还有曾经裹住他躯体的那身衣袍。\n 那幽灵悬在他头顶，起身说道：\n ‘睡了，阿喀琉斯？你把我的朋友给忘了。\n 生前你从不曾把我忽视，如今我死了，你却忘了。\n 快把我埋葬——让我渡过冥界之门。\n 那些灵魂远远地拦住我，\n 那些焚尽、断气的死者的幽影，\n 从不让我渡河，与他们相会……\n 他们任我在外游荡，无依无靠，迷失\n 在有着包罗万象之门的冥府。’”\n    ——荷马，_伊利亚特_，第二十三卷，72-88行。\n     罗伯特·法格尔斯译，1990年。"},
+    {"“I sing the Body electric”\n    -Walt Whitman, “I Sing the Body Electric”, _Leaves of Grass_. 1867.", "“我歌唱带电的肉体”\n    ——沃尔特·惠特曼，《我歌唱带电的肉体》，_草叶集_。1867年。"},
+    {"“And the King went to where the blind men were, and drawing near said to them:\n ‘Do you now know what an elephant is like?’\n ‘Assuredly, Lord: we now know what an elephant is like.’\n ‘Tell me then, O blind men, what an elephant is like.’\n And those blind men, O Bhikkhus, who had felt the head of the elephant, said:\n ‘An elephant, Sir, is like a large round jar.\n Those who had felt its ears, said: 'it is like a winnowing basket.’\n Those who had felt its tusks, said: ‘it is like a plough-share.’\n Those who had felt its trunk, said: ‘it is like a plough.’\n Those who had felt its body, said: ‘it is like a granary.’\n Those who had felt its feet, said: ‘it is like a pillar.’\n Those who had felt its back, said: ‘it is like a mortar.’\n Those who had felt its tail, said: ‘it is a like a pestle.’\n Those who had felt the tuft of its tail, said: ‘it is like a broom.’\n And they all fought amongst themselves with their fists, declaring, ‘such is\n an elephant, such is not elephant, an elephant is not like that, it is like\n this.’”\n And the King, O Bhikkhus, was highly delighted.\n    -_Udāna_, VI “Jaccandhavagga”. ca. 5th cent. B.C.\n     trans. Dawsonne Melanchthon Strong, 1902.", "“国王走到盲人们那里，走近后对他们说：\n ‘你们现在可知道大象是什么样子了？’\n ‘确实如此，大王：我们现在知道大象是什么样子了。’\n ‘那么，盲人们，告诉我大象是什么样子。’\n 于是，诸位比丘啊，那些摸过大象头的盲人说：\n ‘大王，大象像一个圆圆的大罐子。’\n 那些摸过它耳朵的人说：‘它像一个簸箕。’\n 那些摸过它象牙的人说：‘它像一张犁头。’\n 那些摸过它鼻子的人说：‘它像一张犁。’\n 那些摸过它身体的人说：‘它像一座谷仓。’\n 那些摸过它腿的人说：‘它像一根柱子。’\n 那些摸过它背的人说：‘它像一只臼。’\n 那些摸过它尾巴的人说：‘它像一只杵。’\n 那些摸过它尾端毛丛的人说：‘它像一把扫帚。’\n 于是他们全都挥拳相互厮打起来，宣称：‘大象是这样，\n 大象不是那样，大象不像那样，而是像\n 这样。’”\n 而国王，诸位比丘啊，大为欢喜。\n    ——_Udāna_，第六品“Jaccandhavagga”。约公元前5世纪。\n     道森·梅兰克顿·斯特朗译，1902年。"},
+    {"“Portents had occurred indicating [Titus Flavius Vespasianus'] approaching end,\n such as the comet which was visible for a long time and the opening of the\n mausoleum of Augustus of its own accord. When his physicians chided him for\n continuing his usual course of living during his illness and attending to all\n the duties that belonged to his office, he answered: ‘The emperor ought to die\n on his feet.’”\n    -Cassius Dio, _Roman History_, LXVI, xvii, 2. 222 A.D.\n     trans. Earnest Cary, 1925.", "“曾出现预示[提图斯·弗拉维乌斯·韦斯帕芗]大限将至的征兆，\n 例如长时间可见的彗星，以及奥古斯都陵墓\n 自行开启。当他的医生们责备他在病中\n 仍照常生活、并处理一切\n 属于其职位的政务时，他答道：‘皇帝应当\n 站着死去。’”\n    ——卡西乌斯·狄奥，_罗马史_，第六十六章，第17节，第2段。公元222年。\n     欧内斯特·凯里译，1925年。"},
+    {"“Sea horses floundering in the slimy mud,\n Tossed up their heads, and dashed the ooze about them.”\n    -John Dryden. _All For Love_, I, i, 15-17. 1678.", "“海马在黏滑的泥中挣扎，\n 扬起头来，把淤泥溅得四处都是。”\n    ——约翰·德莱顿。_一切为了爱_，第一幕，第一场，15-17行。1678年。"},
+    {"“SEE! warp is stretched\n For warrior’s fall;\n Lo, weft in loom,\n ’Tis wet with blood;\n Now, fight foreboding,\n ’Neath friends’ swift fingers\n Our gray woof waxeth\n With war’s alarms,\n Our warp blood-red,\n Our weft corse-blue.\n\n This woof is y-woven\n With entrails of men;\n This warp is hard weighted\n With heads of the slain;\n Spears blood-besprinkled\n For spindles we use,\n Our loom iron-bound,\n And arrows our reels;\n With swords for our shuttles\n This war-woof we work;\n So weave we, weird sisters,\n Our war-winning woof.\n\n Now war-winner walketh\n To weave in her turn,\n Now Sword-swinger steppeth,\n Now Swift-stroke, now Storm;\n When they speed the shuttle\n How spear-heads shall flash!\n Shields crash, and helm-gnawer\n On harness bite hard!”\n    -_The Story of Burnt Njal_, trans. George Dasent. 1861", "“看哪！经线已经绷紧\n 为战士的陨落；\n 瞧，织机上的纬线，\n 已被鲜血浸透；\n 如今，预感着战斗，\n 在朋友迅捷的指下\n 我们灰色的织物生长，\n 伴随着战争的警讯，\n 我们的经线血红，\n 我们的纬线尸青。\n\n 这织物是用人的\n 肠子织成；\n 这经线被沉沉\n 压上死者的头颅；\n 染血的矛\n 用作我们的纺锤，\n 我们铁箍的织机，\n 箭是我们的卷线筒；\n 以剑为梭\n 我们织出这战争织物；\n 就这样，命运姐妹，我们织着\n 我们那赢得战争的织物。\n\n 如今，胜利者走着\n 轮到她来织；\n 如今，挥剑者迈步，\n 如今是迅击，如今是风暴；\n 当她们加快梭子\n 矛头将如何闪耀！\n 盾牌相撞，啃啮头盔者\n 狠狠咬住铠甲！”\n    ——_焚毁的尼雅尔萨迦_，乔治·达森特译。1861年"},
+    {"“But he had not been long in his hiding-hole, before the awful Ettin came in;\n and no sooner was he in, than he was heard crying:\n ‘Snouk but and snouk ben,\n  I find the smell of an earthly man,\n  Be he living, or be he dead,\n  His heart this night shall kitchen my bread.’”\n    -Joseph Jacobs, _The Red Ettin_", "“可他躲进藏身洞没多久，可怕的埃丁（Ettin）就进来了；\n 它刚进来，就听它喊道：\n ‘这儿嗅嗅，那儿闻闻，\n  我闻到凡人的气味，\n  不管他是死是活，\n  今晚他的心脏就要给我的面包添味。’”\n    ——约瑟夫·雅各布斯，_红埃丁_"},
+    {"“Behold the Lord High Executioner\n A personage of noble rank and title —\n A dignified and potent officer\n Whose functions are particularly vital!\n Defer, defer\n To the Lord High Executioner!\n Defer, defer\n To the noble Lord, to the noble Lord\n To the Lord High Executioner!”\n    -Gilbert and Sullivan, “Behold the Lord High Executioner”,\n      _The Mikado_. 1885", "“看哪，至尊刽子手大人\n 一位身居高贵爵位与头衔的人物——\n 一位尊严而权重的官员\n 其职能尤为至关重要！\n 礼敬，礼敬\n 向至尊刽子手大人！\n 礼敬，礼敬\n 向尊贵的大人，向尊贵的大人\n 向至尊刽子手大人！”\n    ——吉尔伯特与沙利文，《看哪，至尊刽子手大人》，\n      _日本天皇_。1885年"},
+    {"“All the infections that sun sucks up\n From bogs, fens, flats, on Prosper fall and make him\n By inch-meal a disease! His spirits hear me\n And yet I needs must curse.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tempest_, II, 2. 1611.", "“太阳从沼泽、泥塘、洼地里吸起的\n 一切瘴气，都落到普洛斯彼罗身上，让他\n 一寸一寸地变成病夫！他的精灵听得见我，\n 可我还是忍不住要咒骂。”\n    ——威廉·莎士比亚，_暴风雨_，第二幕，第二场。1611年。"},
+    {"“The planet brought forth scintillating jewelled scuttling crabs, which the\n Vogons ate, smashing their shells with iron mallets; tall aspiring trees with\n breathtaking slenderness and colour which the Vogons cut down and burned the\n crab meat with; elegant gazellelike creatures with silken coats and dewy eyes\n which the Vogons would catch and sit on.”\n    -Douglas Adams, _The Hitch-hiker's Guide to the Galaxy_. 1979.", "“行星孕育出闪烁的、镶满珠宝般横行的螃蟹，沃贡人\n 把它们吃掉，用铁锤砸碎它们的壳；还有高耸挺拔的树木，\n 纤细与色彩令人惊叹，沃贡人将它们砍倒，用来\n 烧蟹肉；还有优雅如瞪羚的生灵，身披丝般毛皮，眼眸湿润，\n 沃贡人会抓住它们并坐在上面。”\n    -道格拉斯·亚当斯，《银河系漫游指南》。1979年。"},
+    {"“Solamen miseris socios habuisse doloris.”\n    -Christopher Marlowe, _Doctor Faustus_. 1604", "“同病相怜，是受苦者的慰藉。”\n    -克里斯托弗·马洛，《浮士德博士》。1604年"},
+    {"“From this story it is evident that in former times the jellyfish once had a\nshell and bones something like a tortoise, but, ever since the Dragon King's\nsentence was carried out on the ancestor of the jelly fishes, his descendants\nhave all been soft and boneless just as you see them to-day thrown up by the\nwaves high upon the shores of Japan.”\n    -“The Jellyfish and the Monkey”, adapted by Yei Theodora Ozaki, _Young\n     Folk's Treasury_, vol. II. 1909.", "“从这个故事可以明显看出，从前水母曾拥有\n类似乌龟的壳与骨骼，但自从龙王\n对水母的祖先施以判决以来，它的后代\n全都变得柔软无骨，正如你今日所见，被\n海浪高高抛上日本的海岸。”\n    -《水母与猴子》，Yei Theodora Ozaki 改编，《少年\n     民间故事宝库》，第二卷。1909年"},
+    {"“Always in focus\n You can't feel my stare.\n I zoom into you\n You don't know I'm there.”\n    -Judas Priest, “Electric Eye”. 1982.", "“永远清晰聚焦\n 你察觉不到我的凝视。\n 我拉近镜头对准你\n 你不知道我在那里。”\n    -犹大圣徒乐队，《电眼》。1982年"},
+    {"“In the desert\n I saw a creature, naked, bestial,\n Who, squatting upon the ground,\n Held his heart in his hands,\n And ate of it.\n I said, ‘Is it good, friend?’\n ‘It is bitter — bitter’, he answered,\n ‘But I like it\n Because it is bitter,\n And because it is my heart.’”\n    -Stephen Crane, _The Black Riders and Other Lines_. 1895.", "“在沙漠中\n 我看见一个生灵，赤裸，如兽，\n 他蹲伏在地上，\n 双手捧着自己的心，\n 吃着它。\n 我说：‘好吃吗，朋友？’\n ‘它很苦——很苦’，他答道，\n ‘但我喜欢它\n 因为它苦，\n 还因为它是我的心。’”\n    -斯蒂芬·克莱恩，《黑骑者及其他诗行》。1895年"},
+    {"“El ojo que ves\n no es ojo porque tú lo veas,\n es ojo porque te ve.”\n    -Antonio Machado, Campos de Castilla. 1912-1917.", "“你所见的眼睛\n 并非因你看见它才是眼睛，\n 它之所以是眼睛，是因为它看见了你。”\n    -安东尼奥·马查多，《卡斯蒂利亚的田野》。1912-1917年"},
+    {"“Then he descended softly and beckoned to Nuth. But the gnoles had watched him\n through knavish holes that they bore in trunks of the trees, and the unearthly\n silence gave way, as it were with a grace, to the rapid screams of Tonker as\n they picked him up from behind — screams that came faster and faster until\n they were incoherent. And where they took him it is not good to ask, and what\n they did with him I shall not say.”\n    -Lord Dunsany, “How Nuth Would Have Practised His Art Upon the Gnoles”.\n1912.", "“然后他悄然降下，向努斯招手。但那些诺尔一直注视着他，\n 透过它们在树干上钻出的狡诈孔洞，那非人间的\n 寂静优雅地让位于汤克被从背后抓起时\n 发出的急促尖叫——尖叫越来越快，直到\n 语无伦次。他们把他带去了哪里，不便追问；\n 他们对他做了什么，我也不会说。”\n    -邓萨尼勋爵，《努斯如何对诺尔施展他的技艺》。\n1912年"},
+    {"“Swish, smack! Whip crack!\n Batter and beat! Yammer and bleat!\n Work, work! Nor dare to shirk,\n While Goblins quaff, and Goblins laugh,\n Round and round far underground\n Below, my lad!”\n    -J.R.R. Tolkien, _The Hobbit_", "“嗖，啪！鞭声噼啪！\n 猛敲又猛打！号叫又哀鸣！\n 干活，干活！休想偷懒，\n 当哥布林畅饮，当哥布林大笑，\n 一圈又一圈，在地底深处，\n 在下面，我的伙计！”\n    -J.R.R. 托尔金，《霍比特人》"},
+    {"“You do not come dramatically, with dragons\n That rear up with my life between their paws\n And dash me butchered down beside the wagons”\n    -Philip Larkin, “To Failure”. 1949.", "“你并非戏剧性地降临，带着巨龙\n 它们直立起身，爪间夹着我的性命\n 把我砍碎摔在货车旁”\n    -菲利普·拉金，《致失败》。1949年"},
+    {"“No coveting nor envy burns\n   In thy bright golden eye,\n That calm and innocently turns\n   On all below the sky.”\n    -Hannah Flagg Gould, _The Youth's Coronal_", "“没有贪欲，没有嫉妒燃烧\n   在你明亮的金色眼眸中，\n 它平静而天真地转视\n   天空之下的一切。”\n    -汉娜·弗拉格·古尔德，《青年的花冠》"},
+    {"“‘Listen Reb _Golem_,’ the old man said, wagging his finger. ‘Pay attention to\n  what I say—you understand?’\n ‘Understand...’\n ‘If you want to stay here, you got to do like Mr. Gumbeiner says.’\n ‘Do-like-Mr.-Gumbeiner-says...’\n ‘_That's_ the way I like to hear a golem talk. Malka, give here the mirror\n  from the pocketbook. Look, you see your face? You see on the forehead, what's\n  written? If you don't do like Mr. Gumbeiner says, he'll wipe out what's\n  written and you'll be no more alive.’\n ‘No-more-alive...’\n ‘_That's_ right. Now, listen. Under the porch you'll find a lawnmower. Take\n  it. And cut the lawn. Then come back. Go.’”\n    -Avram Davidson, “The Golem”, 1955.", "“‘听着，Reb _魔像_，’老人摇晃着手指说。‘注意听\n  我说的话——你明白吗？’\n ‘明白……’\n ‘如果你想留在这里，就得按古姆拜纳先生说的做。’\n ‘按-古姆拜纳-先生-说的-做……’\n ‘_这_才是我喜欢听魔像说话的样子。玛尔卡，把镜子\n  从手提包里拿来。看，你看见自己的脸了吗？你看见额头上写着\n  什么了吗？如果你不按古姆拜纳先生说的做，他就会抹掉那\n  写着的东西，你就不再活着了。’\n ‘不再-活着……’\n ‘_这就_对了。现在，听着。门廊下面你能找到一台割草机。拿\n  上它。去剪草坪。然后回来。去吧。’”\n    -阿夫拉姆·戴维森，《魔像》，1955年"},
+    {"“The Second Coming! Hardly are those words out\n When a vast image out of Spiritus Mundi\n Troubles my sight: somewhere in sands of the desert\n A shape with lion body and the head of a man,\n A gaze blank and pitiless as the sun,\n Is moving its slow thighs, while all about it\n Reel shadows of the indignant desert birds.”\n    -W. B. Yeats, \"The Second Coming\". 1920.", "“二次降临！话音未落\n 一个来自世界之魂的巨大影像\n 便扰乱我的视线：沙漠某处\n 一具狮身人首的形体，\n 目光空茫无情如太阳，\n 正缓缓挪动它的大腿，而四周\n 尽是愤怒沙漠之鸟纷乱的阴影。”\n    -W. B. 叶芝，《二次降临》。1920年"},
+    {"“Bird-bodied, girl-faced things they are; abominable their droppings, their\n hands are talons, their faces haggard with hunger insatiable.”\n    -Virgil, Aeneid 3\n\n“And Phineus had scarcely taken the first morsel up when, with as little\n warning as a whirlwind or a lightning flash, they dropped from the clouds\n proclaiming their desire for food with raucous cries. The young lords saw them\n coming and raised the alarm. Yet they had hardly done so before the Harpyiai\n had devoured the whole meal and were on the wing once more, far out at sea.\n All they left was an intolerable stench.”\n    -Apollonius Rhodius, Argonautica 2. 179 — 434", "“它们鸟身女面；排泄物令人作呕，\n 双手是利爪，面容因无法满足的饥饿而憔悴。”\n    -维吉尔，《埃涅阿斯纪》卷三\n\n“菲纽斯刚拿起第一口食物，它们便如旋风或闪电般毫无\n 预警地从云中落下，用沙哑的叫声宣告对食物的渴望。年轻的领主们看见它们\n 飞来，立刻发出警报。然而他们还未行动，\n 哈耳庇厄就已吞尽整餐，\n 再次飞向远海。\n 它们只留下一股难以忍受的恶臭。”\n    -阿波罗尼乌斯·罗得乌斯，《阿尔戈船英雄纪》2. 179 — 434"},
+    {"“About her middle round\n A cry of Hell-hounds never-ceasing barked\n With wide Cerberean mouths full loud, and rung\n A hideous peal; yet, when they list, would creep,\n If aught disturbed their noise, into her womb,\n And kennel there; yet there still barked and howled\n Within unseen.”\n    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book II, 1667.", "“环绕她的腰际\n 一群地狱猎犬不断吠叫\n 张开刻耳柏洛斯般的巨口，声震四野，发出\n 一阵可怖的轰鸣；然而当它们乐意时，会爬进，\n 若有什么扰乱了它们的喧闹，便钻入她的子宫，\n 在那里栖息；然而它们仍在其中看不见地\n 吠叫哀嚎。”\n    -约翰·弥尔顿，《失乐园》，卷二，1667年"},
+    {"“Ok, let's review. It's up to the fair young maiden to rescue the dragon from\n the fire breathing knights in shining armour.”\n    -Exiern", "“好，我们来捋一捋。该由美丽的年轻少女去从\n 喷火的闪亮盔甲骑士手中拯救巨龙。”\n    -Exiern"},
+    {"“A foolish consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds, adored by little\n statesmen and philosophers and divines.”\n    -Ralph Waldo Emerson, _Essays: First Series_, Essay II: Self-Reliance.\n     1841.", "“愚蠢的始终如一，是渺小心灵的鬼怪，为渺小的\n 政客、哲学家和神学家所钟爱。”\n    -拉尔夫·沃尔多·爱默生，《随笔集：第一辑》，第二篇：论自立。\n     1841年"},
+    {"“Fern came slowly down the stairs. Her eyes were red from crying. As she\n approached her chair, the carton wobbled, and there was a scratching noise.\n Fern looked at her father. Then she lifted the lid of the carton. There,\n inside, looking up at her, was the newborn pig. It was a white one. The\n morning light shone through its ears, turning them pink. “He's yours,” said\n Mr. Arable.  “Saved from an untimely death. And may the good Lord forgive me\n for this foolishness.”\n    -E.B. White, _Charlotte's Web_", "“芬慢慢走下楼梯。她哭得眼睛通红。当\n 她走近椅子时，纸箱晃了晃，传来一阵抓挠声。\n 芬看着她的父亲。然后她掀开纸箱的盖子。里面，\n 一只刚出生的小猪正抬头看着她。那是一头白色的小猪。\n 晨光透过它的耳朵，把耳朵照成粉红色。“它是你的了，”\n 阿拉布尔先生说。  “它免于夭折。愿仁慈的上帝宽恕我\n 这桩蠢事。”\n    -E.B. 怀特，《夏洛的网》"},
+    {"“A traveller, by the faithful hound, Half-buried in the snow was found, Still\n grasping in his hand of ice That banner with the strange device, Excelsior!”\n    -Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, “Excelsior”", "“一位旅人，被忠犬发现，半埋在雪中，\n 冰僵的手中仍紧握着那面铭着奇文的旗帜——向上！”\n    -亨利·沃兹沃斯·朗费罗，《向上》"},
+    {"“Do you know\n Do I know\n What's this thing called ‘man’?\n God only knows what a man is!\n I only know his price.”\n    -Bertolt Brecht, “The Measures Taken”. 1930.", "“你知道吗\n 我知道吗\n 这叫作‘人’的东西是什么？\n 只有上帝知道人是什么！\n 我只知道他的价钱。”\n    -贝托尔特·布莱希特，《措施》。1930年"},
+    {"“Once on a time when Brahmadatta was reigning in Benares, the Bodhisatta was\n born an iguana. When he grew up he dwelt in a big burrow in the river bank\n with a following of many hundreds of other iguanas. Now the Bodhisatta had a\n son, a young iguana, who was great friends with a chameleon, whom he used to\n clip and embrace. This intimacy being reported to the iguana king, he sent for\n his young son and said that such friendship was misplaced, for chameleons were\n low creatures, and that if the intimacy was persisted in, calamity would\n befall the whole of the tribe of iguanas. And he enjoined his son to have no\n more to do with the chameleon. But the son continued in his intimacy.”\n    -_Khuddaka Nikāya_, Jātaka 141 “Godha-jātaka”. ca. 4th cent. B.C.\n     trans. Robert Chalmers, 1895.", "“从前，梵授王在波罗奈治国时，菩萨转生为一只鬣蜥。它长大后，\n 居于河岸的一个大洞穴中，追随它的还有数百只其他鬣蜥。那时\n 菩萨有一个儿子，一只年轻的鬣蜥，它和一只变色龙是好友，\n 常与它相拥亲昵。这份亲密被禀报给鬣蜥王，他便召来\n 年轻的儿子，说这样的友谊是不合适的，因为变色龙\n 是低贱的生灵，若再继续这份亲密，灾祸将\n 降临整个鬣蜥族。他吩咐儿子不要\n 再与那只变色龙来往。但儿子仍继续\n 着这份亲密。”\n    -《小部经》，本生经第141《鬣蜥本生》。约公元前4世纪。\n     罗伯特·查尔默斯译，1895年"},
+    {"“Always ready to take advantage of every favourable opportunity, the Jackal is\n a sad parasite, and hangs on the skirts of the larger carnivora as they roam\n the country for prey, in the hope of securing some share of the creatures\n which they destroy or wound.”\n    -John George Wood, _The Illustrated Natural History: Mammalia_. 1865.", "“胡狼总是随时准备利用每一个有利时机，是个可悲的\n 寄生虫，当较大的食肉兽在原野游荡觅食时，它便尾随其后，\n 指望分得一份它们杀死或弄伤的\n 猎物。”\n    -约翰·乔治·伍德，《图说自然史：哺乳动物》。1865年"},
+    {"“Beware of the Blob!\n It creeps\n And leaps\n and glides and slides\n Across the floor\n Right through the door\n And all around the wall,\n A splotch, a blotch...”\n    -Burt Bacharach and Mack David, “Beware of the Blob”. 1958.", "“当心那团黏怪！\n 它爬行\n 又跳跃\n 又滑又溜\n 穿过地板\n 径直穿门\n 绕着墙壁，\n 一滩污渍，一块斑痕……”\n    -伯特·巴卡拉克与马克·大卫，《当心那团黏怪》。1958年"},
+    {"“All the world loves a clown.”\n    -Cole Porter, “Be a Clown”. 1948.", "“全世界都爱小丑。”\n    -科尔·波特，《当个小丑》。1948年"},
+    {"“Kobolds are harmless.”\n    -Bearand the Bold, epitaph (Magic: the Gathering)", "“狗头人是无害的。”\n    -Bearand the Bold, 墓志铭（万智牌）"},
+    {"“The three of us were sitting ashen faced as if we had just witnessed a foul\n and malignant murder. At least if we had been watching a murder the murderer\n wouldn't have been looking us impassively in the eye as he did it. Maybe it\n was the feeling of cold unflinching arrogance that so disturbed us. But\n whatever malign emotions we tried to pin on to the lizard, we knew that they\n weren't the lizard's emotions at all, only ours. The lizard was simply going\n about its lizardly business in a simple, straightforward lizardly way. It\n didn't know anything about the horror, the guilt, the shame, the ugliness that\n we, uniquely guilty and ashamed animals, were trying to foist on it. So we got\n it all straight back at us, as if reflected in the mirror of its single\n unwavering and disinterested eye.”\n    -Douglas Adams, “Last Chance to See”. 1990.", "“我们三个面色苍白地坐着，仿佛刚目睹了一场邪恶\n 而恶毒的谋杀。至少如果我们是在看一场谋杀，凶手\n 不会在行凶时无动于衷地直视我们的眼睛。也许\n 正是那种冷酷而无畏的傲慢感如此令我们不安。但\n 无论我们试图把怎样邪恶的情绪加在这只蜥蜴身上，我们都清楚\n 那根本不是蜥蜴的情绪，只是我们自己的。蜥蜴只是\n 以简单直接、符合蜥蜴本性的方式，做着蜥蜴该做的事。它\n 对恐怖、罪疚、羞耻、丑陋一无所知，而\n 我们这些唯独有罪而羞愧的动物，却想把这些强加给它。于是这一切\n 直直地回落到我们身上，仿佛映在它那只单一\n 坚定而无动于衷的眼睛的镜中。”\n    -道格拉斯·亚当斯，《最后一眼》。1990年"},
+    {"“... Kraken, also called the Crab-fish, which [according to the pilots of\n Norway] is not that huge, for heads and tails counted, he is no larger than\n our Öland is wide [i.e. less than 16 km] ... He stays at the sea floor,\n constantly surrounded by innumerable small fishes, who serve as his food and\n are fed by him in return: for his meal, if I remember correctly what E.\n Pontoppidan writes, lasts no longer than three months, and another three are\n then needed to digest it. His excrements nurture in the following an army of\n lesser fish, and for this reason, fishermen plumb after his resting place ...\n Gradually, Kraken ascends to the surface, and when he is at ten to twelve\n fathoms, the boats had better move out of his vicinity, as he will shortly\n thereafter burst up, like a floating island, spurting water from his dreadful\n nostrils and making ring waves around him, which can reach many miles. Could\n one doubt that this is the Leviathan of Job?”\n    -Jacob Wallenberg, “Min son på galejan”. 1781.", "“……克拉肯，又称蟹鱼，[据挪威的领航员说]\n 它并不那么巨大，因为连头带尾算起来，他也不过\n 与我们厄兰岛的宽度相当[即不足16公里]……他待在海底，\n 始终被无数小鱼环绕，这些小鱼既是他的食物，\n 又反过来被他喂养：据我\n 记得E. 蓬托皮丹所写，他的一餐\n 不超过三个月，之后还需要三个月\n 来消化。他的排泄物随后滋养着一大群\n 较小的鱼，正因如此，渔夫们会在他歇息之处垂线探深……\n 渐渐地，克拉肯升上水面，当他处于十到十二\n 英寻深处时，船只最好离开他的附近，因为他随后\n 便会像一座浮岛般猛然升起，从他可怕的鼻孔中喷出水柱，在身边掀起一圈圈涟漪，可远达数英里。\n 难道有人会怀疑这就是《约伯记》中的利维坦吗？”\n    -雅各布·瓦伦贝里，《Min son på galejan》。1781年"},
+    {"“Freilich verbürgt uns keine Silbe die Existenz von solcherlei Thieren, wenn\n wir uns den Drachen oder Lindwurm als ein Ungeheuer vorstellen, dessen langer\n Hals in einen Adler-, Löwen- oder Delphinenkopf endigt; das auf dem breiten\n Rücken Greifs- oder Nachisittige trägt; und am vielfach\n gerollten Schweif einen Stachel mit Widerhaken hat; Feuer speit; sich in\n Mädchen verliebt und diese entführt; bald diese bald jene Gestalt annimmt; auf\n sauer erworbenen Schatzen ruht — kurz, als ein Ungeheuer, das alle\n Eigenschaften besitzt, welche die Fabel ihm andichtet; dann wäre es Wahnsinn,\n an Drachen und Lindwürmer glauben zu wollen. Nehmen wir aber dafür bloß ein\n furchtbares Ungeheuer überhaupt, welches nun aus unserem Welttheile vertilgt\n ist, so hat der Glaube daran nichts Lächerliches.”\n    -Leopold Ziegelhauſer, _Schattenbilder der Vorzeit: Ein Kranz von\n     Geschichten, Sagen, Legenden, Märchen, Skizzen und Heldenmahlen, Aus allen\n     Gegenden Deutschlands und des österreichischen Kaiserstaates_. 1844.", "“诚然，如果我们把龙或林德虫想象成一个怪物——它长长的\n 脖子末端是鹰、狮或海豚的头；宽阔的\n 背上驮着狮鹫或其他生物的翅膀；在层层\n 盘绕的尾巴上长着带倒钩的毒刺；口吐火焰；\n 爱上少女并将她们掳走；时而化为此形，时而化为彼形；\n 盘踞在辛苦得来的财宝上——一言以蔽之，一个拥有\n 寓言所赋予它的一切特性的怪物；那么，如果我们如此想象，\n 便没有任何一个字能向我们保证此类动物的存在，相信龙\n 与林德虫便是愚蠢的。但如果我们只是把它设想为一种\n 可怕的怪物，一种如今已从我们这片大陆上灭绝的怪物，\n 那么对它的信仰便毫无可笑之处。”\n    -利奥波德·齐格尔豪泽，《往昔的剪影：来自\n     德国及奥地利帝国各地的故事、传说、传奇、童话、素描与英雄画像集》。\n     1844年"},
+    {"“She walks in the twilight, her steps make no sound,\n  Her feet leave no tracks on the dew-covered ground.\n Her hand gently beckons, she whispers your name—\n  But those who go with her are never the same.”\n    -Magic: the Gathering", "“她在暮色中行走，脚步悄然无声，\n  她的双足在沾露的地上不留痕迹。\n 她的手轻柔招引，她低唤你的名字——\n  但随她而去的人，再也不会是原来的自己。”\n    -万智牌"},
+    {"“Ctesias writeth, that in Aethiopia likewise there is a beast which he calleth\n Mantichora, having three rankes of teeth, which when they meet togither are\n let in one within another like the teeth of combes: with the face and eares of\n a man, with red eyes; of colour sanguine, bodied like a lyon, and having a\n taile armed with a sting like a scorpion: his voice resembleth the noise of a\n flute and trumpet sounded together: very swift he is, and mans flesh of all\n others hee most desireth.”\n    -Pliny the Elder, _Natural History_, Book 8, Chapter XXI", "“克特西亚斯写道，埃塞俄比亚同样有一种兽，他称之为\n 蝎尾狮，生有三排牙齿，合拢时\n 如梳齿般彼此交错咬合：长着人的面孔和耳朵，\n 眼睛赤红；肤色血红，身躯如狮，尾巴\n 装有蝎子般的毒刺：它的叫声好似\n 笛声与号声齐鸣，它极为迅捷，而人肉是它\n 最渴望的食物。”\n    -老普林尼，《自然史》，第八卷，第二十一章"},
+    {"“And Heaven came, bringing on night and longing for love, and he lay about Earth\n spreading himself full upon her. Then the son from his ambush stretched forth\n his left hand and in his right took the great long sickle with jagged teeth,\n and swiftly lopped off his own father's members and cast them away to fall\n behind him. And not vainly did they fall from his hand; for all the bloody\n drops that gushed forth Earth received, and as the seasons moved round she bare\n the strong Erinyes and the great Giants with gleaming armour, holding long\n spears in their hands, and the Nymphs whom they call Meliae all over the\n boundless earth.”\n    -Hesiod, _Theogony_, 182-187, trans. H.G. Evelyn-White. 1920", "“于是天穹降临，携来黑夜与对爱的渴望，他舒展身躯覆盖大地，\n 让自己全然铺展在她身上。那儿子自埋伏处伸出\n 左手，右手握起那柄巨大而长、长着锯齿的镰刀，\n 迅疾割下自己父亲的阳物，抛掷出去，任其落于\n 身后。它们并非徒然从他手中落下；因为所有喷涌而出的\n 血滴尽被大地承接，随着季节流转，她生下了\n 强壮的厄里尼厄斯，和身披闪耀铠甲、手持长\n 矛的伟大巨人，以及遍布无垠大地的、\n 被称作墨利亚的宁芙。”\n    -Hesiod, _Theogony_, 182-187, trans. H.G. Evelyn-White. 1920"},
+    {"“Again at length my thought reviving came,\n When I no longer found my self the same;\n Then first this sea-green beard I felt to grow,\n And these large honours on my spreading brow;\n My long-descending locks the billows sweep,\n And my broad shoulders cleave the yielding deep;\n My fishy tail, my arms of azure hue,\n And ev'ry part divinely chang'd, I view.”\n    -Ovid, _Metamorphoses_, XIII, 546-7. 8 A.D.\n     trans. Garth, Dryden, et al.", "“终于，我的思绪再度复苏，\n 那时我发觉自己已不再是从前的我；\n 这时我第一次感到这海绿色的胡须在生长，\n 还有这宽阔额头上硕大的荣耀；\n 我垂落的长发被波涛梳理，\n 我宽阔的双肩劈开顺从的深海；\n 我鱼形的尾，我蔚蓝色的双臂，\n 我望着周身每一处皆已神奇地改变。”\n    -Ovid, _Metamorphoses_, XIII, 546-7. 8 A.D.\n     trans. Garth, Dryden, et al."},
+    {"“Lord, Lord! methought, what pain it was to drown!\n What dreadful noise of waters in mine ears!\n What ugly sights of death within mine eyes!\n Methought I saw a thousand fearful wrecks,\n Ten thousand men that fishes gnawed upon,\n Wedges of gold, great anchors, heaps of pearl,\n Inestimable stones, unvalued jewels,\n All scattered in the bottom of the sea:\n Some lay in dead men’s skulls; and in those holes\n Where eyes did once inhabit, there were crept,\n As ’t were in scorn of eyes, reflecting gems.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _King Richard III_, I, iv. 1597", "“主啊，主啊！我寻思，溺死是何等的痛苦！\n 我耳中水声何等可怖！\n 我眼中所见死亡景象何等丑恶！\n 我仿佛看见千百艘可怕的沉船残骸，\n 一万个被鱼啃噬的人，\n 金锭、巨锚、成堆的珍珠，\n 无价的宝石、难以估值的珠玉，\n 全都散落在海底：\n 有些躺在死人的头骨里；而在那些\n 曾经住着眼睛的孔洞中，竟爬进了\n 反光的宝石，仿佛在嘲弄眼睛。”\n    -William Shakespeare, _King Richard III_, I, iv. 1597"},
+    {"“Then up it raise the mermaiden,\n Wi the comb an glass in her hand:\n ‘Here's a health to you, my merrie young men,\n For you never will see dry land.’”\n    -“Sir Patrick Spens”, Scottish folk song, version 58J in Francis James\n     Child, _The English and Scottish Popular Ballads_. 1898.", "“于是那人鱼少女浮起身来，\n 手执梳子与明镜：\n ‘敬你们一杯，我快活的少年郎，\n 因为你们再也见不到陆地了。’”\n    -“Sir Patrick Spens”, Scottish folk song, version 58J in Francis James\n     Child, _The English and Scottish Popular Ballads_. 1898."},
+    {"“Damn ye! Let Neptune strike ye dead, Winslow! HAAARK! Hark Triton, hark!\n Bellow, bid our father the Sea King rise from the depths full foul in his fury!\n Black waves teeming with salt-foam to smother this young mouth with pungent\n slime, to choke ye, engorging your organs ‘til ye turn blue and bloated with\n bilge and brine and can scream no more -- only when he, crowned in cockle\n shells with slitherin’ tentacled tail and steaming beard take up his fell\n be-finnéd arm, his coral-tine trident screeches banshee-like in the tempest and\n plunges right through yer gullet, bursting ye -- a bulging bladder no more, but\n a blasted bloody film now -- a nothing for the harpies and the souls of dead\n sailors to peck and claw and feed upon, only to be lapped up and swallowed by\n the infinite waters of the dread emperor himself -- forgotten to any man, to\n any time, forgotten to any god or devil, forgotten even to the sea, for any\n stuff or part of Winslow, even any scantling of your soul is Winslow no more,\n but is now itself the sea!”\n    -Thomas Wake, _The Lighthouse_. 2019", "“去你的！让海神尼普顿劈死你，温斯洛！听——啊！听啊，特里同，听啊！\n 咆哮吧，召我们的父、那海之王自深渊升起，满腔污秽的狂怒！\n 满是盐沫的黑浪，用刺鼻的黏液堵住你这张年轻的嘴，\n 呛住你，灌满你的脏器，直到你浑身青紫肿胀，\n 带着舱底污水与盐水，再也喊不出声——唯有当他，以贝壳为冠，\n 拖着滑腻生有触手的尾巴、冒着热气的胡须，举起他那凶残的\n 生鳍之臂，他珊瑚枝般的三叉戟在风暴中如报丧女妖般尖啸，\n 直刺穿你的喉咙，将你爆裂——你不再是鼓胀的膀胱，而成\n 一片被炸开、血淋淋的薄膜——成了任哈耳庇厄与死去水手的\n 亡魂又啄又抓、大快朵颐的虚无，只等着被那可怕帝王\n 自身的无垠海水舐光吞尽——被所有人遗忘，被\n 所有时代遗忘，被所有神与魔遗忘，甚至被大海遗忘，因为\n 温斯洛的任何一点残渣或部分，哪怕你灵魂的一丝一毫，都不再是温斯洛，\n 而如今它本身便是那大海！”\n    -Thomas Wake, _The Lighthouse_. 2019"},
+    {"“Row'd on, in reach of an erected voice,\n The Sirens soon took note, without our noise,\n Tuned those sweet accents that made charms so strong,\n And these learn'd numbers made the Sirens' song:\n ‘Come here, thou worthy of a world of praise,\n That dost so high the Grecian glory raise,\n Ulysses! stay thy ship, and that song hear\n That none past ever but it bent his ear,\n But left him ravish'd, and instructed more\n By us, than any ever heard before.\n For we know all things whatsoever were\n In wide Troy labour'd; whatsoever there\n The Grecians and the Trojans both sustain'd\n By those high issues that the Gods ordain'd.\n And whatsoever all the earth can show\n T' inform a knowledge of desert, we know.’”\n    -Homer, _The Odyssey_, XII, 268-82.\n     trans. George Chapman, 1857.", "“继续划行，在一声高扬的呼唤所能及之处，\n 塞壬随即察觉了我们，无需我们出声，\n 她们调起那甜蜜的嗓音，使魅力如此强大，\n 这些精妙的音律构成了塞壬的歌：\n ‘到这儿来，你这值得全世界赞颂的人，\n 你将希腊的荣耀举得如此之高，\n 奥德修斯！停下你的船，听这支歌\n 凡经过者无不侧耳倾听，\n 无不听得如痴如醉，并从我们这里\n 学到比以往任何人所听过的更多。\n 因为我们知晓在辽阔的特洛伊\n 所辛劳的一切；也知晓在那里\n 希腊人与特洛伊人双方所承受的一切\n 皆出自诸神所注定那崇高的结局。\n 而大地上所能展现的一切，\n 凡是能教人明辨功业的，我们都知晓。’”\n    -Homer, _The Odyssey_, XII, 268-82.\n     trans. George Chapman, 1857."},
+    {"“When within sight of their foe Berserks wrought themselves into such a state\n of frenzy, that they bit their shields and rushed forward to the attack,\n throwing away their arms of defence, reckless of every danger, sometimes\n having nothing but a club, which carried with it death and destruction.”\n    -Paul Belloni Du Chaillu,_The Viking Age: the Early History, Manners, and\n     Customs of the Ancestors of the English Speaking Nations_. 1889.", "“当狂战士望见敌人时，会让自己陷入这样一种\n 癫狂状态：他们咬住盾牌，向前冲锋陷阵，\n 抛掉防身武器，置一切危险于不顾，有时\n 手无寸铁，只握一根带来死亡与毁灭的棍棒。”\n    -Paul Belloni Du Chaillu,_The Viking Age: the Early History, Manners, and\n     Customs of the Ancestors of the English Speaking Nations_. 1889."},
+    {"“I see Egypt and the Egyptians — I see the pyramids and obelisks;\n I look on chisel'd histories, songs, philosophies, cut in slabs\n     of sand-stone, or on granite-blocks;\n I see at Memphis mummy-pits, containing mummies, embalm'd, swathed\n     in linen cloth, lying there many centuries;\n I look on the fall'n Theban, the large-ball'd eyes, the side-drooping\n     neck, the hands folded across the breast.”\n    -Walt Whitman, “Salut au Monde”", "“我看见埃及与埃及人——我看见金字塔与方尖碑；\n 我凝视凿刻在砂岩石板\n     或花岗岩块上的历史、歌谣与哲思；\n 我看见孟菲斯的木乃伊坑，里面躺着涂过香料、裹着\n     亚麻布的遗体，已在那里安卧许多世纪；\n 我凝视那倾颓的底比斯人，那圆睁的大眼，那低垂\n     的脖颈，那交叠于胸前的双手。”\n    -Walt Whitman, “Salut au Monde”"},
+    {"“Amongst the deities and Asuras and celestial Rishis, O amiable lady, the Nagas\n are endued with great energy. Possessed of great speed, they are endued again\n with excellent fragrance. They deserve to be worshipped. They are capable of\n granting boons. Indeed, we too deserve to be followed by others in our train.\n I tell thee, O lady, that we are incapable of being seen by human beings.”\n    -Mahābhārata, Santi Parva, Mokshadharma Parva, section CCCLX. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883.", "“在诸神、阿修罗与天上的仙人之中，啊，可爱的女士，那伽\n 都赋有极大的威能。他们拥有极快的速度，又赋有\n 绝妙的香气。他们理应受到崇拜。他们能够\n 赐予恩惠。诚然，我们自己也理应有人追随于我们的行列。\n 我告诉你，啊，女士，我们乃是凡人所不能见的存在。”\n    -Mahābhārata, Santi Parva, Mokshadharma Parva, section CCCLX. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883."},
+    {"“Then there have also come\n nagas from Lake Nabhasa,\n Vesali, and Tacchaka.\n Kambalas, Assataras,\n Payagas, and their kin.\n And from the River Yamuna\n comes the prestigious naga, Dhatarattha.\n The great naga Eravanna:\n He, too, has come\n to the forest meeting.”\n    -Mahasamaya Sutta, _Dīgha Nikāya_, 20. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Thanissaro Bhikkhu", "“随后还有\n 来自那婆娑湖的\n 那伽，毗舍离的，以及达叉迦的。\n 剑婆罗们、阿萨陀罗们、\n 波耶伽们，及其亲族。\n 而从耶牟那河\n 来了那位尊贵的那伽，达多罗陀。\n 伟大的那伽伊罗婆那：\n 他也来了，\n 来到这林中集会。”\n    -Mahasamaya Sutta, _Dīgha Nikāya_, 20. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Thanissaro Bhikkhu"},
+    {"“Then the sallow oval between Ged's arms grew bright. It widened and spread, a\n rent in the darkness of the earth and night, a ripping open of the fabric of\n the world. Through it blazed a terrible brightness. And through that bright\n misshapen breach clambered something like a clot of black shadow, quick and\n hideous, and it leaped straight out at Ged's face.”\n    -Ursula K. Le Guin, _A Wizard Of Earthsea_. 1968.", "“随后，格得双臂之间那暗黄色的椭圆亮了起来。它扩大、蔓延，成了\n 大地与黑夜之黑暗中的一道裂隙，将世界之织物\n 撕开。可怕的亮光从中迸射而出。而透过那明亮\n 而畸形的裂口，有什么东西爬了出来，像一团黑色的阴影，迅疾而\n 可怖，直直朝格得的脸扑来。”\n    -Ursula K. Le Guin, _A Wizard Of Earthsea_. 1968."},
+    {"“Even now the intensity of his dread power can be felt\n Weakening the body and saddening the heart\n Ultimately they will become empty, mindless spectres\n Stripped of will and soul\n Only their thirst for freedom\n Gives them hunger for vengeance...”\n    -Rush, “The Necromancer”. 1975.", "“即便此刻，仍能感到他那可怖力量的威压\n 削弱身体，令心灵悲戚\n 最终他们将变成空虚、无意识的幽魂\n 被剥夺意志与灵魂\n 唯有对自由的渴求\n 赋予他们复仇的饥渴……”\n    -Rush, “The Necromancer”. 1975."},
+    {"“Humans, eh? Think they’re lords of creation. Not like us cats. We _know_ we\n are. Ever see a cat feed a human? Case proven.”\n    -Terry Pratchett, “The Amazing Maurice and His Educated Rodents”. 2001.", "“人类，是吧？自以为万物之灵。不像我们猫。我们可是_知道_自己\n 是的。见过猫喂人吗？证据确凿。”\n    -Terry Pratchett, “The Amazing Maurice and His Educated Rodents”. 2001."},
+    {"“If thus mutation is influenced by natural selection, it implies, that any\n particular mutation must advance in a direction advantageous for the\n respective species, and, indeed, many examples of mutation known among fossil\n animals are apparently due to the advantage produced by the change. I must add\n here, however that probably not all mutations (in a palaeontological meaning)\n are due to natural selection, but that many do not imply an actual\n improvement.”\n    -_Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society_, Volume XXV, no. 150.\n     1896.", "“如果突变果真受自然选择影响，那就意味着，任何\n 特定的突变都必定朝着对该物种有利的方向\n 推进；而且，化石动物中已知的许多突变实例\n 显然都归因于这种变化所带来的优势。不过，我\n 必须在此补充，很可能并非所有突变（就古生物学意义而言）\n 都源于自然选择，有许多突变并不意味着实际的\n 改良。”\n    -_Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society_, Volume XXV, no. 150.\n     1896."},
+    {"“Each family or tribe has a wizard or conjuring doctor, whose office we could\n never clearly ascertain.”\n    -Charles Darwin, _The Voyage of the Beagle_, ch. X. 1839.", "“每个家族或部落都有一位巫师或施法的巫医，其职责我们\n 始终未能完全弄清楚。”\n    -Charles Darwin, _The Voyage of the Beagle_, ch. X. 1839."},
+    {"“In their brief time together Slothrop forms the impression that this octopus\n is not in good mental health, though where's his basis for comparing? But\n there is a mad exhuberance, as with inanimate objects which fall off of tables\n when we are sensitive to noise and our own clumsiness and don't want them to\n fall, a sort of wham! ha-ha you hear that? here it is again, WHAM! in the\n cephalopod's every movement, which Slothrop is glad to get away from as he\n finally scales the crab like a discus, with all his strength, out to sea, and\n the octopus, with an eager splash and gurgle, strikes out in pursuit, and is\n presently gone.”\n    -Thomas Pynchon, _Gravity's Rainbow_. 1973.", "“在他们短暂的相处中，斯洛思罗普觉得这只章鱼\n 精神不太健康，不过他凭什么作比较呢？但\n 其中有一种疯狂的欢腾，就像那些放在桌上的无生命物件\n 在我们对声响和自己笨手笨脚格外敏感、不愿它们\n 掉落时偏偏落下来，一种砰！哈哈你听见了吗？又来了，砰！这般\n 劲头，在这头头足类动物的每一个动作里，斯洛思罗普很高兴能脱身，\n 他最终像掷铁饼一样用尽全力把那只蟹甩向大海，而\n 章鱼急切地溅起水花、咕嘟作响，追击而去，随即\n 不见了踪影。”\n    -Thomas Pynchon, _Gravity's Rainbow_. 1973."},
+    {"“The little princess, asleep in her cradle, floated on the water, and at last\n she was cast up on the shore of a beautiful country, where, however, very few\n people dwelt since the ogre Ravagio and his wife Tourmentine had gone to live\n there-for they ate up everybody. Ogres are terrible people. When once they\n have tasted raw human flesh they will hardly eat anything else, and\n Tourmentine always knew how to make some body come their way, for she was half\n a fairy.”\n    -Marie-Catherine Le Jumel de Barneville, Baronne d'Aulnoy, “'Orangier et\nl'Abeille”. 1697.\n\n“NO. Layers. Onions have layers. Ogres have layers. Onions have layers. You get\n it? We both have layers.”\n    -Shrek. 2001.", "“小公主睡在摇篮里，漂浮在水面上，最终\n 她被冲上一片美丽国度的海岸，然而那里\n 人烟稀少，因为食人魔拉瓦乔和他的妻子图尔芒蒂娜搬到了\n 那里——他们把所有人都吃光了。食人魔是可怕的家伙。他们一旦\n 尝过生人肉，就几乎再也吃不下别的了，而\n 图尔芒蒂娜总有办法让什么人来到他们跟前，因为她有一半\n 是仙女。”\n    -Marie-Catherine Le Jumel de Barneville, Baronne d'Aulnoy, “'Orangier et\nl'Abeille”. 1697.\n\n“不。层次。洋葱有层次。食人魔有层次。洋葱有层次。明白\n 了吗？我们都有层次。”\n    -Shrek. 2001."},
+    {"“Carbonic acid is one of the three materials which together form the starting\n point of vegetable growth; the others being water and nitric acid. This acid is\n formed of carbon and oxygen in the proportion of one part of the former to\n two of the latter chemically combined. It is a colorless gas, having an acid\n taste and smell; is soluble in water; weighs one-half more than air and can be\n poured from one vessel to another, as a liquid may be; 100 parts of water\n dissolve 106 parts of this gas, and it is from this source that the roots of\n plants derive the needed supplies of it.”\n    -Henry Stuart, _The Culture of Farm Crops: A Manual of the Science of\n    Agriculture, and a Hand-book of Practice for American Farmers_, ch X. 1887.", "“碳酸是共同构成植物生长起点的三种物质之一；\n 另外两种则是水与硝酸。这种酸由碳和氧\n 构成，二者的比例是前者一份对后者两份，\n 经化学结合而成。它是一种无色气体，带有酸味\n 和酸臭；可溶于水；比空气重一半，可以\n 像液体一样从一个容器倒入另一个容器；100份水\n 可溶解106份这种气体，而植物的根正是从这一来源\n 获取所需的这种物质。”\n    -Henry Stuart, _The Culture of Farm Crops: A Manual of the Science of\n    Agriculture, and a Hand-book of Practice for American Farmers_, ch X. 1887."},
+    {"“The popularity and longevity of the oni myth is no doubt partially based on\n the beings’ conventional demonic accoutrements, which have remained\n relatively constant through the ages: they are dreadful super-natural\n beings emerging from the abyss of Buddhist hell to terrify wicked mortals;\n their grotesque and savage demeanor and form instill instant fear; and the\n oni’s omnipresence in the socio-historical and cultural archive of Japan is\n directly attributable to the moral, social, and religious edification that\n stories about oni engender.”\n    -Noriko T. Reider, _Japanese Demon Lore: Oni from Ancient Times to the\n     Present_, ch. 1, 2010.", "“鬼神话的流行与长久，无疑部分归因于\n 这类存在约定俗成的恶魔装备，它们在\n 各个时代中大体保持不变：它们是可怕的超自然\n 存在，从佛教地狱的深渊中涌出，恐吓作恶的凡人；\n 它们怪诞凶蛮的举止与形貌立即唤起恐惧；而\n 鬼在日本社会历史与文化档案中的无处不在，\n 直接归功于关于鬼的故事所带来的\n 道德、社会与宗教上的教化。”\n    -Noriko T. Reider, _Japanese Demon Lore: Oni from Ancient Times to the\n     Present_, ch. 1, 2010."},
+    {"“As I looked at the living creatures, I saw a wheel on the ground beside\n each creature with its four faces. This was the appearance and structure\n of the wheels: They sparkled like chrysolite, and all four looked alike.\n Each appeared to be made like a wheel intersecting a wheel. As they\n moved, they would go in any one of the four directions the creatures faced;\n the wheels did not turn about as the creatures went. Their rims were\n high and awesome, and all four rims were full of eyes all around.”\n    -Ezekiel 1:15-18 (New International Version)", "“我观看那些活物的时候，见地上有一个轮子，在\n 每个有四张脸的活物旁边。轮的形状与构造\n 是这样：它们如橄榄石般闪耀，四个轮子形状相同。\n 每个轮子看上去就像轮中套轮。它们\n 移动时，可向活物所朝四方中的任意一方行去；\n 活物行走时，轮子并不转身。轮辋\n 高而可畏，四个轮辋周围都布满了眼睛。”\n    -Ezekiel 1:15-18 (New International Version)"},
+    {"“In Sparkill buried lies that man of mark\n Who brought the Obelisk to Central Park,\n Redoubtable Commander H.H. Gorringe,\n Whose name supplies the long-sought rhyme for ‘orange.’”\n    -Arthur Guiterman, “Local Note”. 1934.", "“斯帕基尔镇下长眠着那位显赫之人，\n 是他把方尖碑运到了中央公园，\n 令人敬畏的H.H.戈林奇指挥官，\n 他的名字正好为‘orange’提供了久寻不得的韵脚。”\n    -Arthur Guiterman, “Local Note”. 1934."},
+    {"“X gon' give it to ya (what)\n Fuck waitin' for you to get it on your own, X gon' deliver to ya\n Knock knock, open up the door, it's real\n With the non-stop pop-pop from stainless steel\n [...]\n First we gonna rock, then we gonna roll\n Then we let it pop, go, let it go\n X gon' give it to ya, he gon' give it to ya\n X gon' give it to ya, he gon' give it to ya\n    -DMX, “X Gon' Give It To Ya”. 2002", "“X 会把它给你（啥）\n 去他妈的等你自个儿搞定，X 会送到你手上\n 咚咚，把门打开，这是真的\n 伴随着不锈钢不停歇的砰—砰\n [...]\n 先来摇滚，再滚起来\n 然后让它炸响，走，随它去\n X 会把它给你，他会把它给你\n X 会把它给你，他会把它给你\n    -DMX, “X Gon' Give It To Ya”. 2002"},
+    {"“There 's not the smallest orb which thou behold'st\n But in his motion like an angel sings,\n Still quiring to the young-eyed cherubins.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Merchant of Venice_, V, i. 1597.", "“你所见的哪怕最小的天体\n 也在运行中如天使般歌唱，\n 永远和应着目光明澈的智天使。”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Merchant of Venice_, V, i. 1597."},
+    {"“Mean while the winged heralds by command\n Of Sovran power, with awful Ceremony\n And Trumpets sound throughout the Host proclaim\n A solemn Councel forthwith to be held\n At Pandaemonium, the high Capital\n Of Satan and his Peers...”\n    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book I, 1667.", "“与此同时，那些带翼的传令官奉\n 至高权能之命，以庄严的仪典\n 和号角之声，向全军宣告：\n 即刻将在潘地蒙尼乌姆召开\n 一场庄严的会议——那正是撒旦\n 及其同僚的巍峨都城……”\n    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book I, 1667."},
+    {"“Who wondrous things concerning our welfare, And straunge phantomes doth lett\n us ofte foresee.”\n    -Spenser, _The Faerie Queene_ II. xii. 47", "“关乎我等福祉的奇妙之事，以及怪异幻影，\n 常令我等预见。”\n    -斯宾塞，《仙后》II. xii. 47"},
+    {"“Then the LORD rained upon Sodom and upon Gomorrah brimstone and fire from the\n LORD out of heaven; And he overthrew those cities, and all the plain, and all\n the inhabitants of the cities, and that which grew upon the ground. But\n [Lot's] wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt.”\n    -KJV Bible, Genesis 19:24-26.", "“于是，主从天上将硫磺与火降与所多玛和蛾摩拉；\n 主把那些城和全平原，并城里所有的居民，\n 连地上生长的都毁灭了。但\n 罗得的妻子在后边回头一看，就变成了一根盐柱。”\n    -《钦定版圣经·创世记》19:24-26。"},
+    {"“Know thyself.”\n    -Inscription in the pronaos of the Temple of Apollo at Delphi", "“认识你自己。”\n    -德尔斐阿波罗神庙前廊铭文"},
+    {"“The Polar Bear is an animal of tremendous strength and fierceness. Barentz, in\n his voyage in search of a north-east passage to China, had proofs of the\n ferocity of these animals, in the island of Nova Zembla, where they attacked\n his seamen, seizing them in their mouths; carrying them off with the utmost\n ease, and devouring them in the sight of their comrades. It is said that they\n will attack and attempt to board armed vessels, at a great distance from\n shore, and have sometimes been with much difficulty repelled.”\n    -George Shaw, _General Zoology, or, Systematic Natural History_, vol. I,\n     p. 2. 1800.", "“北极熊是一种力大无穷、凶猛异常的动物。巴伦支在\n 其寻找通往中国东北航道的航行中，于新地岛\n 获得了这些动物凶猛的证据：它们袭击他的船员，\n 用嘴叼住他们，轻而易举地将人带走，\n 并在同伴眼前将其吞噬。据说它们会在离岸很远的地方\n 攻击并试图登上武装船只，有时须费很大力气\n 才能将其击退。”\n    -乔治·肖，《普通动物学，或系统自然史》，第一卷，\n     第2页。1800年。"},
+    {"“A spell was cast and the sky turned red\n The angel's heart froze to ice\n In the gloomy sky black clouds were gathering\n The silence was broken by cries\n A spell was cast and the sky turned red\n The angel's heart froze to ice\n In the gloomy sky — The silence where dead angels lie\n\n Touch the snow... Caress the lifeless sculptures\n Die!!!”\n    -Dissection, “Where Dead Angels Lie”. 1996.", "“咒语已施，天空变红\n 天使之心冻结成冰\n 阴沉天空中黑云聚集\n 寂静被哭喊声打破\n 咒语已施，天空变红\n 天使之心冻结成冰\n 阴沉天空——死天使长眠的寂静\n\n 触摸那雪……抚摸无生命的雕塑\n 死！！！”\n    -Dissection，《死天使长眠之处》。1996年。"},
+    {"“If builders built buildings the way programmers wrote programs, then the first\n woodpecker that came along would destroy civilization.”\n    -Gerald Weinberg, Weinberg's Second Law", "“如果建筑工人像程序员写程序那样盖房子，那么第一只\n 路过的啄木鸟就会毁灭文明。”\n    -杰拉尔德·温伯格，温伯格第二定律"},
+    {"“I call upon Proteus,\n key-holding master of the sea,\n first-born, who showed\n the beginnings of all nature,\n changing matter\n into a great variety of forms.\n Honored by all, he is wise,\n and he knows what is now,\n what was before,\n and what will be in the future.\n He has all at his disposal,\n transformed far beyond\n all other immortals\n who dwell on snowy Olympos\n and fly through the air\n and over land and sea,\n for Physis was the first to place\n everything in Proteus.\n Father, attended by holy providence,\n visit the mystic initiates,\n and bring a good end to a life\n of industry and prosperity.”\n    -_The Orphic Hymns_, XXV.\n     trans. Apostolos N. Athanassakis and Benjamin M. Wolkow, 2013.", "“我呼唤普罗透斯，\n 掌管钥匙的海洋之主，\n 初生者，他展示了\n 一切自然的开端，\n 将物质\n 变为千姿百态。\n 众人崇敬，他智慧非凡，\n 知晓当下之事，\n 过去之事，\n 以及未来之事。\n 一切皆在他掌握，\n 其变形远超\n 所有其他不朽者——\n 那些居住在雪白奥林匹斯、\n 翱翔于空中、\n 越过陆地与海洋者，\n 因为自然女神最早将\n 一切置于普罗透斯之中。\n 父啊，在神圣天佑陪伴下，\n 眷顾神秘入教者，\n 并赐予勤勉而繁荣的一生\n 一个美好的结局。”\n    -《俄耳甫斯祷歌》，XXV。\n     译：Apostolos N. Athanassakis 与 Benjamin M. Wolkow，2013年。"},
+    {"“Vaisampayana said, 'Not far from the place where the Pandavas were asleep, a\n Rakshasa by name Hidimva dwelt on the Sala tree. Possessed of great energy and\n prowess, he was a cruel cannibal of visage that was grim in consequence of his\n sharp and long teeth. He was now hungry and longing for human flesh. Of long\n shanks and a large belly, his locks and beard were both red in hue. His\n shoulders were broad like the neck of a tree; his ears were like unto arrows,\n and his features were frightful.”\n    -_Mahābhārata_, Adi Parva, Hidimva-vadha Parva, section CLIV. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883.", "“Vaisampayana 说：‘离般度族沉睡之地不远处，有一\n 名叫希丁巴的罗刹住在娑罗树上。他精力充沛、\n 孔武有力，是个残忍的食人者，因锋利长牙而面目狰狞。\n 他那时饥饿难耐，渴望人肉。他四肢修长，\n 肚腹硕大，须发赤红。他的\n 肩膀宽阔如树干；耳朵如箭矢，\n 相貌令人恐惧。’”\n    -《摩诃婆罗多》，初篇，希丁巴伏诛篇，第154节。约公元前500年。\n     译：Kisari Mohan Ganguli，1883年。"},
+    {"“All our times have come.\n Here but now they're gone.\n Seasons don't fear the reaper,\n Nor do the wind, the sun or the rain.\n We can be like they are.\n Come on baby, don't fear the reaper.”\n\n    -Buck Dharma, Blue Öyster Cult, “(Don't Fear) The Reaper”. 1976.", "“我们所有的时光已然来临。\n 此刻却已消逝。\n 四季不惧死神，\n 风、太阳与雨也不惧。\n 我们可如他们一般。\n 来吧宝贝，不要惧怕死神。”\n\n    -Buck Dharma，Blue Öyster Cult，《(别怕)死神》。1976年。"},
+    {"“While the angels, all pallid and wan,\n   Uprising, unveiling, affirm\n That the play is the tragedy, ‘Man’,\n   And its hero, the Conqueror Worm.”\n    -Edgar Allan Poe", "“当众天使，个个苍白憔悴，\n   起身，揭开面纱，确认\n 这出戏是悲剧《人》，\n   其主角，是那征服之虫。”\n    -埃德加·爱伦·坡"},
+    {"“As for example: the Salamander made in fashion of a Lizard, marked with spots\n like to stars, never comes abroad and sheweth it selfe but in great showers;\n for in faire weather he is not seene. He is of so cold a complexion, that if\n hee do but touch the fire, hee wil quench it as presently, as if yce were put\n into it. The Salamander casteth up at the mouth a certaine venomous matter\n like unto milke, let it but once touch any bare part of a man or womans bodie,\n all the haire will fall off: and the part so touched will change the colour of\n the skin to the white morphew.”\n    -Gaius Plinius Secundus, _Naturalis Historia_, Book X, ch. LXVII. 79 A.D.\n     trans. Philemon Holland, 1601.", "“例如：那形似蜥蜴、带有星状斑点的火蜥蜴，\n 从不外出，只在瓢泼大雨中现身；\n 因为晴天时看不见它。它的体质如此寒冷，若\n 只消触碰火焰，便会立刻将其扑灭，仿佛把冰\n 投入其中。火蜥蜴会从口中吐出某种\n 如牛奶般的毒物，只要它一碰到男人或女人身体的裸露部位，\n 所有毛发都会脱落；被碰触之处会使\n 肤色变成白色麻风。”\n    -盖乌斯·普林尼·塞孔都斯，《自然史》，第十卷，第67章。公元79年。\n     译：Philemon Holland，1601年。"},
+    {"“Those poisonous fields, with rank luxuriance crown'd,\n Where the dark scorpion gathers death around”\n    -Oliver Goldsmith, “The Deserted Village”, l. 351-2. 1770.", "“那些有毒的田野，被繁茂的野草加冕，\n 黑暗的蝎子在四周聚集死亡”\n    -奥利弗·哥尔德斯密斯，《荒村》，第351-2行。1770年。"},
+    {"“In the year that king Uzziah died I saw also the LORD sitting upon a throne,\n high and lifted up, and his train filled the temple.\n\n Above it stood the seraphims: each one had six wings; with twain he covered\n his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly.\n\n And one cried unto another, and said, Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts:\n the whole earth is full of his glory.”\n    -KJV Bible, Isaiah 6:1-3.", "“当乌西雅王崩的那年，我见主坐在高高的宝座上，\n 他的衣裳垂下，遮满圣殿。\n\n 其上有撒拉弗侍立，各有六个翅膀：用两个翅膀遮脸，\n 两个翅膀遮脚，两个翅膀飞翔。\n\n 彼此呼喊说：圣哉！圣哉！圣哉！万军之主；\n 他的荣光充满全地。”\n    -《钦定版圣经·以赛亚书》6:1-3。"},
+    {"“Between the idea\n And the reality\n Between the motion\n And the act\n Falls the Shadow\n     For thine is the Kingdom\n\n Between the conception\n And the creation\n Between the emotion\n And the response\n Falls the Shadow\n     Life is very long”\n    -T.S. Eliot, “The Hollow Men”. 1925.", "“在理念\n 与现实之间\n 在动机\n 与行为之间\n 阴影降临\n     因为国度是你的\n\n 在构想\n 与创造之间\n 在情感\n 与回应之间\n 阴影降临\n     生命漫长”\n    -T.S. 艾略特，《空心人》。1925年。"},
+    {"“As we grow old, we become aware that death is drawing near; his shadow falls\n across our path...”\n    -Stefan Zweig, _Twenty-Four Hours in the Life of a Woman_. 1927.", "“随着我们老去，我们意识到死亡正在临近；他的阴影\n 笼罩我们的道路……”\n    -斯蒂芬·茨威格，《一个女人一生中的二十四小时》。1927年。"},
+    {"“Crux sacra sit mihi lux / Non draco sit mihi dux\n Vade retro satana / Numquam suade mihi vana\n Sunt mala quae libas / Ipse venena bibas\n\n [Let the Holy Cross be my light / Let not the dragon be my guide\n  Step back Satan / Never tempt me with vain things\n  What you offer me is evil / You drink the poison yourself.]”\n    -Full text of initials on the Saint Benedict Medal, worn to ward off evil\n     spirits. Inscription from 11-15th century, formally approved by Pope\n     Benedict XIV in 18th century.", "“Crux sacra sit mihi lux / Non draco sit mihi dux\n Vade retro satana / Numquam suade mihi vana\n Sunt mala quae libas / Ipse venena bibas\n\n [愿神圣十字架为我的光 / 愿恶龙不为我的向导\n  退后，撒旦 / 勿以虚妄诱惑我\n  你所献上的乃是恶 / 你自己饮下毒药。]”\n    -圣本笃勋章上首字母全文，佩戴以驱避邪\n     灵。铭文源自11-15世纪，18世纪由教宗\n     本笃十四世正式批准。"},
+    {"“Yesterday, upon the stair,\n I met a man who wasn't there\n He wasn't there again today\n I wish, I wish he'd go away...”\n    -Hughes Mearns ,_Antigonish_, 1-4. 1899.", "“昨天，在楼梯上，\n 我遇见一个不在那里的人\n 今天他又不在那里\n 我希望，我希望他走开……”\n    -休斯·默恩斯，《安蒂戈尼什》，1-4。1899年。"},
+    {"“There is unrest in the forest\n There is trouble with the trees\n For the maples want more sunlight\n And the oaks ignore their pleas”\n    -Rush, \"The Trees\". 1978.", "“森林里有动荡\n 树木间有麻烦\n 因为枫树想要更多阳光\n 而橡树无视它们的请求”\n    -Rush，《树》。1978年。"},
+    {"“And then th' old Sea-God crept\n From forth the deeps, and found his fat calves there,\n Survey'd, and number'd, and came never near\n The craft we used, but told us five for calves.\n His temples then dis-eased with sleep he salves;\n And in rush'd we, with an abhorred cry,\n Cast all our hands about him manfully;\n And then th' old Forger all his forms began:\n First was a lion with a mighty mane,\n Then next a dragon, a pied panther then,\n A vast boar next, and suddenly did strain\n All into water. Last he was a tree,\n Curl'd all at top, and shot up to the sky.”\n    -Homer, _The Odyssey_, IV, 602-14.\n     trans. George Chapman, 1857.", "“然后那老海神悄悄爬出\n 深海，在那里发现他肥壮的小腿，\n 他察看、计数，却从不靠近\n 我们所用的诡计，只报给我们五条小腿。\n 他的太阳穴因睡意而发沉，便用睡眠安抚；\n 我们便冲上去，发出可憎的喊叫，\n 所有人勇敢地用手抓住他；\n 接着那老变形者开始展现他的各种形态：\n 首先是一头鬃毛雄伟的狮子，\n 接着是一条龙，然后是一只花斑豹，\n 再后来是一头巨猪，突然又\n 全部化为水。最后他变成一棵树，\n 顶部卷曲，直冲云霄。”\n    -荷马，《奥德赛》，IV，602-14。\n     译：George Chapman，1857年。"},
+    {"“There is a silence where hath been no sound,\n There is a silence where no sound may be,—\n In the cold grave, under the deep, deep sea,\n Or in the wide desert where no life is found.”\n    -Thomas Hood, “Silence”. Early 19th cent.", "“有一种寂静，那里从未有过声音，\n 有一种寂静，那里不能有任何声音，——\n 在冰冷的坟墓里，在深深的海底，\n 或在找不到生命的广阔沙漠中。”\n    -托马斯·胡德，《寂静》。19世纪初。"},
+    {"“The simulacrum now hides, not the truth, but the fact that there is none, that\n is to say, the continuation of Nothingness.”\n    -Jean Baudrillard, “Radical Thought”. 1994.\n     trans. François Debrix, 1995.", "“拟像如今隐藏的不是真相，而是没有真相这一事实，也\n 就是说，虚无的延续。”\n    -让·鲍德里亚，《激进思想》。1994年。\n     译：François Debrix，1995年。"},
+    {"“I saw a mouth jeering. A smile of melted red iron ran over it. Its laugh was\n full of nails rattling. It was a child's dream of a mouth.\n A fist hit the mouth: knuckles of gun-metal driven by an electric wrist and\n shoulder. It was a child's dream of an arm.\n The fist hit the mouth over and over, again and again. The mouth bled melted\n iron, and laughed its laughter of nails rattling.\n And I saw the more the fist pounded the more the mouth laughed. The fist is\n pounding and pounding, and the mouth answering.”\n    -Carl Sandburg, “Gargoyle”, _Cornhusker_. 1918.", "“我看见一张嘴在嘲笑。熔化的红铁般的笑容掠过它。它的笑声\n 充满了钉子碰撞的咔嗒声。那是孩童梦中一张嘴。\n 一只拳头击中那张嘴：枪金属的指节由带电的手腕和\n 肩膀驱动。那是孩童梦中一只手臂。\n 拳头一次又一次、反复击中那张嘴。嘴流出熔化的\n 铁，并发出钉子碰撞般的笑声。\n 我看见拳头越猛击，嘴就笑得越厉害。拳头\n 不停地猛击，嘴不停地回应。”\n    -卡尔·桑德堡，《石像鬼》，《玉米剥皮者》。1918年。"},
+    {"“Speak! speak! thou fearful guest!\n Who, with thy hollow breast\n Still in rude armor drest,\n Comest to daunt me!\n Wrapt not in Eastern balms,\n But with thy fleshless palms\n Stretched, as if asking alms,\n Why dost thou haunt me?”\n    -Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, “The Skeleton in Armor”. 1841.", "“说话！说话！你这可怕的客人！\n 你胸膛空洞，\n 仍穿着粗劣的盔甲，\n 来此吓唬我！\n 你没有裹着东方的香膏，\n 却伸出无肉的手掌，\n 仿佛在乞讨，\n 为何你要缠着我？”\n    -亨利·沃兹沃斯·朗费罗，《披甲骷髅》。1841年。"},
+    {"“God save us from the skeleton\n Who sitteth at the feast!”\n    -James Jeffrey Roche, _The Skeleton at the Feast_. 1890.", "“上帝救我们远离那骷髅\n 他坐在宴席之上！”\n    -詹姆斯·杰弗里·罗奇，《宴席上的骷髅》。1890年。"},
+    {"“Her own mother lived the latter years of her life in the horrible suspicion\n that electricity was dripping invisibly all over the house.”\n    -James Thurber, _My Life and Hard Times_. 1934.", "“她自己的母亲在晚年一直怀着一种可怕的猜疑，\n 觉得电力正无形地滴落在整栋房子里。”\n    -詹姆斯·瑟伯，《我与艰难岁月》。1934年。"},
+    {"“The very deep did rot: O Christ!\n That ever this should be!\n Yea, slimy things did crawl with legs\n Upon the slimy sea.”\n    -Samuel Tayor Coleridge, “The Rime of the Ancient Mariner”. 1798.", "“深渊也腐烂了：哦，基督！\n 竟会有这样的事！\n 是啊，黏滑的东西用腿爬行\n 在黏滑的海上。”\n    -塞缪尔·泰耶·柯勒律治，《古舟子咏》。1798年。"},
+    {"“No — it wasn't that way at all. It was everywhere — a gelatin — a slime yet it\n had shapes, a thousand shapes of horror beyond all memory. There were eyes —\n and a blemish. It was the pit — the maelstrom — the ultimate abomination.\n Carter, it was the unnamable!”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft, _The Unnamable_. 1925.", "“不——根本不是那样。它无处不在——一团胶质——一团黏液，却又\n 有形状，一千种超越记忆的恐怖形状。有眼睛——\n 还有一块瑕疵。它是深渊——大漩涡——终极的可憎之物。\n 卡特，它是不可名状者！”\n    -H.P. 洛夫克拉夫特，《不可名状》。1925年。"},
+    {"“Why does the thin grey strand\n Floating up from the forgotten\n Cigarette between my fingers,\n Why does it trouble me?”\n    -D.H. Lawrence, “Sorrow”. 1916.", "“为何那纤细的灰色烟缕\n 从被我遗忘的\n 指间香烟中升起，\n 为何它令我烦扰？”\n    -D.H. 劳伦斯，《悲伤》。1916年。"},
+    {"“After a while they came to a village. ”Now then,“ said Snapping Turtle, ”in\n the morning at daylight, my friends, we will make on attack. I myself will\n first go to the place,“ the leader of the war party said to them.\n\n ”Good,“ said the other little one, ”thou art the one who sees to it what we\n shall do,“ they said to that Snapping Turtle. ”Now then,“ said Snapping\n Turtle, ”verily I am now going to tell you what I shall do.“ Thus he spoke.\n ”Now is the time I shall begin to walk toward this village. Verily at the time\n I shall kill the daughter of the chief will be when the light of day is\n breaking, and at the same instant the sky will glow with red in the direction\n whence the morrow comes. ‘Ho, there, our comrade has killed her!’ will thus be\n the thought in your hearts. Then is the time when you want to make a great\n noise, when you shall whoop all keep it up. Now is the time that you go to\n attack this village.“ Thus he spoke to those his young men.\n\n ”All right!“ said the other little fellows.”\n    -“When Snapping Turtle went to War”, _Publications of the American\n     Ethnological Society, Volume IX: Kickapoo Tales_. 1915.\n     trans. Truman Michelson", "“过了一会儿，他们来到一个村庄。‘那么，’咬龟说，‘明\n 天破晓时，我的朋友们，我们将发动攻击。我自己会\n 先到那地方去，’战争首领对他们说。\n\n ‘好，’另一个小家伙说，‘你是那个决定我们该做什么的人，’\n 他们对咬龟说。‘那么，’咬龟\n 说，‘我现在真要告诉你们我会做什么。’他如此说道。\n ‘现在是我开始朝这个村庄走去的时候。我杀死\n 酋长女儿的时刻，将是天光破晓之时，\n 就在同一瞬间，天空会在明日来临的方向\n 泛红。“嘿，那边，我们的同伴杀了她！”这便会是\n 你们心中的想法。那时你们就要发出巨大的\n 声响，你们要一起呐喊不停。现在就是你们去\n 攻击这个村庄的时候。’他对那些年轻人如此说道。\n\n ‘好吧！’其他小家伙们说。”\n    -《当咬龟去打仗时》，《美国\n     民族学会出版物》第九卷：基卡普故事集。1915年。\n     译：Truman Michelson"},
+    {"“Negation is the mind's first freedom.”\n    -E.M. Cioran, _The Temptation to Exist_. 1956.", "“否定是心灵的第一种自由。”\n    -E.M. 齐奥朗，《存在的诱惑》。1956。"},
+    {"“It was just a colour out of space—a frightful messenger from unformed realms\n of infinity beyond all Nature as we know it; from realms whose mere existence\n stuns the brain and numbs us with the black extra-cosmic gulfs it throws open\n before our frenzied eyes.”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft, “The Colour out of Space”. 1927.", "“那不过是来自太空的一种颜色——一个来自我们所知自然之外的无限\n 混沌疆域的可怕信使；那些疆域仅凭其存在，\n 便以它在\n 我们疯狂眼前敞开的宇宙之外黑色深渊，令人头脑麻痹。”\n    -H.P. 洛夫克拉夫特，《来自太空的颜色》。1927。"},
+    {"“In any composition, the solid narrates the anomalies generated by the void, or\n the infection of the void through the solid (where the void comes to the solid,\n it works as a convoluting plague, a coiling swirling epidemic rather than a\n nullifying process or a solid-annihilating agent); in a composition there is no\n pure solid but a defiled one, a diseased and deflowered solid.\n [...]\n The only way that the solid can initialize its architectonic and compositional\n activities (processes for survival, development, etc.) is by letting the void\n in. The dynamic traits of solid can only be actuated when solid is eaten,\n convoluted and messed up by the void. There is no other option for solid.”\n    -Reza Negarestani, _Cyclonopedia: Complicity with Anonymous Materials_. 2008", "“在任何构成中，固体叙述着虚空所生成的异常，或\n 虚空经由固体的感染（当虚空触及固体时，\n 它运作如一种卷曲的瘟疫，一种盘旋翻涌的流行病，而非\n 一种虚化过程或消灭固体的作用者）；在构成中没有\n 纯粹的固体，只有被玷污的固体，一种染病而失贞的固体。\n [...]\n 固体初始化其建构与构成\n 活动（生存、发展等过程）的唯一方式，就是让虚空\n 进入。固体的动态特性只有在固体被虚空吞噬、\n 卷曲、搅乱时才能被激发。固体别无选择。”\n    -Reza Negarestani，《Cyclonopedia: Complicity with Anonymous Materials》。2008"},
+    {"“On the hungry craving wind\n My Spectre follows thee behind.”\n    -William Blake, “Broken Love”. ca. 1800.", "“在饥渴的狂风中\n 我的幽灵紧随你身后。”\n    -威廉·布莱克，《破碎的爱》。约1800年。"},
+    {"“When you think\n Your toys have gone berserk\n It's an illusion\n You cannot shirk”\n    -Siouxsie and the Banshees, “Spellbound”", "“当你以为\n 你的玩物已发狂\n 那只是幻觉\n 你无从逃避”\n    -Siouxsie and the Banshees，《Spellbound》"},
+    {"“Oedipus speaks: 'Far from me is the crime and shame of cowardice, and my\n valour knows not dastard fears[...] The Sphinx, weaving her words in darkling\n measures, I fled not; I faced the bloody jaws of the fell prophetess and the\n ground white with scattered bones. And when from a lofty cliff, already\n hovering over her prey, she prepared her pinions and, lashing her tail like\n a savage lion, stirred up her threatening wrath, I asked her riddle.\n Thereupon came a sound of dread; her jaws crashed, and her talons, brooking\n no delay, eager for my vitals, tore at the rocks. The lot's intricate,\n guile-entangled words, the grim riddle of the winged beast, I solved.'”\n    -Seneca, _Oedipus_, 1st cent. A.D.\n     trans. Frank Justus Miller, 1917.", "“俄狄浦斯说：‘怯懦的罪与耻离我甚远，我的\n 勇气不识卑怯的恐惧[...]斯芬克斯在幽暗中\n 编织言辞，我未曾逃避；我直面那凶恶女先知的\n 血盆大口，与散落白骨染白的地面。而当她自高崖之上，已\n 在猎物上方盘旋，张开双翼，如狂野雄狮般甩动尾巴，\n 激起她那威胁的怒火时，我向她提出了谜题。\n 随即传来可怕的声响；她的下颚轰然咬合，她的利爪，\n 不容迟疑，渴求我的内脏，抓挠着岩石。\n 命运那错综复杂、狡诈缠绕的言辞，那头有翼之兽的严峻谜题，我解开了。’”\n    -塞内加，《俄狄浦斯》，公元1世纪。\n     译：Frank Justus Miller，1917年。"},
+    {"\"My colleagues may have dismissed the sightings as blink frog egg casings, but\ntoday I shall prove that these creatures came from beyond the firmament. Let's\nbegin the dissection.\"\n        -Ardun Idrick, cryptozoologist, last words", "\"我的同事们或许把那些目击当作瞬蛙卵鞘而置之不理，但\ntoday 我将证明这些生物来自苍穹之外。让\n我们开始解剖吧。\"\n        -Ardun Idrick，神秘动物学家，遗言"},
+    {"“Did you see that star go out?\n I seen it burn.\n\n That little star went out,\n your little eyes went out.\n\n Our burnt little dreams are hid\n up where the stars get lit.”\n    -Thee Silver Mt. Zion, “I Built Myself a Metal Bird”. 2010.", "“你看见那颗星熄灭了吗？\n 我看见它燃烧过。\n\n 那颗小星熄灭了，\n 你的小眼睛也熄灭了。\n\n 我们烧焦的小小梦想藏于\n 群星被点亮之处。”\n    -Thee Silver Mt. Zion，《I Built Myself a Metal Bird》。2010。"},
+    {"“I really believe what you say, answered the knight; for, I have been engaged\n with the giant, in the most obstinate and outrageous combat that I believe I\n shall ever fight in all the days of my life: with one backstroke, slam went\n his head to the ground; and discharged such a quantity of blood, that it ran\n like rills of water, along the field.”\n    -Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra, _The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La\n     Mancha_, IV, 10. 1605.\n     trans. Carlos Fuentes, 1997.", "“我确实相信您所说的话，骑士答道；因为，我曾与\n 那巨人交手，进行我相信此生\n 所经历的最顽固、最凶猛的战斗：只一记反手，他的\n 头颅便砰然落地；鲜血喷涌如\n 溪水，流过田野。”\n    -米格尔·德·塞万提斯·萨维德拉，《拉曼却的\n     奇情异想的绅士堂吉诃德》，IV，10。1605年。\n     译：Carlos Fuentes，1997年。"},
+    {"“Behold him setting in his western skies,\n The shadows lengthening as the vapours rise.”\n    -John Dryden, “Absalom and Achitophel”, I, 268. 1681.\n\n“The sky is sad and beautiful, like a vast altar.\n The sun has drowned in its congealing blood.”\n    -Charles Baudelaire, _Flowers of Evil_, 43: Evening Harmony. 1857.\n     trans. Ruth White, 1969.", "“看他在西方天际沉落，\n 雾气升起时阴影渐长。”\n    -约翰·德莱顿，《押沙龙与阿奇托菲尔》，I，268。1681年。\n\n“天空忧伤而美丽，如同一座巨大的祭坛。\n 太阳已溺亡于它凝结的血中。”\n    -夏尔·波德莱尔，《恶之花》，43：黄昏的和谐。1857年。\n     译：Ruth White，1969年。"},
+    {"“Sweet is the swamp with its secrets,\n Until we meet a snake;\n 'T is then we sigh for houses,\n And our departure take\n\n At that enthralling gallop\n That only childhood knows.\n A snake is summer's treason,\n And guile is where it goes.”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “A Snake”. ca. 1865.", "“沼泽及其秘密何其甜美，\n 直到我们遇见一条蛇；\n 那时我们便叹息着想家，\n 迈开离去的脚步\n\n 以那种令人着迷的疾驰\n 唯有童年才知晓。\n 蛇是夏天的背叛，\n 狡诈便是它的去向。”\n    -艾米莉·狄金森，《蛇》。约1865年。"},
+    {"“The fool, as I think, at the chasm's brink,\n Prone by the swamp or the marsh's side,\n Did, even as I, in the end rejoice,\n Since the voice of death must be His true voice.”\n    -Arthur Edward Waite, “At The End of Things”. 1906.", "“那愚者，我以为，在深渊的边缘，\n 俯卧在沼泽或泥塘旁，\n 最终也如我一般欢欣，\n 因为死亡之声必是祂真正的声音。”\n    -Arthur Edward Waite，《At The End of Things》。1906年。"},
+    {"“A Bird came down the Walk —\n He did not know I saw —\n He bit an Angleworm in halves\n And ate the fellow, raw”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “A Bird came down the Walk”. ca. 1865.", "“一只鸟沿小径落下——\n 它不知我看见——\n 它把一条蚯蚓咬成两半\n 将那家伙生吞”\n    -艾米莉·狄金森，《一只鸟沿小径落下》。约1865年。"},
+    {"“I can puff up my feathers, look real mean\n Be the old man that this here's scene\n I'm a struttin' preenin' bantam rooster looking for a fight.”\n    -Andrew Bird's Bowl of Fire, “Cock o' the Walk”. 1998.", "“我能鼓起羽毛，显得凶神恶煞\n 当这场景里的老大\n 我是一只昂首阔步、搔首弄姿、寻衅斗殴的矮脚公鸡。”\n    -Andrew Bird's Bowl of Fire，《Cock o' the Walk》。1998年。"},
+    {"“Oozing and surging up out of that yawning trap-door in the Cyclopean crypt I\n had glimpsed such an unbelievable behemothic monstrosity that I could not\n doubt the power of its original to kill with its mere sight. Even now I cannot\n begin to suggest it with any words at my command. I might call it gigantic —\n tentacled — proboscidian — octopus-eyed — semi-amorphous — plastic — partly\n squamous and partly rugose — ugh!”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft and Hazel Heald, “Out of the Aeons”, _Weird Tales_, 25, No.\n     4, pp. 478-96. April 1935.", "“从那巨岩墓穴中敞开的活板门里渗出、涌起，我\n 瞥见如此难以置信的庞然巨物，以至于我无法\n 怀疑其原型的威力仅凭一瞥便能致人死命。即便此刻我也无法\n 用我掌握的任何言辞描述它。我或许会称它巨大——\n 有触手——有长鼻——有章鱼般的眼——半无定形——可塑——部分\n 有鳞而部分有皱——呃！”\n    -H.P. 洛夫克拉夫特与 Hazel Heald，《超越万古》，《Weird Tales》，25，第\n     4期，第478-96页。1935年4月。"},
+    {"“And on the other part the Titans eagerly strengthened their ranks, and both\n sides at one time showed the work of their hands and their might. The\n boundless sea rang terribly around, and the earth crashed loudly: wide Heaven\n was shaken and groaned, and high Olympus reeled from its foundation under the\n charge of the undying gods, and a heavy quaking reached dim Tartarus and the\n deep sound of their feet in the fearful onset and of their hard missiles. So,\n then, they launched their grievous shafts upon one another, and the cry of\n both armies as they shouted reached to starry heaven; and they met together\n with a great battle-cry.”\n    -Hesiod, _Theogony_, 8th cent. B.C.\n     trans. H.G. Evelyn-White, 1914.", "“而在另一方，提坦们急切地重整阵型，双方\n 同时展现出手与力的杰作。\n 无垠的大海在四周可怕地轰鸣，大地轰然巨响：广阔的天空\n 震颤呻吟，高耸的奥林波斯在不朽众神的冲击下\n 根基动摇，沉重的震动传到幽暗的塔尔塔罗斯，\n 他们双脚在可怕冲锋中的深沉声响，\n 以及他们坚硬投石的轰鸣。于是，他们彼此投掷着致命的箭矢，\n 两军的呼喊直达繁星点点的天空；\n 他们带着巨大的战吼撞在一起。”\n    -赫西俄德，《神谱》，公元前8世纪。\n     译：H.G. Evelyn-White，1914年。"},
+    {"“But the Seneschal gathered the toadstool fly-bane.”\n    -Adam Mickiewicz, _Pan Tadeusz_, III. 1834.\n     trans. G.R. Noyes, 1917.", "“但总管采走了毒蝇伞。”\n    -亚当·密茨凯维奇，《塔杜施先生》，III。1834年。\n     译：G.R. Noyes，1917年。"},
+    {"“Gentle socks pamper them by day, and shoes cobbled of leather fortify them,\n but my toes hardly notice. All they're interested in is turning out\n toenails—semitransparent, flexible sheets of a hornlike material, as defence\n against—whom?”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, “Toenails”. 1960.\n    trans. Andrew Hurley, 1998.", "“柔软的袜子白天娇宠它们，皮革缝制的鞋子护卫它们，\n 但我的脚趾几乎浑然不觉。它们唯一感兴趣的只是不断长出\n 趾甲——半透明、柔韧、角质般的片状物，用来防御\n ——防御谁呢？”\n    -豪尔赫·路易斯·博尔赫斯，《趾甲》。1960年。\n    译：Andrew Hurley，1998年。"},
+    {"“Thou art to me a delicious torment.”\n    -Ralph Waldo Emerson, _Essays: First Series_, Essay VI: Friendship. 1841.", "“于我而言，你是一种甜美的折磨。”\n    -拉尔夫·沃尔多·爱默生，《随笔集：第一辑》，第六篇：论友谊。1841年。"},
+    {"“Snail, snail, slug-slow,\n To me thy four horns show;\n If thou dost not show me thy four,\n I will throw thee out of the door,\n For the crow in the gutter,\n To eat for bread and butter.”\n    -Silesian rhyme. Quoted by S.W. Singer in _Notes and Queries_, no. 69.\n     1851.", "“蜗牛，蜗牛，慢如鼻涕虫，\n 向我露出你的四只角；\n 你若不向我露出那四只，\n 我就把你扔出门外，\n 让阴沟里的乌鸦，\n 当作黄油面包吃掉。”\n    -西里西亚童谣。S.W. Singer 引于《Notes and Queries》，第69期。\n     1851年。"},
+    {"“Things are only mannequins and even the great world-historical events are only\n costumes beneath which they exchange glances with nothingness, with the base\n and the banal.”\n    -Walter Benjamin, _Protocols to the Experiments on Hashish, Opium and\n     Mescaline 1927-1934_, “Protocol II: Highlights of the Second Hashish\n     Impression”. 15 January 1928.\n     trans. Scott J. Thompson, 1997.", "“事物不过是人体模型，甚至那些伟大的世界历史事件也不过是\n 戏服，其下它们与虚无、与卑下\n 和平庸交换着眼色。”\n    -瓦尔特·本雅明，《1927-1934年哈希什、鸦片与\n     麦司卡林实验记录》，“第二号记录：第二次哈希什\n     印象要点”。1928年1月15日。\n     译：Scott J. Thompson，1997年。"},
+    {" Scully: “Where the hell did it come from?”\n Mulder: “I don’t know, but it looks like I’m going to have to tell Skinner\n          that his suspect is a giant bloodsucking worm after all.”\n    -_The X-Files_, “The Host”. 1994", " Scully：“它到底是从哪儿来的？”\n Mulder：“我不知道，但看来我终究不得不告诉 Skinner，\n          他的嫌疑犯是一条巨大的吸血蠕虫。”\n    -《X档案》，《The Host》。1994"},
+    {"“We were permitted to shriek in the tongue of dwarfs and demons”\n    -Czesław Miłosz, “A Task”.", "“我们被允许用矮人与恶魔的舌头尖声嘶叫”\n    -切斯瓦夫·米沃什，《一项任务》。"},
+    {"“Beauty is no quality in things themselves: It exists merely in the mind which\n contemplates them; and each mind perceives a different beauty. One person may\n even perceive deformity, where another is sensible of beauty; and every\n individual ought to acquiesce in his own sentiment, without pretending to\n regulate those of others.”\n    -David Hume", "“美并非事物本身的性质：它只存在于观照事物的\n 心灵之中；而每颗心灵感知到的美各不相同。一个人或许\n 甚至感知到畸形，而另一个人却感受到美；每个\n 个体都应默许自己的情感，而不应妄图\n 规训他人的情感。”\n    -大卫·休谟"},
+    {"“The awful shadow of some unseen Power\n Floats, tho’ unseen, amongst us.”\n    -Percy Bysshe Shelley, “Hymn to Intellectual Beauty”, I, 1-2. 1816.", "“某种无形力量的可怕阴影\n 虽不可见，却飘浮在我们中间。”\n    -珀西·比希·雪莱，《智力美颂》，I，1-2。1816年。"},
+    {"“Spell for causing a shabti to do work for a man in the realm of the dead:\n O shabti, allotted to me, if I be summoned or if I be detailed to do any work\n which has to be done in the realm of the dead, if indeed any obstacles are\n implanted for you therewith as a man at his duties, you shall detail yourself\n for me on every occasion of making arable the fields, of flooding the banks or\n of conveying sand from east to west; ‘Here I am’, you shall say.\n\n    -Spell 6, _Book of the Dead_, from _Ancient Egyptian Book of the Dead:\n     Journey through the afterlife_, edited by John H. Taylor. 2010", "“使乌沙布提在冥界替人做工的咒语：\n 哦，分配给我的乌沙布提，若我被召唤，或我被指派去做\n 冥界中必须完成的任何工作，若确实有任何障碍\n 如人履职时那般被设置于你面前，你便须\n 在每一次开垦田地、灌溉堤岸或\n 自东向西运送沙子时，替我代劳；你要说：‘我在这里’。\n\n    -咒语6，《亡灵书》，出自《古埃及亡灵书：\n     穿越来世的旅程》，John H. Taylor 编。2010"},
+    {" • Scuze me. My friend Burt says you're a vampire hunter. 'zat so?\n — Uh yes. Yes it is.\n • Well you n'ain't gonna find one round here, mate. They ain't no vampires.\n — You don't believe in vampires?\n • 'course not!\n • But I do believe in con artists. And charletans who like to stir up trouble!\n • Dead people who get up at night and suck blood. How stupid do you think we\n   are?\n • My place is haunted with 16 ghosts and they all say there n'ain't no\n   vampires!\n    -Rich Morris, Yet Another Fantasy Gamer Comic", " • 打扰一下。我朋友 Burt 说你是个吸血鬼猎人。当真？\n — 呃，是的。没错。\n • 那你在这一带可找不着，伙计。这儿没有吸血鬼。\n — 你不相信有吸血鬼？\n • 当然不信！\n • 可我相信有骗子。还有喜欢惹是生非的江湖术士！\n • 死人夜里爬起来吸血。你把我们当\n   多傻？\n • 我那儿闹鬼，有16个鬼，他们都说这儿没有\n   吸血鬼！\n    -Rich Morris，Yet Another Fantasy Gamer Comic"},
+    {"“For something is amiss and out of place\n When mice with wings can wear a human face.”\n    -Theodore Roethke, “The Bat”. 1938.", "“因为某事不对劲，出了岔子\n 当长着翅膀的老鼠能戴上人的面孔。”\n    -西奥多·罗特克，《蝙蝠》。1938年。"},
+    {"“I'm ugly, I'm ugly as sin,\n But beautiful's out, ugly's in.\n If you're ugly like me, you're in good company.\n There are millions of us who are ugly.”\n    -_The Muppet Show_, “Ugly Song”. 1977", "“我很丑，我丑得像罪孽，\n 但美丽过时了，丑陋正流行。\n 如果你像我一样丑，你就有的是同伴。\n 我们这些丑人有千千万万。”\n    -《木偶秀》，《丑陋之歌》。1977"},
+    {"“The monstrous plant bud . . . had grown again with preternatural rapidity,\n from Falmer's head. A loathsome pale-green stem was mounting thickly, and had\n started to branch like antlers after attaining a height of six or seven\n inches.\n\n More dreadful than this, if possible, similar growths had issued from the\n eyes; and their stems, climbing vertically across the forehead, had entirely\n displaced the eyeballs.”\n    -Clark Ashton Smith, “The Seed from the Sepulcher”. 1933.", "“那畸形的植物幼芽……竟以超自然的迅疾再度生长，\n 从法尔默的头上冒出。一根令人作呕的淡绿色茎干粗壮地攀升，而且\n 长到六七\n 英寸高后，开始如鹿角般分叉。\n\n 若说还有什么更可怖的，那便是同样的增生物也从\n 他的双眼钻出；它们的茎垂直爬过额头，\n 已彻底将眼球挤开。”\n    -克拉克·阿什顿·史密斯，《墓穴中的种子》。1933。"},
+    {"“Telimena, wearied with the prolonged wrangling, wanted to go out into the\n fresh air, but sought a partner. She took a little basket from the peg.\n “Gentlemen, I see that you wish to remain within doors,” she said, wrapping\n around her head a red cashmere shawl, “but I am going for mushrooms: follow me\n who will!” Under one arm she took the little daughter of the Chamberlain, with\n the other she raised her skirt up to her ankles. Thaddeus silently hastened\n after her—to seek mushrooms!”\n    -Adam Mickiewicz, _Pan Tadeusz_, II. 1834.\n     trans. G.R. Noyes, 1917.", "“泰利梅娜被漫长的争执弄得疲惫不堪，想出去\n 呼吸新鲜空气，却要找个伴儿。她从挂钩上取下一个小篮子。\n “先生们，我看你们是想待在屋里了，”她一边说，一边\n 用一条红色开司米披肩裹住头，“可我要去采蘑菇：谁愿意\n 就跟我来！”她一只胳膊抱起侍从官的小女儿，\n 另一只手把裙子提到脚踝处。塔德乌什默默地\n 快步跟在她身后——去采蘑菇！”\n    -亚当·密茨凯维奇，《塔杜施先生》，第二卷。1834年。\n     译：G.R. 诺伊斯，1917年。"},
+    {"“Their innumerable sculptures of demons and dragons assumed a lugubrious\n aspect. The restless light of the flame made them move to the eye. There were\n griffins which had the air of laughing, gargoyles which one fancied one heard\n yelping, salamanders which puffed at the fire, tarasques which sneezed in the\n smoke. And among the monsters thus roused from their sleep of stone by this\n flame, by this noise, there was one who walked about, and who was seen, from\n time to time, to pass across the glowing face of the pile, like a bat in front\n of a candle.”\n    -Victor Hugo, _The Hunchback of Notre-Dame_, 10, ch. IV. 1831.\n     trans. Isabel F. Hapgood", "“那数不尽的恶魔与龙形雕刻染上了阴郁的\n 神态。火焰摇曳不定的光使它们在眼前仿佛活了过来。有的\n 狮鹫面带笑意，有的滴水兽让人以为听到了\n 吠叫，有的火蜥蜴朝火堆喷气，有的塔拉斯各兽在\n 烟雾中打喷嚏。在这些因这火焰、这喧嚣而从石之沉睡中\n 醒来的怪物当中，有一个来回走动，人们不时\n 看见它掠过那堆燃烧的正面，像一支蜡烛前\n 的蝙蝠。”\n    -维克多·雨果，《巴黎圣母院》，第十卷，第四章。1831年。\n     译：伊莎贝尔·F. 哈普古德"},
+    {"“Alas, poor Yorick! I knew him, Horatio, a fellow of infinite jest, of most\n excellent fancy. He hath bore me on his back a thousand times, and now how\n abhorr'd in my imagination it is! My gorge rises at it.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark_, V, 1. 1603", "“唉，可怜的约里克！我认识他，霍拉旭，一个爱开玩笑\n 到无穷、想象极为出众的家伙。他背过我上千次，如今\n 在我的想象中，这多么令人厌恶！我一想到就反胃。”\n    -威廉·莎士比亚，《丹麦王子哈姆雷特的悲剧》，第五幕，第一场。1603年"},
+    {"“Teacher: How does a dog smell?\n Student: I don't know.\n Teacher: Correct!”\n    -Traditional Spanish joke.", "“老师：狗是怎么闻味的？\n 学生：我不知道。\n 老师：答对了！”\n    -传统西班牙笑话。"},
+    {"“Oh, but you can't expect to wield supreme executive power just because some\n watery tart threw a sword at you.”\n    -Monty Python and the Holy Grail, 1975.", "“哦，可你不能仅仅因为某个\n 泡在水里的轻浮女子朝你扔了把剑，就指望掌握至高无上的行政权力。”\n    -《蒙提·派森与圣杯》，1975年。"},
+    {"“Unhappie wight, borne to desastrous end,\n That doth his life in so long tendance spend!”\n    -Edmund Spenser, “Mother Hubberds Tale”, _Complaints_. 1591.", "“不幸的生灵，生来注定悲惨的结局，\n 竟将一生耗费在如此漫长的侍奉中！”\n    -埃德蒙·斯宾塞，《妈妈胡伯德的故事》，《怨诗集》。1591年。"},
+    {"“God, though this life is but a wraith,\n Although we know not what we use,\n Although we grope with little faith,\n Give me the heart to fight—and lose.”\n    -Louis Untermeyer, “Prayer”. 1919.", "“上帝啊，纵使此生不过一缕幽魂，\n 纵使我们不知自己所用何物，\n 纵使我们怀着微薄的信仰摸索，\n 也请赐我战斗——并失败的心。”\n    -路易斯·昂特迈耶，《祈祷》。1919年。"},
+    {"“Stare into the sun\n Wherever you may roam\n Feel it calling you home\n Despite the mounting danger\n Stare into the sun\n Let its fire fill you\n Whatever doesn’t kill you\n Only makes you stranger”\n    -The Sunbeams, “The Tug”. 2020", "“凝视太阳\n 无论你漂泊何方\n 感受它唤你归家\n 纵使危机四伏\n 凝视太阳\n 让它的火焰填满你\n 凡杀不死你的\n 只会让你更怪异”\n    -日光乐队，《牵引》。2020年"},
+    {"“My complete answer to the late 19th century question 'what is electrodynamics\n trying to tell us' would simply be this:\n\n Fields in empty space have physical reality; the medium that supports them does\n not.\n\n Having thus removed the mystery from electrodynamics, let me immediately do the\n same for quantum mechanics:\n\n Correlations have physical reality; that which they correlate does not.”\n    -N. David Mermin, _What is quantum mechanics trying to tell us?_, 1998.", "“对于19世纪晚期那个问题‘电动力学\n 想告诉我们什么’，我的完整回答很简单：\n\n 真空中的场具有物理实在；而支撑它们的介质则\n 没有。\n\n 既然借此从电动力学中移除了神秘，那就让我立刻对\n 量子力学如法炮制：\n\n 关联具有物理实在；而它们所关联之物则没有。”\n    -N. 大卫·默明，《量子力学想告诉我们什么？》，1998年。"},
+    {"“He fixed his eyes upon the door, which, slowly opening, disclosed a stranger\n of majestic form, but scowling features, who demanded sternly, why he was\n summoned? ‘I did not summon you,’ said the trembling student. ‘You did!’ said\n the stranger, advancing, angrily; ‘and the demons are not to be invoked in\n vain.’ The student could make no reply; and the demon, enraged that one of the\n uninitiated should have summoned him out of mere presumption, seized him by\n the throat and strangled him.\n\n When Agrippa returned, a few days afterwards, he found his house beset with\n devils. Some of them were sitting on the chimneypots, kicking up their legs in\n the air; while others were playing at leapfrog, on the very edge of the\n parapet. His study was so filled with them that he found it difficult to make\n his way to his desk.”\n    -Charles Mackay, _Memoirs of Extraordinary Popular Delusions_, Vol. III,\n     Part I. 1841.", "“他目不转睛地盯着那扇门，门缓缓打开，现出一个身形\n 威严、却面露凶相的生人，他严厉地质问，为何\n 召唤他？‘我并未召唤你，’那颤抖的学生说。‘你召唤了！’那\n 生人怒冲冲地逼近；‘恶魔可不是白白\n 受召的。’学生无言以对；那恶魔因一个\n 未入门的家伙仅凭妄念就召来了他而暴怒，一把掐住\n 他的喉咙将他扼死。\n\n 几天后阿格里帕回来，发现自宅被\n 群魔围困。有些坐在烟囱顶上，朝\n 空中踢着腿；另一些则在\n 护墙的边缘玩跳背游戏。他的书房里塞满了它们，他发现自己\n 很难挤到书桌前。”\n    -查尔斯·麦凯，《非凡流行谬误回忆录》，第三卷，\n     第一部分。1841年。"},
+    {"“It seemed that while the zombie came from the grave, it was neither a ghost,\n nor yet a person who had been raised like Lazarus from the dead. The zombie,\n they say, is a soulless human corpse, still dead, but taken from the grave and\n endowed by sorcery with a mechanical semblance of life—it is a dead body which\n is made to walk and act and move as if it were alive. People who have the\n power to do this go to a fresh grave, dig up the body before it has had time\n to rot, galvanize it into movement, and then make of it a servant or slave,\n occasionally for the commission of some crime, more often simply as a drudge\n around the habitation or the farm, setting it dull heavy tasks, and beating it\n like a dumb beast if it slackens.”\n    -William Seabrook, _The Magic Island_. 1929.\n\n“They’re not afraid! No matter what we do, no matter how many we kill, they will\n never, ever be afraid!”\n    -Max Brooks, _World War Z: An Oral History of the Zombie War_. 2006.", "“看来，虽然这僵尸来自坟墓，它却既非幽灵，\n 也不是像拉撒路那样从死里复生的人。据说，僵尸，\n 是一具没有灵魂的人类尸体，依然是死的，却被从坟墓中取出，\n 由巫术赋予一种机械般的生命假象——这是一具被\n 驱使着像活人一样行走、行动、移动的死尸。拥有这种\n 力量的人会去一座新坟，趁尸体尚未\n 腐烂将其挖出，用电击令其活动，然后把它变成\n 仆人或奴隶，偶尔用来犯下罪行，更多时候只是当作\n 住所或农场周围干粗活的苦力，让它做沉闷沉重的活计，\n 若是偷懒就像对待哑巴牲畜一样鞭打它。”\n    -威廉·西布鲁克，《魔法岛》。1929年。\n\n“它们不怕！无论我们做什么，无论我们杀多少，它们都\n 永远、永远不会害怕！”\n    -马克斯·布鲁克斯，《僵尸世界大战：僵尸战争口述史》。2006年。"},
